  Example: N=4, a=2, b=4\n        clockwise: 2→3 (bridge2), 3→4 (bridge3) → uses 2 bridges\n        counterclockwise: 2→1 (bridge1), 1→4 (bridge4) → uses 2 bridges\n        total=4, yes.\n\n        So the two simple paths use disjoint sets of bridges, and their union is all bridges.\n\n        So if the short path is clockwise, then it uses a set of `forward` bridges, and the long path uses the other `back` bridges.\n\n        Similarly if short is counterclockwise, it uses `back` bridges.\n\n        And the **other arc** is the long path.\n\n        Therefore, the bridges used in the short path are:\n            if forward < back: the `forward` bridges in the clockwise arc from `a` to `b`\n            elif forward > back: the `back` bridges in the counterclockwise arc from `a` to `b` → which = total bridges - the clockwise arc from `b` to `a`? But easier: it’s the complement of the clockwise arc between `a` and `b`.\n\n        Actually, the counterclockwise arc from `a` to `b` is the same as the clockwise arc from `b` to `a`.\n\n        And the clockwise arc from `b` to `a` has length = (a - b) % N = N - forward.\n\n        So its bridge set is different.\n\n        But we need the bridge set for the short path.\n\n        So for a segment `a->b`:\n\n        if forward < back: short path is clockwise from `a` to `b` → uses bridge set: `clockwise_arc(a, b)` of size `forward`\n\n        if forward > back: short path is counterclockwise from `a` to `b` → which is the same as the clockwise arc from `b` to `a`? \n            counterclockwise from a to b: a -> a-1 -> ... -> b\n            clockwise from b to a: b -> b+1 -> ... -> a\n            -> not the same.\n\n        Let’s take a=2, b=4, N=5:\n        clockwise a to b: 2->3 (bridge2), 3->4 (bridge3) → length=2\n        counterclockwise: 2->1 (bridge1), 1->5 (bridge5), 5->4 (bridge4) → length=3\n        clockwise from b to a: 4->5 (bridge4), 5->1 (bridge5), 1->2 (bridge1) → length=3, same as above.\n\n        Yes! So counterclockwise arc from a to b has the same bridge set as the clockwise arc from b to a.\n\n        And length = (a - b) % N = (2-4)%5= -2%5=3.\n\n        So we can unify:\n\n        - If forward < back:\n            short arc = clockwise from a to b\n            length = forward\n        - If forward > back:\n            short arc = clockwise from b to a\n            length = back\n        - If equal: ignore\n\n        But wait, in the second case, is it the same as the counterclockwise a to b? Yes, both have length = back and use the same bridges.\n\n        Therefore, we can do:\n\n        if forward < back:\n            start = a\n            length = forward\n        else if forward > back:\n            start = b\n            length = back\n        else:\n            continue\n\n        Then the short path uses the ``length` bridges in the clockwise arc starting at `start` for `length` steps.\n\n        And each of these bridges will incur an extra cost of `|back - forward|` if removed.\n\n        Specifically, the penalty for removing any bridge in this arc is not incurred only if the short path is unaffected? No: if we remove a bridge in the short path, we cannot use it, so we must take the long path, which costs `back = N - forward` (in the first case), so extra cost = `back - forward`.\n\n        So penalty = `abs(back - forward) = |N - 2 * forward|` or `|N - 2 * back|` but since forward+back=N, it's `|N - 2 * min(forward, back)|` = `|back - forward|`.\n\n        So for each bridge in the short arc, if removed, this segment's cost increases by `gap = |forward - back| = abs(N - 2 * d_short)`.\n\n        Therefore, we want to add `gap` to each bridge in the short arc.\n\n        So algorithm:\n\n        base = 0\n        penalty = [0]*(N+1)  # penalty[i] = extra cost added to total if bridge i is removed, due to this segment\n\n        But we can use a difference array for circular array.\n\n        However, the short arc might wrap around.\n\n        So for a segment:\n            if forward == back: skip\n            else:\n                d_short = min(forward, back)\n                gap = abs(forward - back)  # = N - 2*d_short\n                if forward < back:\n                    start = a\n                    length = forward\n                else:\n                    start = b\n                    length = back\n\n                The bridges used are:\n                    start, start+1, ..., start+length-1  (mod N, but bridge indices are 1..N)\n\n                But if start+length-1 <= N:\n                    then bridges: [start, start+length-1]\n                else:\n                    part1: [start, N]\n                    part2: [1, start+length-1 - N]\n\n                So we can use a difference array of size N+2.\n\n        Let’s do:\n\n        diff = [0]*(N+2)  # 1-indexed, diff[1..N]\n\n        For each such segment:\n            if we have a contiguous range [L, R] of bridges (inclusive), then:\n                diff[L] += gap\n                diff[R+1] -= gap\n            if it wraps:\n                diff[1] += gap\n                diff[R+1] -= gap\n                diff[L] += gap\n                diff[N+1] -= gap  # but we only care about 1..N\n\n        More carefully:\n\n        Let L = start\n        R = start + length - 1\n\n        if R <= N:\n            diff[L] += gap\n            diff[R+1] -= gap\n        else:\n            # R > N\n            Rmod = R % N\n            if Rmod == 0: Rmod = N\n            # But R = start+length-1\n            # The range is [L, N] and [1, Rmod]\n            diff[L] += gap\n            diff[N+1] -= gap\n            diff[1] += gap\n            diff[Rmod+1] -= gap\n\n        After processing all segments, we do:\n\n        total_penalty[i] = diff[1] + ... + diff[i]  # prefix sum\n\n        Then final cost for removing bridge i: base + total_penalty[i]\n\n        Then answer = min_{i=1}^{N} (base + total_penalty[i])\n\n        But note: what if `start` is 3, `length=5`, N=4: \n            R = 3+5-1=7\n            Rmod = 7 % 4 = 3, but if it was 8, 8%4=0 → then we want Rmod = 4? \n            Actually: bridges: 3,4,1,2,3? Wait, length=5, but only 4 bridges.\n\n        But length should be at most N-1, because d_short < N/2 for non-tie cases? \n\n        Actually, d_short = min(forward, N-forw) < N/2 if there's an inequality.\n\n        So length = d_short < N/2 ≤ N-1 for N>=3.\n\n        So R = start + length - 1 <= start + (N//2) - 1\n\n        For start ≤ N, length < N/2, so R < N + N/2 = 1.5N, but since length < N/2, and start <= N, R < N + N/2 = 1.5N, but we care if R > N.\n\n        And R - N = start + length - 1 - N < N + N/2 - 1 - N = N/2 - 1 < N/2\n\n        So Rmod = start + length - 1 - N\n\n        But we can do:\n\n        R = start + length - 1\n        if R <= N:\n            # normal segment\n            diff[start] += gap\n            diff[R+1] -= gap\n        else:\n            # wraps around\n            # first part: [start, N]\n            diff[start] += gap\n            diff[N+1] -= gap\n            # second part: [1, R - N]\n            diff[1] += gap\n            diff[R - N + 1] -= gap\n\n        But R - N could be >=1 and <= N\n\n        Example: start=3, length=3, N=4 → R=5, R-N=1\n            bridges: 3,4, then 1? Wait: length=3: 3,4,1 → yes, so bridges 3,4,1\n            so [3,4] and [1,1]\n\n        R - N = 5 - 4 = 1, so second part [1,1]\n\n        diff[3] += gap, diff[5] (i.e., diff[N+1]) -= gap\n        diff[1] += gap, diff[2] -= gap\n\n        Then prefix sum:\n            i=1: diff[1] = gap\n            i=2: = gap - gap = 0\n            i=3: = gap (from diff[3]) and from previous: 0? \n\n        But we need: penalty[1]=gap, penalty[3]=gap, penalty[4]=gap\n\n        prefix sum:\n            p[1] = diff[1] = gap\n            p[2] = p[1] + diff[2] = gap + 0 = gap\n            p[3] = p[2] + diff[3] = gap + gap = 2*gap\n            p[4] = p[3] + diff[4] = 2*gap + 0 = 2*gap\n            then p[5] is for diff[5] which is -gap, but we stop at N.\n\n        We see that p[3] and p[4] are 2*gap, not gap.\n\n        Why? We added `gap` at `diff[3]` and `diff[1]`, and subtracted at `diff[5]` and `diff[2]`.\n\n        But `diff` array is for difference, so we need to do:\n\n        diff[3] += gap\n        diff[5] -= gap   # but 5 > N+1? we have N+2, so ok\n        diff[1] += gap\n        diff[2] -= gap\n\n        prefix:\n            p[1] = diff[1] = gap\n            p[2] = p[1] + diff[2] = gap - gap = 0\n            p[3] = p[2] + diff[3] = 0 + gap = gap\n            p[4] = p[3] + diff[4] = gap + 0 = gap\n\n        But we want p[1]=gap, p[3]=gap, p[4]=gap\n\n        p[1]=gap ✓\n        p[2]=0\n        p[3]=gap ✓\n        p[4]=gap ✓\n\n        But p[2]=0, p[1]=gap — that’s correct because bridge 1 is included, bridge 2 is not.\n\n        Yes, we have bridges 1,3,4.\n\n        So bridge 1: penalty=gap, bridge 2:0, bridge3: gap, bridge4: gap.\n\n        But bridge 2 is not used, so should be 0.\n\n        But in the path: after bridge4 (connects 4 and 1), we go to 1, then bridge1 to go to 2? No, we only go to bridge1.\n\n        The bridges used are 3,4,1 — so yes, bridge 1 is used.\n\n        But bridge 1 is the one connecting 1 and 2.\n\n        Does this mean the edge is used? Yes, if we go from 1 to 2 or 2 to 1.\n\n        But in our case, we went from 4 to 1 (bridge4), then do we go from 1 to somewhere? No, the moves are:\n\n        We have a path of length=3: 3->4, 4->1, 1-> ? \n\n        We are on a counterclockwise segment, but in this example, we are using the unified \"clockwise from start\" for the short arc.\n\n        In this example, we had a segment where the short arc is defined as a clockwise arc of length=3 from start=3:\n\n        3->4 (bridge3), 4->1 (bridge4), 1->2 (bridge1) — so yes, if length=3, then three moves: 3,4,1\n\n        But in our earlier example, we wanted to go from a to b in 3 steps counterclockwise, but the short arc length should be `d_short < N/2`, and if N=4, d_short<2, so length cannot be 3.\n\n        Ah! Important: if `forward==2`, then `back=2`, so we skip because it's a tie.\n\n        So `length = d_short < N/2`, so for N>=3, `d_short <= (N-1)//2`\n\n        For example, N=5, d_short<=2\n\n        So R = start + length -1 <= start + floor((N-1)/2) - 1\n\n        This could be larger than N.\n\n        Example: N=5, start=4, length=3: but d_short=3, N=5, then back=2, so we would have used the counterclockwise branch and start=b, and length=2.\n\n        So in the unified approach, we only use `length = d_short = min(forward, back) <= floor((N-1)/2)`\n\n        For N=5, floor((N-1)/2)=2, so length<=2.\n\n        For N=6, d_short <=2 (since if forward=3, back=3, tie → skip)\n\n        So in fact, `length` is at most `(N-1)//2`\n\n        And `start` is at least 1.\n\n        So R = start + length - 1 <= 1 + (N-1)//2 - 1 + N?  start can be up to N.\n\n        start = N, length = L (<= (N-1)//2)\n        R = N + L - 1\n\n        For L=2, N=5: R=5+2-1=6>5 → so wrap.\n\n        So we do need wrap.\n\n        But with L <= (N-1)//2, then R - N = L - 1 + start - N <= (N-1)//2 - 1 + N - N = (N-1)//2 - 1\n\n        which is at least 0? For N=3: (3-1)//2-1 = 1-1=0 → R-N =0? \n\n        But R = start+length-1, start=N, length=min(forward,back), if start=N, and length=1, then R=N+1-1=N, so R<=N.\n\n        Only when start is large and length>=2.\n\n        For N=6, start=5, length=2: R=6 → no wrap.\n        start=5, length=3: not possible, since d_short>=3, but N=6, back=3 → tie → skip.\n\n        So maximum length = floor((N-1)/2)\n\n        For even N: (N-1)/2 = (2k-1)/2 = k-0.5 → floor = k-1, and N=2k, so maximum length = k-1 = N/2 - 1\n        So R = start + (N/2 - 1) - 1 = start + N/2 - 2\n        start<=N, so R<= N + N/2 - 2 = 1.5N - 2\n\n        But we care if R>N: when start > N/2 + 2? \n\n        But in summary, we do need to handle wrap.\n\n        But our difference array method for the wrap case is correct as above.\n\n        However, in the example above with the double counting, we saw that the prefix sum gave the correct penalty only if we do not have overlap.\n\n        Let’s reexamine the difference array method for circular range:\n\n        We want to add `gap` to bridges in the range `[start, R]` if no wrap, or `[start, N] ∪ [1, R-N]` if wrap.\n\n        We can do:\n\n        if R <= N:\n            diff[start] += gap\n            diff[R+1] -= gap\n        else:\n            # R > N\n            diff[start] += gap\n            diff[N+1] -= gap\n            diff[1] += gap\n            if R - N + 1 <= N+1:\n                diff[R - N + 1] -= gap\n            # else, no need\n\n        But note: `R = start + length - 1 > N`, so `R - N = start + length - 1 - N`, and we add 1 -> `R-N+1 = start + length - N`\n\n        Since length <= (N-1)//2, and start <= N, then `start + length - N <= N + (N-1)//2 - N = (N-1)//2` < N\n\n        So `R-N+1` is at most (N-1)//2 + 1 <= N (for N>=2)\n\n        So we can safely do:\n\n        if R > N:\n            diff[1] += gap\n            diff[R - N + 1] -= gap\n\n        But what if `R - N + 1 > N`? We just argued it's at most (N-1)//2 + 1, which is <= N for N>=1.\n\n        So it's safe.\n\n        However, note: we use `diff` of size `N+2`, so index up to `N+1` is safe.\n\n        Example: N=4, start=3, length=2: \n            forward = (b-a)%N = ? not used, but length=2\n            R = 3+2-1=4 <=4 → no wrap\n            diff[3] += gap\n            diff[5] -= gap  → which is ok\n\n        start=4, length=2, N=4:\n            R=4+2-1=5>4 → wrap\n            diff[4] += gap\n            diff[5] -= gap  (this handles [4,4])\n            then add:\n                diff[1] += gap\n                diff[5-4+1] = diff[2] -= gap\n\n            So range: [4,4] and [1,1]\n\n            prefix:\n                p[1] = diff[1] = gap\n                p[2] = p[1] + diff[2] = gap - gap = 0\n                p[3] = p[2] + diff[3] = 0\n                p[4] = p[3] + diff[4] = gap\n\n            So p[1]=gap, p[4]=gap → correct.\n\n        But we want bridge 1 and bridge 4.\n\n        Yes.\n\n        One more: start=5, length=2, N=6: \n            R=6, so no wrap? R=6<=6 → no wrap\n            diff[5] += gap\n            diff[7] -= gap  → diff[7] is out of our loop, but we only do p[1..6]\n\n        If start=6, length=2, N=6:\n            R=6+2-1=7>6 → wrap\n            diff[6] += gap\n            diff[7] -= gap  (handles [6,6])\n            diff[1] += gap\n            diff[7-6+1] = diff[2] -= gap\n\n            So add gap to bridge 6,1\n\n        p[1] = gap\n        p[2] = gap - gap =0\n        p[6] = (p[5]+=0) then p[6] += gap (from diff[6]) → so p[6]= gap\n\n        So bridge 1 and 6 have gap, others not.\n\n        And the arc: 6->1 (bridge6), 1->2 (bridge1) — but we only want two bridges: 6 and 1? \n        But length=2: \n            step1: 6->1: bridge6\n            step2: 1->2: bridge1\n        so yes, bridges 6 and 1.\n\n        But in our representation, we got penalty on bridge 6 and bridge 1.\n\n        But the tour segment is only using bridge6 and bridge1, so if either is removed, the segment must take the long path.\n\n        Correct.\n\n        But note: the penalty array we are building is: for each bridge `i`, the total extra cost from all segments whose short path uses bridge `i`.\n\n        Then total cost if remove bridge `i` is `base + penalty[i]`\n\n        Then answer is min over i.\n\n        However, what if a segment's short path uses bridge `i`, and we remove `i`, then that segment's cost increases by `gap_k` for that segment.\n\n        And we are adding `gap_k` to `penalty[i]` for every such segment.\n\n        So total cost = base + penalty[i]\n\n        Yes.\n\n        Therefore, the plan:\n\n        Steps:\n\n        Read N, M\n        Read X = list of M islands\n\n        base = 0\n        diff = [0]*(N+2)  # index 0 unused, 1..N+1\n\n        for k in range(M-1):\n            a = X[k]\n            b = X[k+1]\n            forward = (b - a) % N\n            if forward == 0:\n                forward = N  # but won't happen\n            back = N - forward\n            if forward == back:\n                # tie, no penalty no matter which bridge is removed\n                base += forward  # = back, which is N/2, but integer? only if N even\n                continue\n\n            d_short = min(forward, back)\n            gap = abs(forward - back)\n            base += d_short\n\n            if forward < back:\n                start = a\n                length = forward\n            else:\n                start = b\n                length = back\n\n            R = start + length - 1\n            if R <= N:\n                diff[start] += gap\n                if R+1 <= N+1:\n                    diff[R+1] -= gap\n            else:\n                # wrap\n                diff[start] += gap\n                diff[N+1] -= gap   # cancel after N\n                diff[1] += gap\n                R_mod = R - N   # R_mod = start + length - 1 - N\n                if R_mod + 1 <= N+1:\n                    diff[R_mod + 1] -= gap\n\n        # Now compute penalties\n        cur = 0\n        min_cost = 10**18\n        for i in range(1, N+1):\n            cur += diff[i]\n            total_cost = base + cur\n            if total_cost < min_cost:\n                min_cost = total_cost\n\n        print(min_cost)\n\n        But wait: in the wrap case, we did:\n            diff[start] += gap  -> will be added to positions [start, N] because we subtract at N+1\n            diff[1] += gap\n            diff[R_mod+1] -= gap  -> so positions [1, R_mod]\n\n            But then when we do prefix sum, from 1 to R_mod: get gap from the second part\n            from start to N: get gap from the first part\n\n            But for i in [start, N] and [1, R_mod], cur will be increased by gap.\n\n            But what about i in [R_mod+1, start-1]? \n 