s a==b, but input ensures X_k != X_{k+1}, so skip\n            # But still, if it happens, use forward = N? But (b-a)%N==0 and a!=b only if a==b mod N, but a,b in [1,N] -> a==b\n            # So skip.\n            continue\n        back = N - forward\n        \n        if forward == back:\n            # Both paths are same, use either, no penalty if any bridge is removed\n            base += forward\n            continue\n        \n        d_short = min(forward, back)\n        gap = abs(forward - back)  # = N - 2*d_short\n        base += d_short\n        \n        if forward < back:\n            start = a\n            length = forward\n        else:\n            start = b\n            length = back\n        \n        # The short arc is a clockwise arc of `length` steps starting from island `start`\n        # It uses bridges: `start`, `start+1`, ..., `start+length-1` (as bridge indices), with wrap if needed.\n        R = start + length - 1  # last bridge index in the arc\n        if R <= N:\n            # No wrap\n            diff[start] += gap\n            diff[R + 1] -= gap\n        else:\n            # Wraps around\n            # First part: from `start` to `N`\n            diff[start] += gap\n            diff[N + 1] -= gap   # because we only care up to N\n            # Second part: from 1 to R-N\n            R_mod = R - N  # R_mod is the last bridge index in the second part\n            diff[1] += gap\n            diff[R_mod + 1] -= gap\n    \n    # Now compute the penalty for each bridge\n    current_penalty = 0\n    min_total = float('inf')\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        current_penalty += diff[i]\n        total_cost = base + current_penalty\n        if total_cost < min_total:\n            min_total = total_cost\n    \n    print(min_total)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nLet’s hope.\n\nBut sample1: we got 2, sample2:8.\n\nWe output.\n\nNote: We must not output anything else.\n\nWe call `solve()`.\n\nThis should be the solution.",
