      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    giants = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[index])\n        b = int(data[index + 1])\n        giants.append((a, b, i))  # (A_i, B_i, original_index)\n        index += 2\n    \n    # Sort giants by (B_i - A_i) in descending order\n    # This greedy approach works because we want to minimize the \"cost\" of having a giant\n    # with a large shoulder height but a small head height relative to their shoulder height.\n    # The key insight is that we should place giants with larger (B_i - A_i) first\n    # to avoid blocking the potential height gain from giants with larger head height.\n    # Actually, the correct criterion is to sort by (B_i - A_i) in descending order\n    # But more precisely, we sort by (B_i - A_i) descending.\n    giants.sort(key=lambda x: x[1] - x[0], reverse=True)\n    \n    # Calculate the total height of the topmost giant's head\n    current_height = 0\n    for a, b, idx in giants:\n        current_height += a\n        # The topmost giant's head height is the cumulative sum of all A_i + B_i of topmost\n        # But wait: this is not quite right.\n        # The topmost giant's head height is the sum of all A_i + B_i of the giants above?\n        # No. Let's re-express:\n        # Giant 1: head at A_1\n        # Giant 2: head at A_1 + B_2\n        # Giant 3: head at A_1 + A_2 + B_3\n        # So total = sum_{i=1}^{N-1} A_i + B_N\n        # But wait: the order is important.\n        # Actually, the head height of the last giant is:\n        # = (A_1) + (A_2) + ... + (A_{N-1}) + B_N\n        # But no: the first giant's head is A_1, and when we put giant 2 on top of giant 1, giant 2's head is at A_1 + B_2.\n        # So when we put giant N on the stack, its head is at height = (sum of A_i for all i=1 to N-1) + B_N.\n        # But that would be if we only add the A_i of the lower giants.\n        #\n        # Actually, let me trace the algorithm:\n        # Let stack be: P1, P2, ..., PN\n        # - P1: shoulders at A[P1], head at B[P1]\n        # - P2: stands on P1's shoulders, so its shoulders at A[P1] + A[P2], head at A[P1] + B[P2]\n        # - P3: shoulders at A[P1] + A[P2] + A[P3], head at A[P1] + A[P2] + B[P3]\n        # - ...\n        # - PN: head at A[P1] + A[P2] + ... + A[P_{N-1}] + B[P_N]\n        #\n        # So the total head height of the topmost giant is:\n        # sum_{i=1}^{N-1} A[P_i] + B[P_N]\n        #\n        # Therefore, we want to minimize the sum of A_i for the first N-1 giants, and maximize B_i for the last giant.\n        #\n        # However, we can choose the permutation. So we want to:\n        # - Put the giant with the largest B_i at the top\n        # - And among the remaining, we want to minimize the sum of A_i for the first N-1 giants\n        #\n        # But note: if we put a giant with a very large A_i in the middle, it increases the height of all giants above.\n        #\n        # Actually, the problem is more subtle: the constraint for placing giant i on top of giant j is that the height of j's shoulders must be at least the A_i of the new giant (which it is, because we are building up the stack). But we don't have to worry about support as long as we are stacking.\n\n        # So the total height = sum_{i=1}^{N-1} A[P_i] + B[P_N] = (sum of A_i for all giants) - A[P_N] + B[P_N]\n        # So total = (sum of all A_i) + (B[P_N] - A[P_N])\n        # Therefore, to maximize the total height, we should choose the giant with the maximum (B_i - A_i) as the topmost giant.\n        # But wait, is that the case? Let me check with sample 1:\n        # Giants: (4,10), (5,8), (2,9)\n        # Sum A_i = 4+5+2 = 11\n        # (B_i - A_i): [6, 3, 7] -> max is 7 (giant 3) -> then total = 11 + 7 = 18 -> matches!\n        #\n        # So the formula is: total = (total_sum_A) + max_i (B_i - A_i)\n        #\n        # But wait: in the stack, we are not necessarily using the giants' A_i for the top giant? Actually, we are using:\n        # The first N-1 giants contribute their A_i to the stack, but the top giant contributes only B_i (not A_i) because it's the top.\n        #\n        # So yes: total = sum of A_i for all giants except the top one + B_i of the top one\n        # = (sum of all A_i) - (A_top) + (B_top)\n        # = sum_A + (B_top - A_top)\n        #\n        # So we want to maximize (B_top - A_top) -> so choose the giant with maximum (B_i - A_i) as the top.\n        # And the rest can be arranged in any order (since their A_i are all being added to the stack height regardless of order).\n        #\n        # But wait: what if the top giant has a very large A_i? Then we are subtracting it from the sum, but if we choose a giant with a smaller A_i, we are subtracting less. However, since we add (B_i - A_i) and we are taking the max of (B_i - A_i), we are already maximizing the gain.\n\n        # So the answer is: sum of all A_i + max_i (B_i - A_i)\n\n        # Let me verify with sample 1:\n        # Giant1: A=4, B=10 -> B-A=6\n        # Giant2: A=5, B=8 -> B-A=3\n        # Giant3: A=2, B=9 -> B-A=7\n        # max = 7, total_A = 4+5+2 = 11, total=11+7=18 -> matches.\n\n        # Sample 2: all (1,1) -> each (B-A)=0, total_A=5, answer=5+0=5 -> matches.\n\n        # So the algorithm is:\n        #   total_A = sum of A_i for all giants\n        #   max_diff = max(B_i - A_i) for all i\n        #   answer = total_A + max_diff\n\n        pass  # we'll compute this\n\n    # So we don't need to sort at all! Just compute the total A and the max (B_i - A_i)\n    total_A = sum(giant[0] for giant in giants)\n    max_diff = max(giant[1] - giant[0] for giant in giants)\n    answer = total_A + max_diff\n    print(answer)\n\nsolve()\n```",
