      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().splitlines()\n    \n    # Read sheet A\n    H_A, W_A = map(int, data[0].split())\n    A = []\n    for i in range(1, 1 + H_A):\n        A.append(data[i])\n    \n    # Read sheet B\n    idx = 1 + H_A\n    H_B, W_B = map(int, data[idx].split())\n    B = []\n    for i in range(idx + 1, idx + 1 + H_B):\n        B.append(data[i])\n    \n    # Read sheet X\n    idx = idx + 1 + H_B\n    H_X, W_X = map(int, data[idx].split())\n    X = []\n    for i in range(idx + 1, idx + 1 + H_X):\n        X.append(data[i])\n    \n    # Get the positions of black squares in A, B, and X\n    def get_black_positions(sheet, H, W):\n        positions = []\n        for i in range(H):\n            for j in range(W):\n                if sheet[i][j] == '#':\n                    positions.append((i, j))\n        return positions\n    \n    A_blacks = get_black_positions(A, H_A, W_A)\n    B_blacks = get_black_positions(B, H_B, W_B)\n    X_blacks = get_black_positions(X, H_X, W_X)\n    \n    total_black_count = len(A_blacks) + len(B_blacks)\n    if total_black_count != len(X_blacks):\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n    \n    # Try all possible placements of A and B on the infinite sheet C\n    # We'll simulate a sufficiently large canvas (since all dimensions <= 10)\n    # We can bound the possible offsets\n    # We'll shift A and B relative to a common origin\n    \n    # We'll try all possible offsets for A and B such that their black squares can fit in an H_X x W_X region\n    # But instead, we can normalize the positions and check if the combined pattern matches X after shifting\n    \n    # For every possible offset where A is placed, and every possible offset where B is placed,\n    # we place A and B on a combined grid and see if some H_X x W_X region matches X exactly\n    # and contains all black squares of A and B\n    \n    # Let's consider placing A such that its top-left is at (a_offset_i, a_offset_j)\n    # and placing B such that its top-left is at (b_offset_i, b_offset_j)\n    # Then all black squares from A will be at (i + a_offset_i, j + a_offset_j)\n    # and from B at (i + b_offset_i, j + b_offset_j)\n    \n    # We then check for every possible cut-out of H_X x W_X if we get exactly X\n    \n    # But rather than try infinite space, we can consider that the relative positions are bounded\n    # The cut-out must contain all black squares from A and B\n    # So we can consider only placements where all black squares of A and B are within a bounded region\n    \n    # Try all possible relative shifts of A and B\n    # The overall bounding box of all black squares must fit in H_X x W_X\n    # So we can try to align the combined pattern with X\n    \n    # Alternative idea: Try every possible placement of the H_X x W_X cutout that can contain all black squares of A and B\n    # For A, the top-left placement can be chosen so that its black squares lie in some region\n    # Let’s fix the cutout area, then decide where A and B are placed such that their black squares fall in the cutout and form X\n    \n    # Instead: Generate all possible merged patterns from A and B with different offsets, then see if any matches X\n    \n    # Let’s try to place A such that its top-left corner is at (oa_i, oa_j) relative to the top-left of the cutout\n    # and B at (ob_i, ob_j)\n    # Then for each position in the cutout, check what would be painted\n    \n    found = False\n    \n    # Try all possible offsets for A and B relative to the top-left of X area\n    # The offset can be negative or positive, but bounded within [-10, 10] due to constraints\n    for oa_i in range(-10, 11):\n        for oa_j in range(-10, 11):\n            for ob_i in range(-10, 11):\n                for ob_j in range(-10, 11):\n                    # Create a grid for the cutout area H_X x W_X\n                    grid = [['.' for _ in range(W_X)] for _ in range(H_X)]\n                    valid = True\n                    all_positions = []\n                    \n                    # Apply A\n                    for i, j in A_blacks:\n                        ni, nj = i + oa_i, j + oa_j\n                        if 0 <= ni < H_X and 0 <= nj < W_X:\n                            grid[ni][nj] = '#'\n                            all_positions.append((ni, nj))\n                        else:\n                            # Black square of A is outside the cutout\n                            valid = False\n                            break\n                    if not valid:\n                        continue\n                    \n                    # Apply B\n                    for i, j in B_blacks:\n                        ni, nj = i + ob_i, j + ob_j\n                        if 0 <= ni < H_X and 0 <= nj < W_X:\n                            grid[ni][nj] = '#'\n                            all_positions.append((ni, nj))\n                        else:\n                            valid = False\n                            break\n                    if not valid:\n                        continue\n                    \n                    # Check if this grid matches X\n                    match = True\n                    for i in range(H_X):\n                        for j in range(W_X):\n                            if grid[i][j] != X[i][j]:\n                                match = False\n                                break\n                        if not match:\n                            break\n                    if match:\n                        # But also, do we have all black squares accounted for? They should be in the cutout\n                        # We already ensured A and B's blacks are in bounds, so this configuration is valid\n                        found = True\n                        break\n                if found:\n                    break\n            if found:\n                break\n        if found:\n            break\n    \n    if found:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
