    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given an undirected graph G with N vertices and M edges.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, M, the i-th edge is an undirected edge connecting vertices u_i and v_i.\nA graph with N vertices is called good if the following condition holds for all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K:\n\n- there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i in G.\n\nThe given graph G is good.\nYou are given Q independent questions. Answer all of them.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th question is as follows.\n\n- Is the graph G^{{(i)}} obtained by adding an undirected edge connecting vertices p_i and q_i to the given graph G good?\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nu_1 v_1\r\nu_2 v_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nu_M v_M\r\nK\r\nx_1 y_1\r\nx_2 y_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nx_K y_K\r\nQ\r\np_1 q_1\r\np_2 q_2\r\n\\vdots\r\np_Q q_Q\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Q lines.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th line should contain the answer to the i-th question: Yes if the graph G^{{(i)}} is good, and No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 0 \\leq M \\leq 2 \\times10^5\n- 1 \\leq u_i, v_i \\leq N\n- 1 \\leq K \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq x_i, y_i \\leq N\n- x_i \\neq y_i\n- i \\neq j \\implies \\lbrace x_i, y_i \\rbrace \\neq \\lbrace x_j, y_j \\rbrace\n- For all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K, there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i.\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq p_i, q_i \\leq N\n- p_i \\neq q_i\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 6\r\n1 2\r\n2 3\r\n2 3\r\n3 1\r\n5 4\r\n5 5\r\n3\r\n1 5\r\n2 6\r\n4 3\r\n4\r\n2 5\r\n2 6\r\n5 6\r\n5 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\nNo\r\nNo\r\nYes\r\nYes\r\n\n\n- For the first question, the graph G^{{(1)}} is not good because it has a path 1 \\rightarrow 2 \\rightarrow 5 connecting vertices x_1 = 1 and y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to efficiently determine whether adding a new edge to a graph preserves a certain \"goodness\" property — specifically, that for each of $ K $ forbidden pairs $ (x_i, y_i) $, there is **no path** between $ x_i $ and $ y_i $.\n\n### Key Ideas:\n\n1. The original graph $ G $ is *good*, so for all $ i $, $ x_i $ and $ y_i $ are in **different connected components**.\n2. We can precompute the connected components of the original graph $ G $ using **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union, DSU)**.\n3. After building the initial connected components, adding a new edge $ (p, q) $ may merge two components. We need to check:  \n   - Does this new connection cause **any** forbidden pair $ (x_i, y_i) $ to now be in the same component?  \n   - But checking all $ K $ pairs for each of the $ Q $ queries is too slow ($ O(QK) $) → we need optimization.\n\n### Optimized Approach:\n\nWe use the following strategy:\n\n- Precompute connected components of the original graph (with Union-Find).\n- For each forbidden pair $ (x_i, y_i) $, note that they must be in **different components**.\n- Assign a unique **component id** (via `find_root`) to each node.\n- For each unordered pair of component IDs $ (c_x, c_y) $ where $ c_x \\ne c_y $, if $ (x_i, y_i) $ is a forbidden pair between those components, we mark that the components **cannot be merged**.\n- When a new edge $ (p, q) $ is added:\n  - If $ p $ and $ q $ are already in the same component → adding the edge doesn’t change connectivity → graph remains good.\n  - Otherwise, we are merging two components $ c_p $ and $ c_q $. We must check whether any **forbidden pair** spans these two components.  \n    So, for performance, we pre-store **forbidden connections between component pairs**.\n\nBut: $ N, K, Q \\leq 2 \\times 10^5 $, so we can’t store all component-component pairs directly.\n\nInstead, here's a better way:\n\n### Efficient Strategy:\n\n1. Use Union-Find to build components from the $ M $ edges.\n2. Compress the graph: represent each node by its root (component leader).\n3. For each forbidden pair $ (x_i, y_i) $, normalize the component pair as $ (min(root_x, root_y), max(root_x, root_y)) $.\n4. Store all such forbidden **inter-component** pairs in a `set`.\n5. For each query $ (p, q) $:\n   - Find roots of $ p $ and $ q $.\n   - If same root → `Yes`.\n   - Otherwise, normalize the pair of roots → check if it exists in the forbidden set.\n     - If **yes**, then merging these components would connect some forbidden $ (x_i, y_i) $ → answer `No`.\n     - If **no**, then no forbidden pair is affected → answer `Yes`.\n\nWhy is this correct?\n\n- The only new connections created by adding $ (p, q) $ are between the two components being merged.\n- So, the only potentially violated forbidden pairs are those with one node in $ p $'s component and one in $ q $'s.\n- We precomputed all such **inter-component** forbidden pairs.\n- So if a forbidden pair exists across this component boundary, then merging them would make $ x_i $ and $ y_i $ connected → violates goodness.\n\nThus, we can answer each query in $ O(1) $ after preprocessing.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(300000)\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    ptr = 0\n    N = int(data[ptr]); ptr += 1\n    M = int(data[ptr]); ptr += 1\n    \n    # Union-Find initialization\n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    rank = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n    \n    # Read M edges and build initial graph\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(data[ptr]); v = int(data[ptr + 1]); ptr += 2\n        union(u, v)\n    \n    # Normalize all roots\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        find(i)\n    \n    # Read K forbidden pairs\n    K = int(data[ptr]); ptr += 1\n    forbidden_components = set()\n    \n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(data[ptr]); y = int(data[ptr + 1]); ptr += 2\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        # Since graph is good, rx != ry. But we can still add.\n        if rx > ry:\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        forbidden_components.add((rx, ry))\n    \n    # Read Q queries\n    Q = int(data[ptr]); ptr += 1\n    out_lines = []\n    \n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(data[ptr]); q = int(data[ptr + 1]); ptr += 2\n        rp = find(p)\n        rq = find(q)\n        if rp == rq:\n            # Already connected, no new paths introduced\n            out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            # Normalize component pair\n            if rp > rq:\n                rp, rq = rq, rp\n            if (rp, rq) in forbidden_components:\n                out_lines.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    # Output each result on a separate line\n    print('\\n'.join(out_lines))\n\nmain()\n```\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of Sample:\n\nGiven:\n- Graph has 6 nodes, 6 edges (including self-loop `5-5` and multi-edge `1-2`, `2-3`, `3-1` forming a cycle).\n- So components:\n  - `{1,2,3}` (connected via edges),\n  - `{4,5}` (via edge `5-4`; self-loop doesn't affect),\n  - `{6}` (isolated).\n- Forbidden pairs (must not be connected):\n  - `1-5`: components `1` and `5` → comp1: root of 1, comp2: root of 5 → form a banned component pair.\n  - `2-6`: comp of `2` and `6` → another banned inter-component pair.\n  - `4-3`: comp of 4 (=`5`) and 3 (=`1`) → same as `1-5`? But 3 and 4 → components `1` and `5` again → same as (min(root1, root5), ...) → same pair.\n\nWait: nodes:\n- After union-find, assume root of 1,2,3 → say `1`\n- root of 4,5 → say `4`\n- root of 6 → `6`\n\nThen:\n- (1,5): roots (1,4) → normalized (1,4)\n- (2,6): (1,6) → (1,6)\n- (4,3): (4,1) → (1,4) again → same as first\n\nSo forbidden component pairs: `{(1,4), (1,6)}`\n\nQueries:\n1. `2 5`: nodes 2 (comp 1), 5 (comp 4) → merging (1,4) → this is **forbidden** → **No**\n2. `2 6`: 2 (comp1), 6 (comp6) → (1,6) → **forbidden** → **No**\n3. `5 6`: 5 (comp4), 6 (comp6) → pair (4,6) → not in forbidden → **Yes**\n4. `5 4`: already in same component → nothing changes → **Yes**\n\nMatches sample output.\n\n---\n\n✅ This solution runs in $ O((M + K + Q) \\alpha(N)) $, which is efficient for the constraints.",
