yer $ i $, start as a singleton: `components[i] = {i}`, `size[i] = 1`, `ans[i] = 0`\n3. For each match between `p` and `q`:\n    - Find the components of `p` and `q`: say `comp_a` and `comp_b`, sizes `a`, `b`\n    - Compute `prob_a = a / (a+b)`, `prob_b = b / (a+b)` mod 998244353\n    - Add `prob_a` to **every player** in `comp_a`\n    - Add `prob_b` to **every player** in `comp_b`\n    - Merge the two components: new component with members `comp_a ∪ comp_b`\n    - Use small-to-large: iterate over the smaller list, update `ans[player] += add_value`, then merge\n\nWe need to:\n- Represent fractions mod 998244353\n- Division: use modular inverse\n\nWe'll define:\n```python\nMOD = 998244353\n```\n\nTo compute $ a / b \\mod MOD $, use $ a \\cdot b^{-1} \\mod MOD $\n\n---\n\n### Code Structure\n\n- Read input\n- `ans = [0]*(N+1)`\n- `parent[i] = i`, `size[i] = 1`, `members[i] = [i]` (we can store for root)\n- For each match:\n  - Find root of `p`, root of `q`\n  - If same, skip (shouldn't happen by constraint)\n  - Otherwise, get a = size[root_p], b = size[root_q]\n  - total = a + b\n  - prob_a = a * inv(total) % MOD\n  - prob_b = b * inv(total) % MOD\n  - Update all in `members[root_p]`: `ans[i] = (ans[i] + prob_a) % MOD`\n  - Update all in `members[root_q]`: `ans[i] = (ans[i] + prob_b) % MOD`\n  - Merge: choose new root, combine members using small-to-large\n  - Update parent, size, and members\n\nWe need `inv(x)` function.\n\n---\n\n### Runtime\n\nSmall-to-large merging ensures each player is moved $ O(\\log N) $ times. Total operations: $ O(N \\log N) $\n\nEach update is $ O(1) $, and each merge involves iterating and appending list.\n\nWe use list and extend, always merge small into large.\n\nWe need to update answers during the match, before merge.\n\nBut note: when merging, we are adding expected win counts from **this match** to each player in the two teams.\n\nEach match contributes $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $ to each player in first team, etc.\n\nYes.\n\n---\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nWe'll avoid full DSU path compression because we are tracking `members` per root, and we want fast lookup.\n\nBut we can do union-find with parent pointers, and update root correctly.\n\nBut simpler: we store `root[i]` and update it during merge.\n\nAlternative: we don’t do path compression, but update all members' root during merge.\n\nBut in small-to-large, we can update the root for each moved node.\n\nWe store:\n- `root[i]`: current root of i’s component\n- for each root, store: `size[root]`, `members[root]`\n\nWhen we merge small into large:\n- update `root[j]` for each j in small component to point to new root\n- add small’s members to large’s members\n- update size\n\nBut updating `root[j]` for $ O(\\text{size}) $ per merge is acceptable with small-to-large.\n\nWe do:\n\n```python\n# Initialize:\nroot = [i for i in range(n+1)]\nsz = [1] * (n+1)\n# members[i] will be list for root i; only valid for roots\nmembers = [[i] for i in range(n+1)]  # members[i] for root i\n```\n\nThen for each match (p, q):\n\n```python\nrp = root[p]\nrq = root[q]\nif rp == rq: continue  # shouldn't happen\na = sz[rp]\nb = sz[rq]\ntotal = a + b\n\n# Compute prob_a = a / total, prob_b = b / total mod MOD\nprob_a = a * mod_inverse(total, MOD) % MOD\nprob_b = b * mod_inverse(total, MOD) % MOD\n\n# Add to all in component rp\nfor player in members[rp]:\n    ans[player] = (ans[player] + prob_a) % MOD\n# Add to all in rq\nfor player in members[rq]:\n    ans[player] = (ans[player] + prob_b) % MOD\n\n# Merge small into large\nif len(members[rp]) > len(members[rq]):\n    main, minor = rp, rq\nelse:\n    main, minor = rq, rp\n\n# Merge minor into main\nfor player in members[minor]:\n    root[player] = main\n    # also append to main's members\n    members[main].append(player)\nmembers[main] = members[main]  # no need, just extend\n# but we already appended, so we can clear minor if we want\nsz[main] = len(members[main])\n# Invalidate minor somehow? Not necessary.\n\n# Also, we can clear minor's members to save space? Optional.\n\n```\n\nBut actually, we should avoid appending in loop — better to extend.\n\nBetter:\n\n```python\nmembers[main].extend(members[minor])\n```\n\nThen update `sz[main] = len(members[main])` or just `a + b`\n\nWe can just do `sz[main] = a + b`\n\nBut we already have that.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\nsz[main] = a + b\n```\n\nWe must be careful: `a` and `b` are from before merge.\n\nAlso, after merge, `minor` is no longer a root. We could avoid using it again.\n\nWe don’t need to clear, but we won’t use `minor` as root again.\n\n---\n\n### Modular Inverse\n\n```python\ndef mod_inverse(a, mod):\n    return pow(a, mod - 2, mod)\n```\n\nSince MOD is prime.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nNote: the input gives **player indices** starting at 1.\n\nWe'll store everything 1-indexed.\n\n---\n\n### Final Code\n\n```python\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef mod_inverse(a, mod=MOD):\n    return pow(a, mod - 2, mod)\n\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    matches = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        p = int(data[index]); q = int(data[index+1]); index += 2\n        matches.append((p, q))\n    \n    # Initialize DSU-like structures\n    root = [i for i in range(n+1)]  # root[i] is the representative of i's component\n    sz = [1] * (n+1)  # size of component for root i\n    # members[i] for i in 1..n: list of players in component if i is root\n    members = [[] for _ in range(n+1)]\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        members[i] = [i]\n    \n    ans = [0] * (n+1)  # ans[i] for player i\n    \n    # Process each match\n    for p, q in matches:\n        rp = root[p]\n        rq = root[q]\n        if rp == rq:\n            continue  # shouldn't happen\n        a = sz[rp]\n        b = sz[rq]\n        total = a + b\n        # Probability that rp's team wins: a/(a+b), so each player in rp gets a/(a+b)\n        # Similarly for rq\n        prob_a = a * m