bers[rq]` to avoid re-use? But we set `root[player]` so we won't use `members[rq]` again as root.\n\nBut we only use `members[root]`, and `rq` is no longer root — so safe.\n\nWe can leave it.\n\n---\n\n✅ **Final Answer Code**\n\n\n```python\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef mod_inverse(a, mod=MOD):\n    return pow(a, mod - 2, mod)\n\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    matches = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        p = int(data[index])\n        q = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        matches.append((p, q))\n    \n    root = list(range(n + 1))\n    members = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        members[i] = [i]\n    \n    ans = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    for p, q in matches:\n        rp = root[p]\n        rq = root[q]\n        if rp == rq:\n            continue\n        a = len(members[rp])\n        b = len(members[rq])\n        total = a + b\n        prob_a = a * mod_inverse(total, MOD) % MOD\n        prob_b = b * mod_inverse(total, MOD) % MOD\n        \n        for player in members[rp]:\n            ans[player] = (ans[player] + prob_a) % MOD\n        for player in members[rq]:\n            ans[player] = (ans[player] + prob_b) % MOD\n        \n        if len(members[rp]) < len(members[rq]):\n            rp, rq = rq, rp\n        \n        for player in members[rq]:\n            root[player] = rp\n        members[rp].extend(members[rq])\n        members[rq] = []\n    \n    print(\" \".join(str(x) for x in ans[1:]))\n\nmain()\n```",
