      "generated_answer": "We are given:\n\n- `N` plans, and a week consists of `A + B` days:\n  - Days `1` to `A`: **holidays**\n  - Days `A+1` to `A+B`: **weekdays**\n\n- Takahashi's `i`-th plan is scheduled `D_i` days from today.\n- He doesn't know what day of the week it is today.\n- We need to determine: **is there any starting day (i.e. any offset modulo the week length) such that *all* the plans fall on holidays?**\n\n---\n\n### Key Insight:\n\nLet the week have `L = A + B` days.\n\nEach `D_i` days from today means the day of the week for that plan is:\n\n```\n(today + D_i) mod L\n```\n\nWe can assume \"today\" is some unknown value `s` (mod `L`), where `s ∈ [0, L-1]` (we'll use 0-indexing for modulo math).\n\nThen the actual day of the week for plan `i` is:\n\n```\n(s + D_i) % L\n```\n\nWe want this to fall in the holiday part of the week.\n\nSince days 1 to A are holidays → in 0-indexing: **0 to A-1**.\n\nSo we need:\n\n```\n(s + D_i) % L ∈ [0, A-1]   for all i\n```\n\nOur goal: **does there exist an `s ∈ [0, L-1]`** such that for all `D_i`, `(s + D_i) % L < A`?\n\n---\n\n### Strategy\n\nRather than checking all `s` (which is impossible because `L` can be up to `2e9`), we can **analyze constraints on `s`**.\n\nFor a single `D_i`, the condition is:\n\n```\n(s + D_i) mod L < A\n```\n\nThis means `s` must lie in certain **intervals modulo L**.\n\nLet’s define:\n\n```\nLet x = s mod L\nWe want (x + D_i) mod L < A\n```\n\nWhich means:\n- Either `x + D_i < L` → then `x + D_i < A` → `x < A - D_i`, but this only applies if `x < L - D_i`\n- Or wrap around: `x + D_i >= L` → then `(x + D_i) % L = x + D_i - L`, and we want that `< A` → `x < A + L - D_i`\n\nAlternatively, it is easier to consider the **forbidden region** for `s`.\n\nBut a better idea: since the condition is periodic, we can **look at the valid intervals for `s`**.\n\n---\n\n### Alternative Insight:\n\nLet’s define `r_i = D_i mod L`.\n\nThen `(s + D_i) mod L = (s + r_i) mod L`.\n\nSo WLOG, we can assume 