. 3 in S1? no. So intersection empty.\n\nNot useful.\n\nTry:\n\n- S0: start=1, A=3 → [1,3] (1,2,3)\n- S1: start=2, A=3 → [2,4] (2,3,4)\n- S2: start=8, A=4 → [8,1] wrap: 8,9,0,1\n\nIntersection of [1,3], [2,4], [8,1] → must be in all.\n\n[1,3] ∩ [2,4] = [2,3]\n[2,3] ∩ [8,9,0,1] = empty\n\nTry:\n\n- S0: start=0, A=4 → [0,3]\n- S1: start=8, A=4 → [8,1] (8,9,0,1)\n- S2: start=9, A=3 → [9,1] (9,0,1)\n\nIntersection: [0,1]\n\nNow, starts are: 0,8,9\n\nTry s=0:\n- in S0: yes\n- in S1: 0 in [8,9,0,1] → yes\n- in S2: 0 in [9,0,1] → yes → works\n\nSo we find it.\n\nTry harder: is there a non-empty intersection that contains no start point?\n\nSuppose:\n- S0: start=1, A=3 → [1,3]\n- S1: start=2, A=2 → [2,3]\n- S2: start=3, A=1 → [3,3]\n\nThen intersection: only 3\n\n3 is the start of S2 → we try it.\n\nAnother:\n- S0: start=1, A=4 → [1,4]\n- S1: start=2, A=4 → [2,5]\n- S2: start=8, A=6 → [8,3] wrap: 8,9,0,1,2,3\n\nIntersection: max start = 2, min end = 3? wait\nS0: [1,4]\nS1: [2,5]\nS2: [8,3] → 8,9,0,1,2,3\n\nOverlap: values in [2,3] that are in S2 → 2,3 are in S2 → so [2,3]\n\nStarts: 1,2,8\n\nWe try:\n- s=1: in S1? 2<=1<=5? no\n- s=2: in S0: yes, S1: yes, S2: yes → works\n\nAgain we catch it.\n\nIt seems **if the intersection is non-empty, then at least one of the start points (i.e., `(-r_i) % L`) lies in it**, because the \"lowest\" index in the intersection (in some order) must be the start of some interval.\n\nBut on a circle, we can break at any point.\n\nBut since we try **all** start points, and the intersection is a sub-interval, and each interval contributes its start, and intersections are bounded by these, it is **very likely** that at least one start point lies in the intersection.\n\nActually, consider the point in the intersection with the smallest value. But if it's 0, and no interval starts at 0, might fail?\n\nBut that smallest-value point must be on the \"linear\" part.\n\nHowever, known similar problems (e.g. arc intersection) use the method of trying all endpoints.\n\nAnd in practice, this works.\n\nSo **proposed solution**:\n\n- L = A + B\n- For each D_i, compute r_i = D_i % L\n- For each i, candidate_s = (-r_i) % L  → equivalent to (L - r_i) % L, but if r_i==0 then 0\n- For each candidate_s in the set of such values (can be duplicate), check:\n  - For all j: (candidate_s + D_j) % L < A\n- If any candidate_s satisfies all, print \"Yes\"\n- If none does, print \"No\"\n\nBut wait: what if r_i = 0, candidate_s = 0\nr_j = 1, candidate_s = L-1\netc.\n\nBut we should avoid duplicates? No, duplicate is fine, but we can use a set.\n\nHowever, worst-case N=2e5, so we do 2e5 * 2e5 = 40e9 checks — too slow.\n\nWe need to optimize.\n\nFor each candidate, checking all j takes O(N), and we have O(N) candidates → O(N^2) → 4e10, too slow.\n\nSo we need a better way.\n\nAlternative: can we **compute the intersection of intervals directly**?\n\nYes.\n\nWe maintain the current intersection as a set of intervals on the circle.\n\nStart with [0, L-1]\n\nFor each `i`:\n- Compute the valid interval for `s`: \n  - start_i = (-r_i) % L\n  - end_i = (start_i + A - 1) % L\n  - But if start_i + A - 1 < L, then interval is [start_i, start_i + A - 1]\n  - else: wraps around: [start_i, L-1] and [0, (start_i + A - 1) % L]\n\nBut easier: store as one or two intervals.\n\nThen intersect.\n\nBut this is complicated.\n\nHowever, since `A` and `L` are large, but only `N` is 2e5, we can do this.\n\nBut easier: use the following:\n\nWe can try only **one candidate**: `s = 0`, and see if it works, but no.\n\nBack to known solutions.\n\nActually, a known solution for this exact problem is:\n\n- Let L = A+B\n- r_i = D_i % L\n- Sort r_i\n- Then look at the gaps between consecutive r_i\n\nBut wait, from sample input 3: A=347, B=347, L=694\nD = [347,700,705,710]\n\nr_i = D_i % 694: \n- 347 % 694 = 347\n- 700 % 694 = 6\n- 705 % 694 = 11\n- 710 % 694 = 16\n\nSo r_i = [347, 6, 11, 16] → sorted: [6,11,16,347]\n\nThe condition is: (s + r_i) % 694 < 347\n\nWe want this for all i.\n\nThe longest gap between consecutive r_i (in sorted order, and considering circle) determines if there's room for a full holiday period.\n\nBut here's a known idea: if the plans are at r_i, then the condition fails if for some i, the next plan is more than A days away in the cycle.\n\nBut not exactly.\n\nActually: consider the sorted r_i.\n\nThen the values (s + r_i) mod L must all be in [0, A-1]\n\nThis means that the **distance between any two r_i and r_j** in the cycle must be such that they can both be mapped into an interval of length A.\n\nBut the whole set of r_i shifted by s must lie in an arc of length A.\n\nTherefore: the condition is equivalent to: **the minimum length of an arc that contains all the r_i is <= A**\n\nBecause if there's an arc of length A that contains all r_i, then setting s such that s + r_i falls in [0, A-1] is possible.\n\nWait, not exactly.\n\nWe want: there exists s such that for all i, (s + r_i) mod L in [0, A-1]\n\nWhich means: the set { -s } + r_i mod L ⊆ [0, A-1]\n\nSo the set r_i mod L must be contained in [ -s, -s + A - 1 ] mod L\n\nSo the set {r_i} must be contained in some arc of length A.\n\nTherefore, **the answer is \"Yes\" if and only if the minimum arc length that contains all the r_i is <= A**\n\nYes! This is the key.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n- Let L = A + B\n- r_i = D_i % L for each i\n- If the set of r_i can be covered by some arc of length A, then \"Yes\", else \"No\"\n\nHow to compute the minimum arc length that contains all r_i?\n\n- Sort the r_i\n- Consider the circle, try all gaps between consecutive points (in sorted order, with gap from last to first)\n- The minimum arc is L - max_gap\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause the largest gap between consecutive points (in sorted order) is the gap that you don't need to cover.\n\nSo the smallest arc containing all points is `L - max_gap_between_consecutive`\n\nThen we need: `L - max_gap <= A`\n i.e., `max_gap >= L - A = B`\n\nBut wait:\n\n- If the largest gap between consecutive r_i is G, then the points are contained in an arc of length L - G.\n\nWe want L - G <= A  → G >= L - A = B\n\nSo condition: **max_gap >= B**\n\nThen it can be covered by an arc of length A.\n\nBut let's test.\n\nSample 1: r_i = [1,2,2] → unique: [1,2], but duplicates? keep all? no, but we should use unique or not?\n\nActually, the set is {1,2}\n\nSorted: [1,2]\nGaps: 2-1=1, and from 2 to 1: 1->2 is 1, 2->1 is L-1=6\n\nSo gaps: 1 and 6 → max_gap = 6\n\nL=7, B=5\n\nmax_gap=6 >= B=5 → true → \"Yes\" → correct.\n\nSample 2: N=2, A=5, B=10, L=15\nD=[10,15] -> r=[10,0] (15%15=0)\n\nSorted: [0,10]\nGaps: 10-0=10, and from 10 to 0: 0 - 10 + 15 = 5? wait, gap from 10 to 0 is (0 - 10) mod 15 = 5? no.\n\nConsecutive in sorted order: [0,10]\n- gap from 0 to 10: 10\n- gap from 10 to 0: (0 + L - 10) = 5\n\nSo max_gap = 10\n\nB=10\n\nmax_gap >= B → 10>=10 → true → \"Yes\", but expected \"No\"\n\nContradiction.\n\nWait, expected \"No\".\n\nBut our condition gives \"Yes\".\n\nSo mistake.\n\nWe want the set {0,10} to be coverable by an arc of length A=5.\n\nIs there an arc of length 5 that contains both 0 and 10?\n\nDistance from 0 to 10 is 10, from 10 to 0 is 5.\n\nThe smallest arc containing both: length min(10,5)? but to contain both, the arc must cover from 10 to 0 clockwise: 10,11,12,13,14,0 -> length 5 (6 points) but arc length in terms of difference.\n\nThe smallest arc containing 0 and 10: \n- if we go from 0 to 10: length 10\n- if we go from 10 to 0: length 5 (10->11->12->13->14->0, that's 5 steps, so length 5 in terms of difference is 5.\n\nBut an arc of length 5 can be [10,14] doesn't include 0.\n[11,0] -> from 11 to 0 is 4 steps? (11,12,13,14,0) -> indices covered, difference is 5 if we allow [x, x+5) mod L.\n\nUsually, arc of length A means a set of the form [x, x+A) mod L.\n\nCan we cover 0 and 10 with an arc of length 5?\n\nTry:\n- [10,15) = [10,0) -> includes 10,11,12,13,14 -> not 0\n- [9,14) -> includes 9,10,11,12,13 -> not 0\n- [0,5) -> 0,1,2,3,4 -> not 10\n-