lem statement, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- P_{{i, j, k}} is # or ..\n- The given polyominoes are connected. In other words, the squares that make up a polyomino can be reached from each other by following only the squares up, down, left, and right.\n- The given polyominoes are not empty.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n....\r\n###.\r\n.#..\r\n....\r\n....\r\n.###\r\n.##.\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n.##.\r\n.##.\r\n.##.\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\r\n\nThe figure below shows the shapes of the polyominoes corresponding to Sample Input 1.\n\nIn this case, you can fill the grid with them to satisfy the conditions in the problem statement by placing them as shown in the figure below.\n\nThus, the answer is Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n###.\r\n#.#.\r\n##..\r\n....\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\r\n####\r\n##..\r\n#...\r\n#...\n\nSample Output 2\n\nYes\r\n\nAs in the first polyomino in Sample Input 2, a polyomino may be in the shape of a polygon with a hole.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n##..\r\n#..#\r\n####\r\n....\r\n....\r\n##..\r\n.##.\r\n....\r\n.#..\r\n.#..\r\n.#..\r\n.#..\n\nSample Output 3\n\nNo\r\n\nNote that the polyominoes may not be flipped over when filling the grid.\n\nSample Input 4\n\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\n\nSample Output 4\n\nNo\n\nSample Input 5\n\n....\r\n####\r\n#...\r\n#...\r\n....\r\n####\r\n...#\r\n..##\r\n....\r\n..##\r\n..#.\r\n..##\n\nSample Output 5\n\nNo\n\nSample Input 6\n\n###.\r\n.##.\r\n..#.\r\n.###\r\n....\r\n...#\r\n..##\r\n...#\r\n....\r\n#...\r\n#...\r\n#...\n\nSample Output 6\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    from itertools import permutations\n    \n    # Read input\n    lines = []\n    for line in sys.stdin:\n        lines.append(line.strip())\n    \n    # Parse the three polyominoes\n    polyominoes = []\n    for i in range(3):\n        grid = []\n        for j in range(4):\n            grid.append(list(lines[i*4 + j]))\n        polyominoes.append(grid)\n    \n    def count_hash(pattern):\n        return sum(row.count('#') for row in pattern)\n    \n    total_hashes = sum(count_hash(poly) for poly in polyominoes)\n    if total_hashes != 16:\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n    \n    def rotate90(grid):\n        return [[grid[3-j][i] for j in range(4)] for i in range(4)]\n    \n    def get_rotations(grid):\n        rotations = []\n        curr = [row[:] for row in grid]\n        for _ in range(4):\n            rotations.append(curr)\n            curr = rotate90(curr)\n        # Remove duplicates\n        unique_rotations = []\n        for r in rotations:\n            if not any(all(r[i][j] == ur[i][j] for i in range(4) for j in range(4)) for ur in unique_rotations):\n                unique_rotations.append(r)\n        return unique_rotations\n    \n    def get_offsets_and_shape(grid):\n        # Find the positions of '#' in the grid\n        positions = []\n        for i in range(4):\n            for j in range(4):\n                if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                    positions.append((i, j))\n        \n        # Normalize positions so the top-left '#' is at (0,0)\n        if not positions:\n            return []\n        \n        min_i = min(pos[0] for pos in positions)\n        min_j = min(pos[1] for pos in positions)\n        normalized = [(i - min_i, j - min_j) for i, j in positions]\n        return sorted(normalized)\n    \n    def can_place(grid, board, offset_i, offset_j):\n        for i in range(4):\n            for j in range(4):\n                if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                    ni, nj = i + offset_i, j + offset_j\n                    if ni < 0 or ni >= 4 or nj < 0 or nj >= 4:\n                        return False\n                    if board[ni][nj] != '.':\n                        return False\n        return True\n    \n    def place(grid, board, offset_i, offset_j, marker):\n        new_board = [row[:] for row in board]\n        for i in range(4):\n            for j in range(4):\n                if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                    ni, nj = i + offset_i, j + offset_j\n                    new_board[ni][nj] = marker\n        return new_board\n    \n    # Get all possible placements for each polyomino\n    polyomino_placements = []\n    for poly in polyominoes:\n        placements = []\n        rotations = get_rotations(poly)\n        for rot in rotations:\n            # Try all possible translations\n            for offset_i in range(-3, 4):\n                for offset_j in range(-3, 4):\n                    if can_place(rot, [['.' for _ in range(4)] for _ in range(4)], offset_i, offset_j):\n                        shape = get_offsets_and_shape(rot)\n                        # Apply offset to the shape\n                        placed_shape = [(i + offset_i, j + offset_j) for i, j in shape]\n                        # Filter out positions outside the 4x4 grid\n                        if all(0 <= i < 4 and 0 <= j < 4 for i, j in placed_shape):\n                            placements.append(placed_shape)\n        # Remove duplicates\n        unique_placements = []\n        for p in placements:\n            if p not in unique_placements:\n                unique_placements.append(p)\n        polyomino_placements.append(unique_placements)\n    \n    # Try all permutations of polyominoes and their placements\n    for poly_order in permutations(range(3)):\n        for placement_0 in polyomino_placements[poly_order[0]]:\n            # Create board with first polyomino placed\n            board1 = [['.' for _ in range(4)] for _ in range(4)]\n            for i, j in placement_0:\n                board1[i][j] = '0'\n            \n            for placement_1 in polyomino_placements[poly_order[1]]:\n                # Check if second polyomino can be placed without overlapping\n                valid = True\n                for i, j in placement_1:\n                    if board1[i][j] != '.':\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if not valid:\n                    continue\n                \n                # Place second polyomino\n                board2 = [row[:] for row in board1]\n                for i, j in placement_1:\n                    board2[i][j] = '1'\n                \n                # Try to place third polyomino in remaining spaces\n                remaining = []\n                for i in range(4):\n                    for j in range(4):\n                        if board2[i][j] == '.':\n                            remaining.append((i, j))\n                \n                # Check if the remaining cells match exactly one of the placements of the third polyomino\n                third_poly_i = poly_order[2]\n                found_match = False\n                for placement_2 in polyomino_placements[third_poly_i]:\n                    if sorted(placement_2) == sorted(remaining):\n                        found_match = True\n                        break\n                \n                if found_match:\n                    print(\"Yes\")\n                    return\n    \n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
