    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nA regular pentagon P is shown in the figure below.\n\nDetermine whether the length of the line segment connecting points S_1 and S_2 of P equals the length of the line segment connecting points T_1 and T_2.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nS_1S_2\r\nT_1T_2\n\nOutput\n\nIf the length of the line segment connecting points S_1 and S_2 of P equals the length of the line segment connecting points T_1 and T_2, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- Each of S_1, S_2, T_1, and T_2 is one of the characters A, B, C, D, and E.\n- S_1 \\neq S_2\n- T_1 \\neq T_2\n\nSample Input 1\n\nAC\r\nEC\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\r\n\nThe length of the line segment connecting point A and point C of P equals the length of the line segment connecting point E and point C.\n\nSample Input 2\n\nDA\r\nEA\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\r\n\nThe length of the line segment connecting point D and point A of P does not equal the length of the line segment connecting point E and point A.\n\nSample Input 3\n\nBD\r\nBD\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to determine whether two given line segments (chords) in a **regular pentagon** have the same length.\n\nIn a **regular pentagon**, all sides and angles are equal. The vertices are labeled (in order) as A, B, C, D, E. Because of the symmetry, the length of a chord between two vertices depends only on the **number of steps** between them along the perimeter.\n\nLet’s assign positions:\n\n```\nA = 0\nB = 1\nC = 2\nD = 3\nE = 4\n```\n\nFor any two points, the shortest distance (in terms of steps around the pentagon) is:\n\n```python\nmin(|i - j|, 5 - |i - j|)\n```\n\nThis gives the **step distance** (or arc length) between two points. In a regular pentagon, chords that span the same number of sides (i.e., have the same step distance) are of equal length.\n\nSo, two chords are of equal length if their **step distances** are equal.\n\n### Step distances in pentagon:\n- Distance 1: adjacent vertices → side of pentagon\n- Distance 2: skip one vertex → diagonal\n- Distance 3: same as distance 2 (because min(3, 5-3)=2)\n- Distance 4: same as distance 1\n\nSo only two distinct chord lengths exist: \n- length corresponding to distance 1 (or 4)\n- length corresponding to distance 2 (or 3)\n\nThus, we can compute the **circular distance** for both pairs and compare.\n\n### Plan:\n1. Map each vertex to an index: A→0, B→1, C→2, D→3, E→4\n2. For each pair (S1, S2) and (T1, T2), compute the circular distance:\n   ```python\n   d = abs(i - j)\n   step = min(d, 5 - d)\n   ```\n3. If both pairs have the same step distance → print \"Yes\", else \"No\"\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    # Read input\n    line1 = input().strip()\n    line2 = input().strip()\n    \n    # Map points to indices\n    mapping = {'A': 0, 'B': 1, 'C': 2, 'D': 3, 'E': 4}\n    \n    s1, s2 = line1[0], line1[1]\n    t1, t2 = line2[0], line2[1]\n    \n    # Get indices\n    i1, i2 = mapping[s1], mapping[s2]\n    j1, j2 = mapping[t1], mapping[t2]\n    \n    # Compute circular distance (minimum steps along pentagon)\n    def chord_length(a, b):\n        diff = abs(a - b)\n        return min(diff, 5 - diff)\n    \n    d1 = chord_length(i1, i2)\n    d2 = chord_length(j1, j2)\n    \n    if d1 == d2:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nmain()\n```",
