      "generated_answer": "We are given a **weighted directed graph** with possible **negative weights**, but **no negative cycles**, and we are to:\n\n- Determine whether there exists a **walk** that visits **each vertex at least once**.\n- If such a walk exists, compute the **minimum total weight** over all such walks.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations:\n\n- The graph has up to $ N = 20 $ vertices → allows for bitmasks over subsets.\n- We can use **dynamic programming with state (vertex, visited_mask)**.\n- The walk may revisit vertices and reuse edges → so we're not limited to simple paths.\n- Since negative weights are allowed but **no negative cycles**, we can still use shortest paths safely as long as we avoid infinite loops.\n- We need the **shortest walk** that **visits all vertices at least once**, possibly reusing edges.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy:\n\nWe will:\n1. Compute all-pairs shortest paths using **Floyd-Warshall**, since $ N \\leq 20 $.\n   - This lets us know the shortest distance from any vertex $ i $ to $ j $, even via intermediate nodes.\n2. Once we have shortest paths, we can consider transitions between vertices in a TSP-like dynamic programming.\n\nBut note: **we don’t require visiting each vertex exactly once**, but **at least once**. And we’re minimizing weight. Because of this, and due to **negative edges**, revisiting might help reduce cost. However, if we have shortest paths (without negative cycles), then going from $ i $ to $ j $ we can assume we take the shortest route.\n\nSo, the **optimal walk** can be thought of as moving from one vertex to another via shortest paths, forming a **superpath** that covers all vertices.\n\nBut can we just reduce it to a TSP on the shortest path graph?\n\nYes — because:\n\n- We can always replace any segment from $ u $ to $ v $ by the shortest path between them.\n- The minimum total cost walk that visits all vertices can be viewed as visiting the vertices in some order $ v_1, v_2, \\dots, v_k $ where $ k \\geq N $, but we only care about visiting all $ N $ vertices at least once.\n\nHowever, because we can **reuse** edges and **revisit** vertices, the best way to go from a vertex $ u $ to $ v $ is via the shortest path.\n\nSo we can:\n- Compute $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $: shortest path from $ i $ to $ j $ for all $ i, j $ using Floyd-Warshall.\n- Then use **DP over subsets**: $ \\text{dp}[mask][i] $ = minimum total weight to have visited all vertices in $ mask $, ending at vertex $ i $.\n- Start DP from each vertex.\n- Transition: from vertex $ i $ to $ j $ not yet visited (or even if visited, but since we are doing shortest paths, and mask only grows, we assume going to unvisited might be useful — but we can also stay in mask and go to already visited; however, that would only be useful if it leads to cheaper way to go out later — but since Floyd-Warshall already captured shortest paths, we can restrict transitions to go only to unvisited nodes? No — not necessarily.)\n\nWait — actually, in the subset DP used in TSP, we only go from visited set to unvisited, and use shortest path between nodes.\n\nBut in our case, **we are allowed to go to any node at any time**, but since we already have shortest paths, **going from $ i $ to $ j $ costs $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $**.\n\nAnd the idea is: if we are at $ i $, have visited some set $ S $, and want to go to $ j $ (whether in $ S $ or not), it would not grow the set if $ j \\in S $. So **only going to unvisited nodes expands the state**.\n\nHence, we design a **TSP-style DP** that begins at each vertex, and then transitions only to **not yet visited** vertices, using shortest paths.\n\nBut wait: what if the shortest way to reach a new vertex goes *through* already visited ones? That's fine. The **dist[i][j]** already accounts for that.\n\nAnd in our DP: from state $ (mask, i) $, we can go to any $ j \\notin mask $, and the cost is $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $. Once all vertices are visited, we are done.\n\nBut note: we don’t need to return anywhere. We just need to have visited all vertices. So we can stop as soon as mask is full.\n\nBut is the optimal walk necessarily corresponds to a path that goes from vertex to vertex directly via shortest paths, only adding unvisited nodes?\n\nYes — because:\n\n- Any optimal walk can be decomposed into segments between the **first visits** to each vertex.\n- Between the first time we visit $ u $ and the first time we visit a new vertex $ v $, we go from $ u $ to $ v $, possibly visiting others (already visited), so the minimum cost is $ \\text{dist}[u][v] $.\n- Therefore, we can model the process as starting at some vertex, and then moving to unvisited vertices via shortest paths.\n\nHence, **this reduces to an asymmetric TSP on the shortest path metric**, but we don't need to return to start — it's the **minimum cost path that visits all vertices**, starting anywhere.\n\nSo we do:\n\n1. Compute all-pairs shortest paths using Floyd-Warshall.\n2. If for any $ i $, $ \\text{dist}[i][i] < 0 $, that would be a negative cycle — but problem says no negative cycles, so we can ignore.\n3. Use DP with bit masks:\n\n   $$\n   \\text{dp}[mask][i] = \\text{minimum cost to have visited all vertices in } mask, \\text{ ending at vertex } i\n   $$\n\n4. Initialize: $ \\text{dp}[1 << (i-1)][i] = 0 $ for all $ i $. (Because we start at vertex $ i $ with cost 0 — no edge traversed yet.)\n\nWait: we start *at* a vertex with no edge cost. So total edge cost is 0 so far.\n\n5. Then for each mask, for each $ i \\in mask $, for each $ j \\notin mask $, update:\n\n   $$\n   \\text{dp}[mask \\cup \\{j\\}][j] = \\min(\\text{dp}[mask \\cup \\{j\\}][j], \\text{dp}[mask][i] + \\text{dist}[i][j])\n   $$\n\n6. After DP, answer is $ \\min_{i} \\text{dp}[(1<<N)-1][i] $\n\n7. But: what if $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $ is infinity (i.e., no path)? Then we skip.\n\nSo we need to check connectivity: **from a vertex $ i $, can we reach $ j $?**\n\nBut Floyd-Warshall will help us detect that.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Steps:\n\n- Read input.\n- Initialize $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $: if $ i = j $, 0; else $ \\infty $.\n- Add edges: $ \\text{dist}[u][v] = \\min(\\text{dist}[u][v], w) $\n- Run Floyd-Warshall.\n- If any $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $ remains $ \\infty $, it means no path from $ i $ to $ j $.\n- But to complete the walk, we need to be able to reach all vertices from some walk.\n- Our DP will only be able to expand mask if $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $ is finite.\n\nSo in DP, only consider transitions where $ \\text{dist}[i][j] $ is finite.\n\n- Initialize DP table with `inf`, size $ 2^N \\times N $\n- For each vertex $ i $, $ \\text{dp}[1 << i][i] = 0 $\n- Iterate over all masks, for each vertex $ i $ in mask, if $ \\text{dp}[mask][i] $ is finite, then for each vertex $ j $ not in mask, and if $ \\text{dist}[i+1][j+1] $ is finite (adjusting for 1-indexing), update.\n- Finally, min over $ \\text{dp}[(1<<N)-1][i] $ for $ i \\in [0, N-1] $\n\nIf the min is still $ \\infty $, print \"No\", else print the min.\n\n---\n\n### But wait: Sample Input 2\n```\n3 2\n1 2 0\n2 1 0\n```\nWe have only edges: 1→2, 2→1. So vertex 3 is **unreachable**.\nSo in Floyd-Warshall, $ \\text{dist}[i][3] $ will be inf for all i, and $ \\text{dist}[3][i] $ also inf (except itself), so starting from 1 or 2 we can never reach 3.\n\nHence, final mask of 7 (111) will not be reached → answer \"No\" → correct.\n\nAlso, Floyd-Warshall will show no way to get to vertex 3, so mask with bit 2 (which is vertex 3) can't be reached.\n\n---\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nWe'll use `float('inf')` for infinity.\n\nNote: vertices are 1-indexed in input → we store dist[0][1] = dist from vertex 1 to vertex 2.\n\nSo in code:\n- Convert vertex $ x $ to index $ x-1 $\n\nSteps:\n1. Read N, M\n2. Initialize dist[0..N-1][0..N-1] with inf, and 0 on diagonal\n3. For each edge, update dist[u-1][v-1] = min(dist[u-1][v-1], w)\n4. Run Floyd-Warshall\n5. Check: after Floyd-Warshall, if for every possible starting vertex, we cannot reach some other vertex, then we might not be able to cover all. But Floyd will reveal that.\n6. Then do TSP-like DP on subset of visited vertices.\n\n---\n\n### Let’s test on Sample 1:\n\n```\nN=3, M=4\nEdges:\n1 2 5     → 0→1: 5\n2 1 -3    → 1→0: -3\n2 3 -4    → 1→2: -4\n3 1 100   → 2→0: 100\n```\n\nCompute Floyd-Warshall (3x3):\n\nInitialize:\n```\ndist = [\n  [0, 5, inf],\n  [-3, 0, -4],\n  [100, inf, 0]\n]\n```\n\nAfter Floyd-Warshall:\n\n- i=0 (via 0): no change\n- i=1: via 1\n\n  - 0→1→2: 5 + (-4) = 1 → update dist[0][2] = 1\n  - 0→1→0: 5 + (-3) = 2\n  - 2→1→2: 100 + (-4) = 96\n  - etc.\n\nLet's compute:\n\nAfter allowing intermediate 0:\n- nothing much via 0\n\nBetter to do full:\n\n**Step 0: dist**\n```\n[  0,   5, inf]\n[ -3,   0,  -4]\n[100, inf,   0]\n```\n\n**k=0 (via node 0):**\n- i→j via 0: dist[i][0] + dist[0][j]\n\n- i=1: dist[1][0] = -3, so:\n  - 1→0→1: -3 + 5 = 2, but 0 < 2 → no improve\n  - 1→0→2: -3 + inf = inf\n- i=2: dist[2][0]=100:\n  - 2→0→1: 100+5=105 → better than inf → update dist[2][1]=105\n  - 2→0→2: skip\n- i=0: nothing\n- So dist[2][1] = 105\n\nNow:\n```\n[  0,   5, inf]\n[ -3,   0,  -4]\n[100, 105,   0]\n```\n\n**k=1 (via node 1):**\n\n- dist[i][1] + dist[1][j]\n\n- dist[0][1]=5:\n  - 0→1→0: 5 + (-3)=2 → update dist[0][0]? 0 vs 2 → no\n  - 0→1→2: 5 + (-4)=1 → update dist[0][2]=1\n- dist[1][1]=0: nothing\n- dist[2][1]=105:\n  - 2→1→0: 105 + (-3)=102 → better than 100? no\n  - 2→1→2: 105 + (-4)=101 → worse than 0 → no\n\nNow dist[0][2] = 1\n\nNow:\n```\n[0,  5,  1]\n[-3, 0, -4]\n[100,105,0]\n```\n\n**k=2 (via node 2):**\n- dist[i][2] + dist[2][j]\n\n- dist[0][2]=1:\n  - 0→2→0: 1 + 100 = 101 → worse than 0\n  - 0→2→1: 1 + 105 = 106 → worse than 5\n- dist[1][2]=-4:\n  - 1→2→0: -4 + 100 = 96 → better than -3? no\n  - 1→2→1: -4 + 105 = 101 → worse than 0\n- dist[2][2]=0: nothing\n\nSo final dist:\n\n```python\ndist = [\n  [0, 5, 1],\n  [-3, 0, -4],\n  [100, 105, 0]\n]\n```\n\nNow run TSP-like DP:\n\ndp[mask][i] for mask in [1..7], i in [0,1,2]\n\nInitialize:\n\n- dp[1<<0][0] = dp[1][0] = 0\n- dp[1<<1][1] = dp[2][1] = 0\n- dp[1<<2][2] = dp[4][2] = 0\n\nNow expand:\n\nFrom mask=1 (0b001), i=0:\n- j=1 (not in mask): dp[3][1] = min(dp[3][1], 0 + dist[0][1] = 5)\n- j=2: dp[5][2] = 0 + dist[0][2] = 1\n\nFrom mask=2 (0b010), i=1:\n- j=0: dp[3][0] = 0 + dist[1][0] = -3\n- j=2: dp[6][2] = 0 + dist[1][2] = -4\n\nFrom mask=4 (0b100), i=2:\n- j=0: dp[5][0] = 0 + dist[2][0] = 100\n- j=1: dp[6][1] = 0 + dist[2][1] = 105\n\nNow mask=3 (0b011), contains 0,1:\n- From i=0: j=2 → dp[7][2] = dp[3][0] + dist[0][2] = -3 + 1 = -2\n- From i=1: j=2 → dp[7][2] = min(-2, dp[3][1] + dist[1][2] = 5 + (-4) = 1) → so -2\n\nMask=5 (0b101): 0,2\n- i=0: j=1 → dp[7][1] = dp[5][0] + dist[0][1] = 100 + 5 = 105\n- i=2: j=1 → dp[7][1] = min(105, dp[5][2] + dist[2][1] = 1 + 105 = 106) → 105\n\nMask=6 (0b110): 1,2\n- i=1: j=0 → dp[7][0] = dp[6][1] + dist[1][0] = 105 + (-3) = 102\n- i=2: j=0 → dp[7][0] = min(102, dp[6][2] + dist[2][0] = -4 + 100 = 96) → 96\n\nSo dp[7][0] = 96, dp[7][1] = 105, dp[7][2] = -2\n\nMinimum = -2 → correct.\n\nSo the answer is -2.\n\n---\n\nNow, let's code accordingly.\n\nWe assume that `dist[i][j]` may remain `inf`, so we skip transitions where `dist[i][j] == float('inf')`.\n\nWe do the same for initialization: only run DP if the dist between nodes is finite.\n\n---\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n    \n    # Parse first two tokens as N, M\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n    \n    INF = float('inf')\n    # Distance matrix for Floyd-Warshall, 0-indexed\n    dist = [[INF] * N for _ in range(N)]\n    \n    # Initialize diagonal to 0\n    for i in range(N):\n        dist[i][i] = 0\n        \n    # Read edges\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(it)) - 1\n        v = int(next(it)) - 1\n        w = int(next(it))\n        if w < dist[u][v]:\n            dist[u][v] = w\n            \n    # Floyd-Warshall\n    for k in range(N):\n        for i in range(N):\n            if dist[i][k] == INF:\n                continue\n            for j in range(N):\n                if dist[i][k] != INF and dist[k][j] != INF:\n                    if dist[i][j] > dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]:\n                        dist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]\n    \n    # DP over subsets: dp[mask][i] = min cost to have visited all vertices in mask and ended at i\n    total_mask = 1 << N\n    dp = [[INF] * N for _ in range(total_mask)]\n    \n    # Initialize: starting at each vertex, with no edge cost (we are just there)\n    for i in range(N):\n        dp[1 << i][i] = 0\n        \n    # Iterate over all masks\n    for mask in range(total_mask):\n        # For each vertex i in the current mask\n        for i in range(N):\n            if dp[mask][i] == INF:\n                continue\n            if not (mask & (1 << i)):\n                continue\n            # Try to go to any vertex j not in mask\n            for j in range(N):\n                if mask & (1 << j):\n                    continue\n                if dist[i][j] == INF:\n                    continue\n                new_mask = mask | (1 << j)\n                new_cost = dp[mask][i] + dist[i][j]\n                if new_cost < dp[new_mask][j]:\n                    dp[new_mask][j] = new_cost\n                    \n    # Find the minimum cost to have visited all vertices\n    full_mask = total_mask - 1\n    ans = min(dp[full_mask][i] for i in range(N))\n    \n    if ans == INF:\n        print(\"No\")\n    else:\n        print(ans)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet’s test with the samples.\n\n### Sample 2:\n- N=3, M=2\n- Edges: 1→2, 2→1 → only vertices 0 and 1 connected, vertex 2 not reachable.\n- So from vertex 0 or 1, cannot reach 2 → dist[0][2] = INF, dist[1][2] = INF\n- In DP:\n  - Start mask: 1 (0b001) at 0 → can go to 1 → mask 3 → then try to go to 2 → but dist[1][2] = INF? wait — we had direct edge 2→3: edge 2 3 -4 → no, sample 2:\n    ```\n    3 2\n    1 2 0\n    2 1 0\n    ```\n    So no edge involving 3 (vertex index 2). So no way to reach vertex 2.\n\nSo Floyd-Warshall: dist[0][2] = INF, dist[1][2] = INF, etc.\n\nSo in DP:\n- From mask 1 (0b001) → go to 1 → mask 3 (0b011) → then j=2 → cannot go\n- Similarly, from 2: mask 4 → but no way to go to 0 or 1\n- So full mask 7 is never reached → ans = INF → print \"No\"\n\nCorrect.\n\n### Sample 3:\nWe'll trust the code.\n\nBut let's run through quickly.\n\nAfter Floyd-Warshall, we build the shortest path matrix.\n\nThen run TSP-like DP → eventually get a value.\n\nExpected: -449429\n\nOur algorithm should handle that.\n\n---\n\nSo final code:\n\n(Note: In Floyd-Warshall, we must be careful with INF checks to avoid overflows.)\n\nWe are using `float('inf')`, which is fine for comparisons.\n\nLet's run.\n\nBut note: it's possible that the DP never reaches full mask → then \"No\", else print min.\n\nWe’ll now write the code accordingly.\n\nFinal code below.",
