    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_298",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi has a playlist with N songs.\nSong i (1 \\leq i \\leq N) lasts T_i seconds.\nTakahashi has started random play of the playlist at time 0.\nRandom play repeats the following: choose one song from the N songs with equal probability and play that song to the end.\nHere, songs are played continuously: once a song ends, the next chosen song starts immediately.\nThe same song can be chosen consecutively.\nFind the probability that song 1 is being played (X + 0.5) seconds after time 0, modulo 998244353.\n\nHow to print a probability modulo 998244353\nIt can be proved that the probability to be found in this problem is always a rational number.\nAlso, the constraints of this problem guarantee that when the probability to be found is expressed as an irreducible fraction \\frac{{y}}{{x}}, x is not divisible by 998244353.\nThen, there is a unique integer z between 0 and 998244352, inclusive, such that xz \\equiv y \\pmod{{998244353}}. Report this z.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN X\nT_1 T_2 \\ldots T_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the probability, modulo 998244353, that the first song in the playlist is being played (X+0.5) seconds after time 0.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N\\leq 10^3\n- 0 \\leq X\\leq 10^4\n- 1 \\leq T_i\\leq 10^4\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 6\n3 5 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n369720131\n\nSong 1 will be playing 6.5 seconds after time 0 if songs are played in one of the following orders.\n\n- Song 1 \\to Song 1 \\to Song 1\n- Song 2 \\to Song 1 \n- Song 3 \\to Song 1 \n\nThe probability that one of these occurs is \\frac{{7}}{{27}}.\nWe have 369720131\\times 27\\equiv 7 \\pmod{{998244353}}, so you should print 369720131.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5 0\n1 2 1 2 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n598946612\n\n0.5 seconds after time 0, the first song to be played is still playing, so the sought probability is \\frac{{1}}{{5}}.\nNote that different songs may have the same length.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n5 10000\n1 2 3 4 5\n\nSample Output 3\n\n586965467",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given a probabilistic process where a random song from a playlist of `N` songs is chosen uniformly each time, and played until it ends — this repeats indefinitely.\n\nWe are to compute the **probability that *song 1* is being played exactly at time `X + 0.5` seconds**, modulo `998244353`.\n\nThis means:\n- Time starts at 0.\n- At time `X + 0.5`, we want to know whether the currently playing song is **Song 1**.\n\n### Key Observations:\n\n1. Since the playlist chooses songs randomly (with replacement), and plays them immediately, the timeline is a sequence of songs.\n\n2. Each song has a deterministic duration.\n   - Song `i` has duration `T[i]`.\n\n3. We want the probability that at time `t = X + 0.5`, we are **in the middle of playing Song 1**.\n\n4. That means the current song that started at time `s` satisfies:\n   - `s ≤ X + 0.5 < s + T_1`, and the current song is Song 1.\n\n5. But `X` is an integer, so `X + 0.5` lies strictly in the middle of second `X` and second `X+1`.\n\n6. So any song that is playing at time `X + 0.5` must have started at some time `s ≤ X` and lasts until `s + T_i > X`.\n\nSo to rephrase: we want the sum over all sequences of songs that result in:\n- The total accumulated time before the current song is `s ≤ X`\n- The current song is song 1\n- `s + T_1 > X` → i.e., the song started at `s` is still playing at `X + 0.5`\n\nThis is a **dynamic programming** problem over time (`X ≤ 10^4`) with `N ≤ 1000`.\n\n---\n\n### Plan\n\nLet’s use DP.\n\nLet `dp[t]` = probability that time `t` is reached **exactly** (i.e., the total time of songs played so far sums to `t`), and now we’re about to pick the next song.\n\nWe start at time 0: `dp[0] = 1`.\n\nAt each time `t`, for each song `i`, we can choose song `i` with probability `1/N`, and it will occupy time `T_i`, so we add to `dp[t + T_i]`:\n\n```python\ndp[t + T_i] += dp[t] * inv(N)\n```\n\nHowever, we don’t just care about being at time `t`, we care about **what song is playing at time X+0.5**.\n\nSo instead of just `dp[t]` representing mass that arrives at time `t`, we can use it to **contribute** to the probability that **a particular song is playing across the interval [t, t+T_i)**.\n\nSo: when we play song `i` starting at time `t`, and its duration is `T_i`, then it is playing during `[t, t+T_i)`.\n\nWe want to know: does this interval include `X + 0.5`?\n\nThat happens if:\n```python\nt <= X < t + T_i\n```\nBecause:\n- `X + 0.5 < t + T_i` ⇨ `X < t + T_i`\n- `X + 0.5 >= t` ⇨ `t <= X`\n\nSo for each song 1, if it starts at time `t`, and `t <= X < t + T_1`, then it contributes to the probability.\n\nThe contribution is: `dp[t] * (1/N)` → because `dp[t]` is the probability that time `t` is reached, then we choose song 1 with probability `1/N`.\n\nSo the total answer is:\n```python\nans = sum_{t=0}^{X} [ if t + T_1 > X: dp[t] * inv(N) ]\n```\n\nBut note: we must not include states beyond `X`, since `t > X` → can't cover `X + 0.5`.\n\n---\n\n### Algorithm Steps\n\n1. Read input: `N`, `X`, and list `T` of durations.\n\n2. Let `mod = 998244353`.\n\n3. Precompute modular inverse of `N` modulo `998244353`.\n\n4. Initialize `dp[0] = 1`. `dp[t]` for `t = 0..X` (we don’t need beyond `X`, since any song starting after time `X` cannot affect time `X + 0.5`).\n\n5. For `t` from `0` to `X`:\n   - If `dp[t] > 0`, then for each song `i`:\n     - `next_time = t + T[i]`\n     - If `next_time <= X`, then we can propagate: add `dp[t] * invN` to `dp[next_time]`.\n\n     Why stop at `X`? Because to know whether a song starting at `s <= X` covers `X+0.5`, we need to consider all start times `<= X`. But to compute `dp[t]` for `t <= X`, we only need transitions from earlier times, and we only add `t + T[i]` if it doesn't exceed `X`. However, even if `t + T[i] > X`, we **cannot** propagate forward (for future propagation), but we **can** use the current `dp[t]` to contribute to the answer if the next song is song 1 and covers `X+0.5`.\n\nWait — **Important**: we don't need to propagate states beyond `X`, because they are not starting points for songs that can cover `X+0.5`. But when we are at time `t`, we can **always** choose a song (even if it ends after `X`), and that choice may contribute to the probability.\n\nThus, **we do not need to store `dp[t]` for `t > X`**, but **we must consider the choice of each song at every `t <= X`**, and if that song covers `X+0.5`, we record the contribution.\n\nSo modify:\n\n- We run `dp[t]` only for `t in [0, X]`.\n\n- For each `t` in `0..X`:\n   - For each song `i`:\n      - if `t + T[i] <= X`: then `dp[t + T[i]] += dp[t] * invN`\n      - **regardless**, if the song `i` is song 1 **and** `t <= X < t + T[0]` (since `T[0]` is duration of song 1), then we add `dp[t] * invN` to answer.\n\nWait — that's not correct: the probability of choosing a song is **always** `invN`, and we **must** account for all possible choices. But in the DP, we split the probability.\n\nBut we cannot both update future DP and also consume the probability for the answer — we **must** do one or the other? No: we are simulating all paths. The idea is:\n\nAt time `t`, we are about to pick a song. We simulate the choice:\n- For each song, we branch into a new state.\n- But if the chosen song runs past `X`, and we care about time `X + 0.5`, then we can **immediately account** for whether this song is song 1.\n\nSo we do:\n\n```python\nfor t in 0 to X:\n    for each song i:\n        if t + T[i] <= X:\n            dp[t + T[i]] += dp[t] * invN\n        else:\n            # This song ends after X, so it plays over time X+0.5\n            # So if i == 0 (song 1), then we add to answer\n            if i == 0:  # i.e., song 1\n                ans += dp[t] * invN\n```\n\nBut also: what if `t + T[i] > X` and i == 0, then this path leads to song 1 playing at X+0.5.\n\nThat’s the key.\n\nBut we must be careful: every time we “use” `dp[t]` to make a choice, we are splitting the probability among the `N` choices. In this approach:\n\n- For each time `t`, we are adding contributions to:\n   - future `dp` (if song ends by `X`)\n   - answer (if the chosen song is song 1 and lasts past `X`)\n- But what about choices where the chosen song is **not** song 1 and lasts past `X`? We don’t care — because we only care about whether **song 1** is playing.\n\nAnd that’s it. Because those other songs ending after `X` do not contribute to our desired event.\n\nBut note: we are not double-counting, and we are correctly splitting probability.\n\nHowever, we must realize that **each `dp[t]` is the probability of reaching time `t`** — i.e., the probability that the cumulative song durations have reached exactly `t`.\n\nAnd at each such time, we choose a song: probability `1/N` for each.\n\nSo the total probability mass is preserved **only if** we account for all choices. But in our DP update, we only propagate when `t + T[i] <= X`. For other transitions, we only record contributions to answer if song is 1.\n\nBut for songs that are not song 1 and end after `X`, we **don’t record** anything — which is fine. We just \"discard\" those paths because they don't contribute to the answer.\n\nSo the algorithm is:\n\n- Let `dp[0..X]` be a list of integers mod `998244353`, initially `dp[0] = 1`, others 0.\n- `ans = 0`\n- `invN = pow(N, mod-2, mod)`\n- For `t` in `0 .. X`:\n   - For `i` in `0 .. N-1`:\n      - Let `nt = t + T[i]`\n      - If `nt <= X`:\n          - `dp[nt] = (dp[nt] + dp[t] * invN) % mod`\n      - Else:  # nt > X\n          - If `i == 0`:  # song 1\n              - `ans = (ans + dp[t] * invN) % mod`\n\n**But** this is **not** entirely correct.\n\nWait: when we are at time `t`, and we choose a song, **we must not reuse the probability mass** in multiple places. In this loop, for each song `i`, we are branching the probability `dp[t] * invN` to either `dp[nt]` or to `ans` (if i==0).\n\nBut that’s correct: each song choice is a separate outcome.\n\nBut note: the `dp[t]` is not reduced — we are using it in multiple branches. This is fine because `dp[t]` is the incoming mass, and we are distributing it **fully** to the next states.\n\nActually, we are doing:\n\n```python\nfor each i:\n    contribution = dp[t] * invN\n    if nt <= X: add to dp[nt]\n    else if i == 0: add to ans\n```\n\nThis means we are not adding to `ans` for non-song-1 songs that run past `X`, which is correct.\n\nBut we are **not accounting for song 1 only when it starts at t and lasts beyond X**.\n\nBut what if song 1 has duration such that `t + T[0] <= X`? Then we just propagate to `dp[t + T[0]]`, and maybe later, when we start another song 1, we might still have a chance.\n\nYes — this is correct. Because if song 1 ends before or at `X`, it does not cover `X + 0.5`, so it doesn't contribute.\n\nSo only when song 1 is started at `t` and `t + T[0] > X`, then it covers `X + 0.5`.\n\nSo the above logic is correct.\n\nBut: the same `dp[t]` is used for all `i`. So we are distributing the probability `dp[t]` to `N` directions, each with `dp[t] * invN`.\n\nBut we are **not** removing `dp[t]` — it's purely incoming.\n\nYes — that’s how DP on time works.\n\nThis is similar to a \"infinite branching\" process, but we’re only tracking the probability that we land at each time `t <= X`.\n\nAnd all choices are independent.\n\n---\n\n### But wait: What if multiple songs are played?\n\nYes — this DP models: after some sequence of songs, we reach time `t`. Then we choose the next song.\n\nSo `dp[t]` = probability that the last song ended at `t` (or we start at 0).\n\nThen we pick the next song uniformly.\n\nThis matches the process.\n\n---\n\n### Example: N=3, X=6, T = [3,5,6]\n\nWe want sequences where song 1 is playing at 6.5.\n\nSuch as:\n- 1->1->1: times: 0->3->6->9 → at 6.5, 3rd song (1) is playing → started at 6, T=3 → 6 <= 6 < 6+3 → yes.\n  - Probability: (1/3)^3 = 1/27\n- 2->1: 0->5->8 → at 5, start song 1 → runs 5 to 8 → includes 6.5? yes.\n  - Probability: (1/3)^2 = 1/9 = 3/27\n- 3->1: 0->6->12 → starts song 1 at 6 → 6 <= 6 < 12 → yes.\n  - Probability: (1/3)^2 = 1/9 = 3/27\n- Also: could we have song 1 at start: 1->2? 0->3, then 3->8 → song 2. But does song 1 start again? We could have:\n  - 1->1: 0->3->6 → then at 6, choose song 1? Then it starts at 6, and we already counted that in 1->1->1.\n  - But what about: song 1 at 0: runs 0 to 3 → doesn't cover 6.5.\n  - So only the **current** song matters.\n\nWait: is there a path like:\n- 1->3: 0->3->9 → at 3, play song 3 → runs to 9 → but that's not song 1.\n- No: only the final song being 1 **and** overlapping `X+0.5` counts.\n\nBut what if we have four songs?\n\n- 1->1->1: we get to time 6, then play song 1 → covers 6.5 → correct.\n\nBut also:\n- Can we have more? Like 2 songs of length 3: 3+3=6 → start song 1 at 6 → same as above.\n\nBut what about starting song 1 at t=4? We can't reach t=4 — with only lengths 3,5,6.\n\nSo only:\n- t=0: play song 1? Then ends at 3 → doesn't reach 6.5.\n- t=3: can come from:\n   - 1->1: now at 3\n   - or is there another? No.\n   - from 3: play song 1 → ends at 6 → doesn’t cover 6.5? 6 <= 6 < 6+3 → 6 < 6? no → 6 < 6+3 is true, but we need `X < t + T[i]` → `6 < 6+3` → true. `t <= X` → 6 <= 6 → true. So `t=6` qualifies.\n\nAh! So **t=6** is allowed.\n\nBut `t=6`, `T[0]=3` → `t + T[0] = 9 > 6` → so yes, song 1 starting at 6 does cover 6.5.\n\nSo we need to consider all `t` from 0 to 6 inclusive.\n\nNow, let's simulate:\n\nInitialize: `dp[0] = 1`\n\nt=0:\n- Choose song 0 (T=3): `nt=3` <=6 → `dp[3] += 1 * inv3`\n- Choose song 1 (T=5): `nt=5` <=6 → `dp[5] += 1 * inv3`\n- Choose song 2 (T=6): `nt=6` <=6 → `dp[6] += 1 * inv3`\n- None end after 6 → no addition to ans\n\nNow: `dp[3] = inv3`, `dp[5] = inv3`, `dp[6] = inv3`\n\nt=3:\n- From dp[3] = inv3\n- song0: 3+3=6 <=6 → dp[6] += (inv3)*(inv3) = inv9\n- song1: 3+5=8 >6 → and i=1 ≠0 → no contribution\n- song2: 3+6=9 >6 → i=2 ≠0 → no contribution\n→ So only add to dp[6]\n\nNow `dp[6] = inv3 + inv9 = 4/9`\n\nt=4: dp[4]=0 → skip\n\nt=5:\n- dp[5] = inv3\n- song0: 5+3=8>6 → i=0 → so add to ans: += (inv3)*(inv3) = 1/9\n- song1: 5+5=10>6 → i≠0 → ignore\n- song2: 5+6=11>6 → i≠0 → ignore\n\nans += 1/9\n\nt=6:\n- dp[6] = 4/9\n- song0: 6+3=9>6 → i=0 → add to ans: += (4/9)*(1/3) = 4/27\n- others: i≠0 → ignore\n\nTotal ans = 1/9 + 4/27 = 3/27 + 4/27 = 7/27 → correct!\n\nSo the algorithm works.\n\nBut 1/9 is `invN^2`, and 4/9 * 1/3 = 4/27.\n\nWe must do modular arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Details\n\n- `mod = 998244353`\n- Use `pow(N, mod-2, mod)` for modular inverse.\n- Use an array `dp[0..X+1]` (size `X+1`)\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nEdge: X=0\n\nThen we start at t=0, and choose a song:\n- If song 1 has T[0] >=1 → then `0 + T[0] > 0` → so if we choose song 1, it contributes.\n- So probability = `1/N`\n\nSample Input 2: N=5, X=0, any T_i>=1 → answer = 1/5 → 1 * inv(5) mod 998244353\n\nLet's compute: `pow(5, 998244353-2, 998244353)` → we can compute.\n\nBut output is 598946612 → check: 5 * 598946612 % 998244353 = ?\n\nLet me check: 5 * 598946612 = 2994733060\n\nNow divide 2994733060 by 998244353:\n\n998244353 * 3 = 2994733059\n\n2994733060 - 2994733059 = 1 → so yes, 5 * 598946612 ≡ 1 mod 998244353 → so 598946612 ≡ 1/5 → correct.\n\nSo algorithm works.\n\nNow let’s code it.\n\n---\n\nWe’ll do:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nmod = 998244353\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    X = int(data[1])\n    T = list(map(int, data[2:2+N]))\n    \n    invN = pow(N, mod-2, mod)\n    \n    # dp[t] = probability (mod mod) of reaching time t\n    dp = [0] * (X+1)\n    dp[0] = 1\n    \n    ans = 0\n    \n    for t in range(X+1):\n        if dp[t] == 0:\n            continue\n        for i in range(N):\n            nt = t + T[i]\n            prob = (dp[t] * invN) % mod\n            if nt <= X:\n                dp[nt] = (dp[nt] + prob) % mod\n            else:\n                # This song will be playing at X+0.5 if it's song 1\n                if i == 0:\n                    ans = (ans + prob) % mod\n    \n    print(ans)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet’s test with sample 1:\n\nN=3, X=6, T=[3,5,6]\n\ninvN = 1/3 mod mod.\n\nWe can trust the math.\n\nRun the simulation:\n\ndp[0]=1\n\nt=0:\n- i=0: nt=3 -> <=6 -> dp[3] += 1 * inv3\n- i=1: nt=5 -> dp[5] += inv3\n- i=2: nt=6 -> dp[6] += inv3\n\nt=1: dp[1]=0 → skip\nt=2: skip\nt=3: dp[3]=inv3\n- i=0: nt=6 -> dp[6] += (inv3)*(inv3) = inv9\n- i=1: nt=8>6 → i!=0 → skip\n- i=2: nt=9>6 → skip\n\nNow dp[6] = inv3 + inv9 = (3*inv9 + inv9) = 4*inv9? But modulo.\n\ninv3 = pow(3, mod-2, mod)\nWe don't need exact number now.\n\nt=4: 0\nt=5: dp[5]=inv3\n- i=0: nt=8>6 → i==0 → ans += inv3 * inv3 = inv9\n\nt=6: dp[6] = (inv3 + inv9) = (3*inv9 + inv9) = 4*inv9\n- i=0: nt=9>6 → i==0 → ans += (4*inv9) * inv3 = 4 * inv27\n\nSo total ans = inv9 + 4*inv27 = 3*inv27 + 4*inv27 = 7*inv27\n\nWe need `7 * inv(27) mod mod`\n\ninv27 = pow(27, mod-2, mod)\n\nThen 7 * inv27 mod mod → that should be 369720131.\n\nYes, sample output.\n\nSo code is correct.\n\nLet’s run the sample inputs.\n\nWe trust the logic.\n\nFinal code:\n\nNote: use integers mod.\n\nLet’s write it.\n\n---\n\n**Important**: We must do all operations mod `998244353`.\n\nWe do.\n\nNow output.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nmod = 998244353\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    N = int(data[0])\n    X = int(data[1])\n    T = list(map(int, data[2:2+N]))\n    \n    invN = pow(N, mod-2, mod)\n    \n    dp = [0] * (X + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1\n    \n    ans = 0\n    \n    for t in range(X + 1):\n        if dp[t] == 0:\n            continue\n        for i in range(N):\n            nxt_time = t + T[i]\n            contribution = (dp[t] * invN) % mod\n            if nxt_time <= X:\n                dp[nxt_time] = (dp[nxt_time] + contribution) % mod\n            else:\n                if i == 0:  # song 1\n                    ans = (ans + contribution) % mod\n                    \n    print(ans)\n\nmain()\n```",
