merged leaves.\n\nBut we know:\n- There are exactly M centers\n- sum of their degrees = N - M\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Read N, build tree, compute degree for each vertex.\n2. Count `L = number of vertices with degree 1`\n3. Compute `M = (N - L + 2) // 3` (must be integer)\n4. We need to select M vertices as centers such that:\n   - their degrees sum to `N - M`\n   - and they are possibly among vertices with degree ≥2\n   - but prefer: vertices with degree ≥3 must be centers.\n\nBut can a degree-2 vertex be a non-center (i.e., a merged leaf)? Yes.\n\n- Such a vertex was originally a leaf, merged once.\n\nCan a degree-2 vertex be a center? Yes — if it was center of a level-2 star.\n\nBut we can distinguish?\n\nWait: We know:\n- The centers are the only ones whose degree reflects their level.\n- The sum of the degrees of the M centers must be `N - M`.\n\nAnd vertices with degree ≥3 must be centers.\n\nLet:\n- Let `candidates` = all vertices with degree ≥2\n- Let `forced_centers` = all vertices with degree ≥3\n- Let `F = len(forced_centers)`\n- Then we need to pick `M - F` additional centers from the degree-2 vertices.\n\nLet `S_forced = sum of degrees of forced centers`\n\nThen the additional sum needed from degree-2 centers: `(N - M) - S_forced`\n\nSince each degree-2 center contributes 2, we need:\n- `2 * (M - F)` additional sum\n\nSo check if:\n- `(N - M) - S_forced == 2 * (M - F)`\n\nThen it works.\n\nBut we don't know if all degree-2 vertices are eligible.\n\nAlternative:\n\nWe don't need to explicitly pick — because the **sum of all vertex degrees** is `2(N-1)`\n\nLet:\n- `sum_centers = N - M`\n\nLet T = sum of degrees of all vertices = `2N - 2`\n\nSum of degrees of non-centers (the leaves in the original stars) = `T - (N - M) = 2N - 2 - N + M = N + M - 2`\n\nNon-centers are `N - M` vertices (since there are M centers)\n\nAnd non-centers are the original leaves, each of which:\n- originally had degree 1 (within star)\n- possibly increased by 1 if used in a merge\n\