→ output: \"2 2 2\" → matches.\n\nBut wait — are vertices 3,4,5,6,7,8,9 all degree 2? Then L=2?\n\nBut vertices 1 and 2 have deg 1, yes.\n\nBut are there only two degree-1 vertices? Yes.\n\nSo M=3.\n\nAnswer: three level-2 stars → works.\n\nBut is each center degree 2? Yes.\n\nBut in the degree list, there are 7 vertices with deg 2.\n\nWe need to pick 3 of them as centers.\n\nBut we don’t care — their degree is 2.\n\nSo levels: three 2s.\n\n---\n\n### Sample Input 3:\n\n20 vertices.\n\nWe are not going to draw, but let's compute degrees.\n\nWe trust:\n\n- Count degree of each vertex from list\n\nInput:\n```\n20\n8 3\n8 18\n2 19\n8 20\n9 17\n19 7\n8 7\n14 12\n2 15\n14 10\n2 13\n2 16\n2 1\n9 5\n10 15\n14 6\n2 4\n2 11\n5 12\n```\n\nNow count degree for each vertex from 1 to 20.\n\nLet’s go:\n\n- 1: [2] → deg 1\n- 2: [19,15,13,16,1,4,11] → 7 edges → deg 7\n- 3: [8] → deg 1? Wait, 8-3 and then nothing else? Wait, line: \"8 3\" and \"7 3\" from \"7 3\"? No, \"7 3\" not there. Wait:\n\nWait list:\n\n1. 8 3\n2. 8 18\n3. 2 19\n4. 8 20\n5. 9 17\n6. 19 7\n7. 8 7\n8. 14 12\n9. 2 15\n10. 14 10\n11. 2 13\n12. 2 16\n13. 2 1\n14. 9 5\n15. 10 15\n16. 14 6\n17. 2 4\n18. 2 11\n19. 5 12\n\nWait — missing one? Should be 19 edges.\n\nList has 19 lines after N=20.\n\nNow:\n\n- 1: [2] → deg 1\n- 2: [19,15,13,16,1,4,11] → edges to: 19,15,13,16,1,4,11 → 7 edges\n- 3: [8] → deg 1\n- 4: [2] → deg 1\n- 5: [9,12] → wait: 9 5 and 5 12 → two\n- 6: [14] → deg 1\n- 7: [19,8] → 19 7 and 8 7 → deg 2\n- 8: [3,18,20,7] → and earlier? 8-3,8-18,8-20,8-7 → deg 4\n- 9: [17,5] → deg 2\n- 10: [14,15] → deg 2\n- 11: [2] → deg 1\n- 12: [14,5] → wait: 14 12 and 5 12? Yes → deg 2\n- 13: [2] → deg 1\n- 14: [12,10,6] → and? 14-12,14-10,14-6 → deg 3\n- 15: [2,10] → deg 2\n- 16: [2] → deg 1\n- 17: [9] → deg 1\n- 18: [8] → deg 1\n- 19: [2,7] → deg 2\n- 20: [8] → deg 1\n\nNow list degrees:\n\n- deg 1: vertices [1,3,4,6,11,13,16,17,18,20] → 10 vertices\n- deg 2: [5,7,9,10,12,15,19] → 7 vertices\n- deg 3: [14]\n- deg 4: 