      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the longest substring of `word` that doesn't contain any string from `forbidden` as a substring.\n\nMy approach:\n1. Use a sliding window technique with two pointers\n2. Expand the right pointer and check if adding the new character creates any forbidden substring\n3. If a forbidden substring is found, move the left pointer past the point where the forbidden substring starts\n4. Keep track of the maximum length found\n\nThe key insight is that when we find a forbidden substring ending at position `right`, we need to move the left pointer to `right - len(forbidden_str) + 1 + 1` (one position after the start of the forbidden substring).\n\n```python\ndef longestValidSubstring(word, forbidden):\n    forbidden_set = set(forbidden)\n    n = len(word)\n    max_length = 0\n    left = 0\n    \n    for right in range(n):\n        # Check all possible substrings ending at 'right' that could be forbidden\n        for forbidden_str in forbidden:\n            len_forbidden = len(forbidden_str)\n            # Check if this forbidden string ends at position 'right'\n            if (right + 1 >= len_forbidden and \n                word[right - len_forbidden + 1:right + 1] == forbidden_str and\n                right - len_forbidden + 1 >= left):\n                # Move left pointer to just after the start of the forbidden substring\n                left = right - len_forbidden + 2\n                break\n        \n        max_length = max(max_length, right - left + 1)\n    \n    return max_length\n```",
