t we don't know the detailed train schedule, we only know f(B).\n            # Actually, f(B) is the latest starting time from B to reach N.\n            # But if we arrive at B at time T, then we can only use trains that depart at time >= T.\n            # And the latest such departure that is part of a successful journey is f(B) (if f(B) >= T).\n            # So if T <= f(B), then we can connect.\n            # But if f(B) < T, then no train from B departs after f(B), so we cannot leave.\n            # But we might have a train that departs at time <= f(B) and >= T? \n            # But f(B) is the latest departure time that eventually reaches N, so any departure up to f(B) is allowed.\n            # So if T <= f(B), then we can connect.\n            # Therefore, we need t+c <= f(B)  => t <= f(B) - c.\n            \n            max_t = fB - c\n            if max_t < l:\n                # No valid departure time\n                candidate = -10**30\n            else:\n                # The departure times: t_j = l + j * d, j=0,1,...,k-1\n                # We want the largest t_j <= max_t\n                # j_max = min(k-1, floor((max_t - l) / d))\n                j_max = (max_t - l) // d\n                if j_max < 0:\n                    candidate = -10**30\n                else:\n                    j_max = min(j_max, k-1)\n                    t_candidate = l + j_max * d\n                    candidate = t_candidate\n            \n            if candidate > res[A]:\n                res[A] = candidate\n                heapq.heappush(pq, (-res[A], A))\n    \n    # Output f(1), f(2), ..., f(N-1)\n    for i in range(1, N):\n        if res[i] < 0:  # meaning unreachable (our initial -10**30 or below)\n            print(\"Unreachable\")\n        else:\n            print(res[i])\n\nsolve()\n```",
