= 2`, then `t = 2.5`, but no — wait: 2.5 is < 3, so we can't print again until after `2`? No: after `t`, we need to wait **1 microsecond**, so next print ≥ `t + 1`. So if we print at `2`, next must be ≥ `3`.\n\nSo we’re trying to pick a **set of (product, print_time)** pairs such that:\n\n- `print_time ∈ [T_i, T_i + D_i]`\n- The chosen `print_time`s are **at least 1 apart**: if we print at `t`, next one must be at `≥ t + 1`\n\nWe want to **maximize the number** of such prints.\n\nSo we can reduce this to: select as many jobs as possible such that we can assign print times (one per selected job) in their intervals, and all times are at least 1 apart.\n\nThis is equivalent to **maximum throughput scheduling** where processing time is zero but with cooldown between operations.\n\n---\n\n### Optimal Strategy (Greedy Solution)\n\nWe can solve this using a well-known greedy idea:\n\n**Step 1**: Sort products by `(T_i + D_i)` → the **leave time** (i.e. deadline = `T_i + D_i`), ascending.\n\nWhy? Because we want to prioritize products with earlier end times (Earliest Deadline First), a common greedy strategy in scheduling.\n\n**Step 2**: Traverse the products in order of increasing deadline, and maintain the **last print time**.\n\nFor each product, try to print **as early as possible**, but **after the last print time + 1**, and **within the product's interval**.\n\nThat is, for product `i`:\n\n- The earliest possible print time is `max(T_i, last_print_time + 1)`\n- If this is ≤ `T_i + D_i`, then we can print this product\n- Then set `last_print_time = max(T_i, last_print_time + 1)`\n\nThis greedy strategy ensures we use as little \"time space\" as possible for each product, leaving more room for others.\n\nWhy sort by deadline?\n\nBecause if a product leaves early, we have to act fast. It's a standard trick: process shorter intervals or earlier deadlines first in scheduling.\n\n---\n\n### Why is this optimal?\n\nThis is similar to the classic problem: \"maximum number of 