    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef constructProductMatrix(grid):\n    n = len(grid)\n    m = len(grid[0])\n    MOD = 12345\n    \n    # Calculate total product of all elements modulo MOD\n    total_product = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(m):\n            total_product = (total_product * grid[i][j]) % MOD\n    \n    # Create result matrix\n    p = [[0] * m for _ in range(n)]\n    \n    # For each position, calculate product of all elements except the current one\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(m):\n            if grid[i][j] % MOD == 0:\n                # If current element is divisible by MOD, we need to recalculate\n                # the product without this element\n                product_without_current = 1\n                for row in range(n):\n                    for col in range(m):\n                        if row != i or col != j:\n                            product_without_current = (product_without_current * grid[row][col]) % MOD\n                p[i][j] = product_without_current\n            else:\n                # Use modular multiplicative inverse\n                # (total_product / grid[i][j]) % MOD = (total_product * mod_inverse(grid[i][j], MOD)) % MOD\n                current_val_mod = grid[i][j] % MOD\n                if current_val_mod == 0:\n                    p[i][j] = 0\n                else:\n                    # Calculate modular inverse using Fermat's little theorem\n                    # Since MOD is prime, inverse of a is a^(MOD-2) mod MOD\n                    inv = pow(current_val_mod, MOD - 2, MOD)\n                    p[i][j] = (total_product * inv) % MOD\n    \n    return p\n```",
