is small.\n\nBut **10 is small** — the only sum we care about is **whether 10 is achievable**.\n\nWe can use **DP where state is the set of subset sums ≤ 10 that are achievable**. The number of achievable sums (integers from 0 to 10) is at most 11, so we could represent this with bit masks (11 bits), but 2^11 = 2048 states.\n\nWait — actually, the number of possible subset sum states (which sums ≤ 10 can be formed) is at most $ 2^{11} $, but we can manage that.\n\nBut better: **we only care about whether sum 10 is achievable**.\n\nSo we want:\n$$\n\\Pr[\\exists S \\subseteq [N] \\text{ s.t. } \\sum_{i \\in S} x_i = 10]\n$$\n\nWe can use **inclusion-exclusion** or **complementary probability**:\n$$\n\\Pr[\\text{some subset sums to 10}] = 1 - \\Pr[\\text{no subset of dice sums to 10}]\n$$\n\nBut “no subset sums to 10” is difficult due to dependencies.\n\nInstead, we do **DP over the set of achievable sums ≤ 10**.\n\nLet’s maintain a distribution over the **set of currently achievable subset sums (≤ 10)**.\n\nBut the number of subsets of {0,1,...,10} is $ 2^{11} = 2048 $, which is acceptable.\n\nBut: the state space is 2048, and $ N \\leq 100 $, and each die has up to $ A_i $ values.\n\nWe can do:\n\n- State: a bitmask of achievable sums in {0,1,...,10}. We only care about sums ≤ 10.\n- Start with sum 0 achievable → state has bit 0 set.\n- For each die $ i $, we roll a number $ v \\in [1, A_i] $ uniformly.\n  Then, we may **choose** to include it or not.\n\nBut **we can always choose not to include a die**, but if we include it, it adds its value to existing subset sums.\n\nBut the decision of whether to include or not is **ours**, so we can form new subset sums: for each current achievable sum $ s $, and if $ s + v \\leq 10 $, then $ s + v $ becomes achievable.\n\nSo after each die, the new set of achievable sums is:\n$$\nS_{\\text{new}} = S_{\\text{old}} \\cup \\{ s + v \\mid s \\in S_{\\text{old}}, s + v \\leq 10 \\}\n$$\n\nBut $ v $ is random.\n\nWe are to compute the **probability** that at the end, $ 10 \\in S $.\n\nWe can use **DP over achievable subset sets** (bit masks of achievable sums).\n\nLet $ dp[\\text{mask}] $ be the probability that the current set of achievable sums (≤ 10) is exactly **contained in the closure** represented by mask.\n\nBut: the transition is: for each die, for each value $ v \\in [1, A_i] $, with probability $ 1/A_i $, and then we merge the reachable subsets.\n\nWe maintain $ dp[\\text{mask}] $: the probability that the current set of achievable subset sums is represented by the bitmask `mask`.\n\nEach bit from 0 to 10 is represented.\n\nInitial: $ dp[1 << 0] = 1 $, since sum 0 is always achievable (empty subset).\n\nFor each die $ i $:\n  Create new DP array $ ndp $\n  For each current mask, with probability $ dp[mask] $\n     For each possible roll $ v \\in [1, A_i] $:\n         - compute new mask after adding this die with value $ v $\n         - new_mask = old_mask OR (shifted old_mask by v, clipped to ≤ 10)\n         - add $ dp[mask] / A_i $ to $ ndp[new\\_mask] $\n\nHowever, $ A_i $ can be up to $ 10^6 $, so iterating over $ v = 1 $ to $ A_i $ for each die and each mask is too slow ($ 2048 \\times 10^6 \\times 100 $ >> $ 2 \\times 10^{11} $).\n\nWe need to optimize the inner loop.\n\n---\n\n### Optimization:\n\nFor fixed die $ i $ with max face $ A_i $, and for a fixed current mask, we want to compute:\nFor how many $ v \\in [1, A_i] $, the value $ v $ leads to a particular new_mask?\n\nBut new_mask depends on:\n$$\n\\text{new\\_mask} = \\text{mask} \\cup \\left( \\{ s + v \\mid s \\in \\text{mask}, s + v \\leq 10 \\} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet’s define:\n- $ T_v = \\{ s + v \\mid s \\in \\text{mask}, s + v \\leq 10 \\} $\n- Then $ \\text{new\\_mask} = \\text{mask} \\mid (\\text{bit representation of } T_v) $\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{new\\_mask} = \\text{mask} \\mid (\\text{mask} \\ll v) \\text{ and then masked with } \\text{0x7FF} \\text{ (bits 0 to 10)}\n$$\n\nBut `mask << v` may shift bits beyond 10.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{added} = ((\\text{mask} \\ll v) \\& \\text{full\\_mask}) \\quad \\text{where } \\text{full\\_mask} = (1 << 11) - 1\n$$\nThen:\n$$\n\\text{new\\_mask} = \\text{mask} \\mid \\text{added}\n$$\n\nSo for a fixed mask, we want to compute the total number of values $ v \\in [1, A_i] $ such that this `new_mask` is a particular value.\n\nBut note: **many values of $ v $** will result in the **same shift effect**.\n\nBut observe: the effect of $ v $ depends on which $ s \\in \\text{mask} $ satisfy $ s + v \\leq 10 $, i.e., $ v \\leq 10 - s $.\n\nSo for each subset of mask elements that are “active” (i.e., $ v \\leq 10 - s $), the added set depends on $ v $.\n\nBut since $ \\text{mask} $ has at most 11 bits, we can partition the range $ v \\in [1, A_i] $ into intervals where the set $ \\{ s \\in \\text{mask} \\mid s + v \\leq 10 \\} $ is constant.\n\nThe critical points are $ v = 1, 2, \\ldots, 10 - s $ for each $ s \\in \\text{mask} $, so critical points are integers $ 1 $ to $ 10 $.\n\nSo we can break $ v \\in [1, A_i] $ into intervals bounded by 1 to 11 (since $ v > 10 $, then $ s + v > 10 $ for all $ s \\geq 1 $, but $ s = 0 $, then $ v \\leq 10 $ only).\n\nSpecifically, for $ v > 10 $, only $ s = 0 $ could contribute, and then $ s + v = v $, which is $ > 10 $ if $ v > 10 $. So:\n\n- If $ v > 10 $, then $ s + v > 10 $ for all $ s \\geq 1 $ and $ s + v = v > 10 $ for $ s = 0 $, so **no new sum is added** via inclusion.\n\n⇒ For $ v > 10 $, adding the die does **not** help achieve any new sum ≤ 10.\n\nSo for $ v > 10 $, the new mask = old mask.\n\nSo we can split:\n- Case 1: $ v > 10 $: doesn't add any new sum ≤ 10.\n- Case 2: $ v \\in [1, 10] $: may add some new sums.\n\nSo for fixed mask, the number of $ v \\in [1, A_i] $ such that:\n- $ v > 10 $: contributes `max(0, A_i - 10)` values, all give same new_mask = mask\n- For $ v = 1 $ to $ \\min(10, A_i) $: only 10 values to loop\n\nSo we can do:\n\nFor each die $ i $, and for each current mask:\n   count