\nBut we update dp[k+1] from dp[k], so we must iterate k from high to low to prevent using a just-updated dp[k].\n\nWait: no, the recurrence uses the old dp[k] to update dp[k+1], so if we go from high k to low k, we use the same old values.\n\nBut we are reading dp[k] and writing dp[k+1]. They are different indices.\n\nSo even if we go forward, it's safe? \n\nBut consider:\n   we have dp[k] and update dp[k+1]\n   then later we use dp[k+1] to update dp[k+2]\n\nBut that would be using the new dp[k+1] which includes the current p, which is wrong.\n\nBut our recurrence is: use current p and old dp[k] (before any update in this contest) to update dp[k+1].\n\nSo if we go forward from k=0 to N-1, and we update dp[1] using dp[0], then later use updated dp[1] to update dp[2], that would be incorrect, because it might include the current p.\n\nExample: k=0: use dp[0] to set dp[1] = p*1 + dp[0]\nThen k=1: use dp[1] (which now includes p) to set dp[2] = p*0.9 + dp[1] → this would be like taking p twice.\n\nSo we must iterate k from high to low to avoid this.\n\nTherefore: loop k from N-1 down to 0.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nWe’ll use:\n\n   for p in P_rev:\n       for k in range(N-1, -1, -1):\n           if dp[k] != -10**18:\n               new_val = p * (0.9 ** k) + dp[k]\n               if new_val > dp[k+1]:\n                   dp[k+1] = new_val\n\nBut 0.9**k might be expensive? We can precompute powers? But k up to 5000, and N=5000, so total operations about O(N^2) = 25e6, and 25e6 exponentiations? No, 0.9**k: we can precompute power[0..N]\n\nBut k is the length, so we can precompute:\n\n   pow09 = [1.0] * (N+1)\n   for i in range(1, N+1):\n       pow09[i] = pow09[i-1] * 0.9\n\nThen use pow09[k] instead of 0.9**k.\n\nSimilarly, total_weight = 10 * (1 - pow09[k])\n\nLet's do it.\n\nBut for k=0, 0.9**0=1\n\nCode:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    N = int(data[0])\n    P = list(map(int, data[1:1+N]))\n    \n    # Precompute powers of 0.9 up to exponent N\n    pow09 = [1.0] * (N+1)\n    for i in range(1, N+1):\n        pow09[i] = pow09[i-1] * 0.9\n    \n    # dp[k] = best numerator (sum of weighted performances) for a sequence of length k\n    # Initialize with -inf, except dp[0]=0\n    NEG_INF = -10**18\n    dp = [NEG_INF] * (N+1)\n    dp[0] = 0.0\n    \n    # Process in reverse order (from last contest to first)\n    for p in reversed(P):\n        # Update dp from high k to low k to avoid overwriting used values\n        for k in range(N-1, -1, -1):\n            if dp[k] != NEG_INF:\n                new_val = p * pow09[k] + dp[k]\n                if new_val > dp[k+1]:\n                    dp[k+1] = new_val\n    \n    best_rating = -10**18\n    for k in range(1, N+1):\n        if dp[k] != NEG_INF:\n            total_weight = 10.0 * (1.0 - pow09[k])  # = sum_{i=0}^{k-1} 0.9^i\n            rating = dp[k] / total_weight - 1200.0 / (k ** 0.5)\n            if rating > best_rating:\n                best_rating = rating\n    \n    print(best_rating)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet's run with the samples.\n\nSample 1: should be ~256.735\nSample 2: ~261.423\nSample 3: -1100.0\n\nWe output exactly as computed.\n\nNote: the problem says output any floating point number with sufficient precision.\n\nPrint as is — Python will handle.\n\nWe'll call the function.\n\nEdge: N=1\n\nLet me try: P=[100]\ndp[0]=0\nprocess p=100:\n   k=0: new_val = 100*1 +0 =100 → dp[1]=100\nk=1: total_weight = 10*(1-0.9)=1\nrating=100/1 -1200/1 = -1100\n\nCorrect.\n\nWe are good.\n\nFinal code:\n\n\nNote: We are printing the float directly. Python may default to fewer digits, but the problem accepts absolute or relative error <= 1e-6.\n\nTo be safe, we can format to more digits, but `print` on float already uses smart formatting.\n\nAlternatively, use formatting to ensure precision.\n\nBut the sample output has 15 digits.\n\nWe can use:\n\n   print(\"{:.15f}\".format(best_rating))\n\nBut note: if the number is integer, it might print 123.000000000000000\n\nBut that's fine.\n\nLet's do that.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    P = list(map(int, data[1:1+N]))\n    \n    if N == 0:\n        print(0.0)\n        return\n        \n    # Precompute powers of 0.9: pow09[i] = 0.9 ** i\n    pow09 = [1.0] * (N+1)\n    for i in range(1, N+1):\n        pow09[i] = pow09[i-1] * 0.9\n    \n    NEG_INF = -10**18\n    dp = [NEG_INF] * (N+1)\n    dp[0] = 0.0\n    \n    # Process contests in reverse chronological order\n    for p in reversed(P):\n        # Update dp backwards to prevent overwriting values we still need\n        for k in range(N-1, -1, -1):\n            if dp[k] != NEG_INF:\n                new_val = p * pow09[k] + dp[k]\n                if new_val > dp[k+1]:\n                    dp[k+1] = new_val\n    \n    best_rating = -10**18\n    for k in range(1, N+1):\n        if dp[k] != NEG_INF:\n            denominator_sum = 10.0 * (1.0 - pow09[k])  # geometric series sum\n            rating = dp[k] / denominator_sum - 1200.0 / (k ** 0.5)\n            if rating > best_rating:\n                best_rating = rating\n                \n    print(\"{:.15f}\".format(best_rating))\n\nmain()\n```",
