      "generated_answer": "To solve this, we need to:\n\n1. Iterate over all possible **contiguous subarrays** of the input array `nums`.\n2. For each subarray:\n   - Sort it to get `sarr`.\n   - Compute its **imbalance number** by counting how many consecutive elements in the sorted subarray differ by more than 1.\n3. Sum up the imbalance numbers of all subarrays.\n\nWe can do this directly with nested loops due to the constraint `nums.length <= 1000`, and the number of subarrays is about $ O(n^2) $, which is acceptable.\n\nHere is the **function body** that computes the required result:\n\n```python\ndef sumOfImbalanceNumbers(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    total_imbalance = 0\n    \n    # Iterate over all subarrays\n    for i in range(n):\n        subarray = []\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            subarray.append(nums[j])\n            if len(subarray) < 2:\n                continue\n            sorted_sub = sorted(subarray)\n            imbalance = 0\n            for k in range(len(sorted_sub) - 1):\n                if sorted_sub[k + 1] - sorted_sub[k] > 1:\n                    imbalance += 1\n            total_imbalance += imbalance\n            \n    return total_imbalance\n```\n\nThis function correctly calculates the sum of imbalance numbers across all subarrays. It avoids using any external libraries and runs within acceptable complexity for the given constraints.",
