      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    B = int(input().strip())\n    \n    # We need to find A such that A^A = B\n    # Since A^A grows very quickly, we only need to check small values of A\n    # For A > 1, A^A grows rapidly. For example, 60^60 is astronomically large\n    # So we only need to check A from 1 to a reasonable upper bound\n    \n    # The maximum possible A would be when A^A <= 10^18\n    # We can check values of A up to around 60, but realistically much smaller\n    for A in range(1, 61):  # 60 is a safe upper bound\n        power = A ** A\n        if power == B:\n            print(A)\n            return\n        elif power > B:\n            # Since A^A is strictly increasing for A >= 1, we can stop\n            break\n    \n    print(-1)\n\nsolve()\n```",
