    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_269",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a race through checkpoints 1,2,\\dots,N in this order on a coordinate plane.\nThe coordinates of checkpoint i are (X_i,Y_i), and all checkpoints have different coordinates.\nCheckpoints other than checkpoints 1 and N can be skipped.\nHowever, let C be the number of checkpoints skipped, and the following penalty will be imposed:\n\n- \\displaystyle 2^{{C−1}} if C>0, and\n- 0 if C=0.\n\nLet s be the total distance traveled (Euclidean distance) from checkpoint 1 to checkpoint N plus the penalty.\nFind the minimum achievable value as s.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nX_1 Y_1\nX_2 Y_2\n\\vdots\nX_N Y_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer. Your output is considered correct if the absolute or relative error from the true value is at most 10^{{-5}}.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- All input values are integers.\n- 2 \\le N \\le 10^4\n- 0 \\le X_i,Y_i \\le 10^4\n- (X_i,Y_i) \\neq (X_j,Y_j) if i \\neq j.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6\n0 0\n1 1\n2 0\n0 1\n1 0\n2 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n5.82842712474619009753\n\nConsider passing through checkpoints 1,2,5,6 and skip checkpoints 3,4.\n\n- Move from checkpoint 1 to 2. The distance between them is \\sqrt{{2}}.\n- Move from checkpoint 2 to 5. The distance between them is 1.\n- Move from checkpoint 5 to 6. The distance between them is \\sqrt{{2}}.\n- Two checkpoints are skipped, so the penalty of 2 is imposed.\n\nIn this way, you can achieve s = 3 + 2\\sqrt{{2}} \\approx 5.828427.\nYou cannot make s smaller than this value.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n10\n1 8\n3 7\n9 4\n4 9\n6 1\n7 5\n0 0\n1 3\n6 8\n6 4\n\nSample Output 2\n\n24.63441361516795872523\n\nSample Input 3\n\n10\n34 24\n47 60\n30 31\n12 97\n87 93\n64 46\n82 50\n14 7\n17 24\n3 78\n\nSample Output 3\n\n110.61238353245736230207",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given a race through checkpoints on a coordinate plane, starting at checkpoint 1 and ending at checkpoint N. We can skip some intermediate checkpoints (but must include the first and last). Each time we skip `C` checkpoints, we incur a penalty of:\n\n- $ 0 $ if $ C = 0 $\n- $ 2^{C-1} $ if $ C > 0 $\n\nOur goal is to minimize:\n$$\ns = \\text{total Euclidean distance traveled} + \\text{penalty}\n$$\n\nWe must find the **minimum achievable** value of $ s $, considering all valid subsequences from checkpoint 1 to N (with increasing indices), where we are allowed to skip any number of intermediate checkpoints.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations:\n\n- We are allowed to skip **any** subset of checkpoints from 2 to N−1.\n- But the **penalty depends on total number of skipped checkpoints**, not on how we skip.\n- That is, if we skip `C` checkpoints in total, the penalty is:\n  $$\n  P(C) = \n  \\begin{cases}\n  0 & C = 0 \\\\\n  2^{C-1} & C > 0\n  \\end{cases}\n  $$\n\nSo if from N total checkpoints, we visit K checkpoints (including first and last), then:\n- $ C = N - K $ checkpoints are skipped\n- Penalty = $ 2^{N-K-1} $ if $ N - K > 0 $, else 0\n\nThe total cost is:\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{K-1} \\text{dist}(p_{k_i}, p_{k_{i+1}}) + \\begin{cases} 0 & \\text{if } K = N \\\\ 2^{N-K-1} & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWe need to **minimize this over all valid subsequences** starting at 1 and ending at N.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy\n\nThis is a **dynamic programming (DP)** problem:\n\nLet’s define:\n- `dp[i]` = minimum total cost (travel distance + penalty from future skips) **to reach checkpoint i**, assuming that all decisions up to `i` are made, but **we haven’t yet finalized the total number of skips**.\n\nBut the problem is that **penalty is global** — it depends on how many checkpoints we skip in total, not locally.\n\nSo we cannot assign the penalty incrementally unless we know total skips.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nWe **iterate over possible numbers of visited checkpoints (K)** from 2 to N (minimum 2: must include 1 and N), and for each, solve a DP where we:\n- Choose a subsequence from 1 to N with exactly K checkpoints\n- Compute total distance\n- Add penalty $ 2^{N-K-1} $ if $ N-K > 0 $, else 0\n- Track the minimum over all K and all such subsequences\n\nBut $ N \\le 10^4 $, so K can go up to 10^4, and iterating over all possible K and running a DP for each would be too slow.\n\nAlternative Insight:\n\nActually, the **penalty depends only on the total number of skipped checkpoints**. So instead of fixing K, we can do this:\n\nLet’s try **DP where state is the index**, and we keep track of the cost so far **excluding** the penalty, but track how many checkpoints we’ve visited so far (or skipped), but that still makes state space large.\n\nWait — penalty is only applied **once**, at the end, based on **total skips**.\n\nHence, we **cannot** split the penalty into transitions.\n\nSo we need to consider the following:\n\nWe can run a DP where:\n- `dp[i]` = minimum total **travel distance** to reach checkpoint i, for all possible **numbers of visited checkpoints** (or equivalently, total skipped up to i)\n\nBut even then, the future skips from i to N also matter.\n\nAlternatively: we know that the total number of visited checkpoints determines the penalty.\n\nSo we can do:\n\nFor each possible number of visited checkpoints $ K $ from 2 to N:\n- Find minimum total travel distance along any path from 1 to N that visits exactly $ K $ checkpoints (in order)\n- Add penalty $ P = \\begin{cases}0 & K = N \\\\ 2^{N-K-1} & K < N\\end{cases} $\n- Track the minimum over all K\n\nBut this would require us to do $ O(N) $ passes, each doing $ O(N^2) $, leading to $ O(N^3) $, which is too slow for $ N=10^4 $.\n\nWe need a better way.\n\nWait — but note: $ 2^{C-1} $ grows **exponentially** with $ C $. So skipping too many checkpoints results in large penalties.\n\nFor example, skipping 20 checkpoints gives penalty $ 2^{19} \\approx 500,000 $, but the maximum possible total distance is at most $ N \\times \\sqrt{2} \\times 10^4 \\approx 10^4 \\times 1.4 \\times 10^4 = 140,000 $. So if you skip more than ~18 checkpoints, the penalty alone exceeds potential gains.\n\nHence, **we can cap the number of skips** — only small numbers of total skips are useful.\n\nBut wait: we are **minimizing**, and penalty grows fast, so it might be better to skip only a few.\n\nBut also: sometimes skipping a very long detour might justify skipping a few checkpoints and paying $ 2^{C-1} $.\n\nHowever, note that skipping 28 checkpoints gives $ 2^{27} \\approx 134M $, which is huge.\n\nSo in practice, **only small values of total skips** (say $ C \\le 20 $) are worth considering.\n\nBut even $ C=20 $ means visiting only $ N - 20 $ checkpoints. For $ N=10^4 $, that’s still too many.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nWe try **DP over indices**, but **keep track of how many we have visited so far**. But again, for N=10^4, that’s too heavy.\n\nWait — actually, the exponential penalty grows **so fast** that optimal number of skips is **small**.\n\nTherefore, we can do this:\n\nLet’s suppose that **in the optimal path, the number of skipped checkpoints is at most about 20** — because $ 2^{20} $ is over a million, and total distance is at most a few hundred thousand, so skipping more than 20 is never beneficial.\n\nIn fact, with $ N \\le 10^4 $, and max coordinate 10^4, the max edge is $ \\sqrt{2}\\times10^4 \\approx 14142 $. Total straight path is at most $ (N-1)\\times14142 \\approx 141e6 $? Wait no — that’s worst-case, but we want to minimize.\n\nBut in practice, the total distance if visiting all points is $ O(N \\times 10^4) $, say up to $ 10^8 $ in extreme, but actually coordinates are up to $ 10^4 $, so two points differ by at most 10000 in x and y, so max distance between two points is $ \\sqrt{2} \\times 10^4 \\approx 14142 $.\n\nSo the total distance if visiting all N points is at most $ (N-1) \\times 14142 \\le 10^4 \\times 14142 \\approx 141e6 $? Wait no: we are going through a sequence — each consecutive pair is at most 14142, so total at most $ 10^4 \\times 14142 = 141,420,000 $. But that’s way too high.\n\nBut in real scenarios, distances are much smaller. And penalty for skipping 30 checkpoints is $ 2^{29} \\approx 536e6 $ — more than worst-case travel cost.\n\nHence, **skipping more than, say, 30 checkpoints is suboptimal** — because penalty > potential max savings.\n\nBut actually: the **minimum** travel cost by visiting all checkpoints is already fixed. If we skip checkpoints, we may reduce travel cost, but add penalty.\n\nThe **benefit** of skipping is: reduction in travel cost minus the penalty.\n\nSo we only want to skip if the savings > penalty.\n\nBut penalty is $ 2^{C-1} $. So for C=20, penalty ≈ 500,000.\n\nSo unless skipping 20 checkpoints saves more than 500,000 in distance, it’s not worth it.\n\nBut the typical savings per skip is maybe a few hundred? So unless the path is extremely convoluted, skipping many is not worth it.\n\nSo in practice, **optimal number of skipped checkpoints is small**.\n\nHence, we can try this:\n\nLet $ K $ be the number of visited checkpoints. Then $ K = N - C $. So $ K \\geq N - C_{\\max} $. If we assume $ C_{\\max} = 20 $, then we only need to consider paths with at least $ K = N - 20 $ checkpoints.\n\nBut that’s still **huge**, since we’d need to select $ N - 20 $ from $ N $, which is too many.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nTry **DP where state is index i**, and **dp[i] is the minimum total cost to reach checkpoint i**, with the following twist:\n\nBut we don't know total skips yet.\n\nBut here's a better idea:\n\nLet’s assume that the number of **skipped checkpoints is small**, so we can try **all possible subsets of skipped checkpoints of small size**? But number of such subsets is $ \\binom{N-2}{C} $, which for $ C=10 $ is too large.\n\nWe need an efficient DP.\n\n---\n\n### Insight: We can use DP over indices, and approximate that penalty depends on number of skips, but we can structure it as:\n\nLet’s define:\n- `dp[i]` = minimum **total cost** (distance traveled so far + penalty that will eventually be paid) when ending at checkpoint i.\n\nBut penalty depends on **how many were skipped overall**, and we haven't finished.\n\nSo we can't compute penalty until the end.\n\nSo we need to **track the number of visited checkpoints** in the path.\n\nLet’s define:\n- `dp[i][k]` = minimum total travel distance to reach checkpoint i, having visited exactly `k` checkpoints so far (including i)\n\nThen, after filling the DP table, for each `k` from 2 to N:\n- total skips = `C = N - k`\n- penalty = `0 if C == 0 else 2**(C-1)`\n- total cost = `dp[N][k] + penalty`\n- minimize over k\n\nNow, what is the state size?\n- i from 1 to N: 10^4\n- k from 1 to N: 10^4\n- total states: 10^8, each requiring a loop over previous indices: O(N) → total O(N^3) = 10^12 → too slow.\n\nWe need to optimize.\n\nBut note: because of exponential penalty, **only paths with large k (few skips) are promising**.\n\nSpecifically, if total skips C ≥ 30, penalty ≥ 2^29 ≈ 536e6, which is huge.\n\nBut the total travel cost is at most (N-1) * 14142 ≈ 141e6, so it’s impossible to save more than 141e6.\n\nHence, if the all-points path cost is 141e6, then even if skipping everything else saved all of it, skipping 30 would cost 536e6 > savings.\n\nSo we only need to consider C ≤ about 20 ⇒ k ≥ N - 20.\n\nBut if N is large (e.g., 10000), then k from 9980 to 10000: still about 20 values of k.\n\nBut for each state `dp[i][k]`, we need to try all j < i such that we came from j, with `k-1` visited.\n\nSo total work per i: O(i * number_of_k_values) → over all i: roughly O(N^2 * 20) = 20 * (10^4)^2 = 2e9 → might be borderline in Pyton, but maybe acceptable in C++ but not in Python.\n\nBut constraints say N ≤ 10^4, and we need a better way.\n\nBut wait: maybe we can try a different approach.\n\nSince **only paths that skip a small number of checkpoints are viable**, we can try to do DP over indices, but keep only the best values for each (i, k), with k being the number of visited checkpoints, but **limit k to be ≥ max(2, N - MAX_SKIP)**, and MAX_SKIP = 20 or 25.\n\nBut still, k would be from N - MAX_SKIP to N — only MAX_SKIP + 1 possible values.\n\nSo let’s do:\n\nLet MAX_SKIP = 20  (since 2^19 = 524288, and 2^20 = 1e6, which may still be acceptable? But depends on gains)\n\nBut in sample 3, answer is ~110, so if we skip 10 checkpoints, penalty = 2^9 = 512, which is way bigger than total cost — impossible.\n\nWait! In sample 1: answer ≈ 5.828, penalty = 2 (since skipped 2 checkpoints), 2^{2-1} = 2^1 = 2 → yes.\n\nSample 2: answer ~24.6, penalty can't be more than that, so 2^{C-1} < 25 → C-1 ≤ 4 → C ≤ 5.\n\nSimilarly, sample 3: ~110.6, so 2^{C-1} < 110 → C-1 ≤ 6 → C ≤ 7.\n\nSo in practice, **optimal number of skips is small**, say C ≤ 10.\n\nHence, we can do:\n\nWe will consider only paths that skip at most MAX_SKIP = 20 checkpoints.\n\nSo the number of visited checkpoints k must be at least K_min = max(2, N - MAX_SKIP)\n\nWe’ll run a DP such that:\n- `dp[i][v]` = minimum total travel distance to reach checkpoint i, having visited exactly v checkpoints in total (including i), where v ranges from 1 to N, but we’ll only store v >= K_min, i.e., v ≥ N - MAX_SKIP\n\nBut note: even if total final skips is small, intermediate paths might have few visited so far.\n\nBut we can restrict: only allow states where `v ≥ current_index - MAX_SKIP_remaining`, but it's complex.\n\nInstead, we can simply do:\n\nLet MAX_C = 20  # maximum total skips we consider\nThen minimum visited checkpoints: K_min = max(2, N - MAX_C)\n\nBut we don’t know final v until the end.\n\nSo let’s instead let `dp[i][c]` = minimum travel distance to reach checkpoint i, having skipped exactly `c` checkpoints **so far**.\n\n\"so far\": from checkpoint 1 to i, how many between 1 and i (exclusive of 1, exclusive of i?) were skipped?\n\nActually: from 1 to i, the number of checkpoints **in the full list** is i, and we are at i.\n\nSuppose we have visited v checkpoints (including 1 and i), then skips so far = (i - 1) - (v - 1) = i - v? Wait:\n\nFrom index 1 to i: there are i checkpoints.\n\nIf we visit v of them (including 1 and i), then we skipped i - v checkpoints in [1, i].\n\nBut the total skips in the whole path will be at most `i - v` + (N - i) = N - v\n\nBut we will eventually skip up to N - v, so penalty = 0 if v = N, else 2^{N-v-1}\n\nBut we want to minimize over v.\n\nSo define:\n\n`dp[i][v]` = minimum travel distance to reach point i, having visited exactly `v` checkpoints (in the entire path so far, which are all in [1..i])\n\nBut v is the number of checkpoints visited in the path ending at i.\n\nWe only care about v such that total skips C = N - v ≤ MAX_C ⇒ v ≥ N - MAX_C\n\nSo we only store states where `v >= max(1, N - MAX_C)`\n\nBut at early i, v cannot be that large — so for small i, it's impossible.\n\nSo we need to allow all v from 1 to i, but only keep v such that `v >= N - MAX_C`? No — because for i < N - MAX_C, N - MAX_C might be larger than i, so no state possible.\n\nActually, if we require final v >= N - MAX_C, then along the way, v can be from 1 up to i.\n\nBut the total number of v values we store per i is at most MAX_C+1 (since v ≥ N - MAX_C), but for i < N - MAX_C, we cannot satisfy that — so we must allow intermediate v < N - MAX_C.\n\nBut we can prune: if at position i, the minimum possible additional skips from i to N is (N - i) (if we visit all), so minimum total skips = current_skips + 0 = (i - v) → but total skips = N - v (final)\n\nSo if N - v > MAX_C, then prune.\n\nSo only keep states where `N - v <= MAX_C` → `v >= N - MAX_C`\n\nSo per state `dp[i][v]`, we require `v >= N - MAX_C`\n\nBut for i < N - MAX_C, it's impossible to have v >= N - MAX_C → so no states? But then we cannot build path.\n\nBut wait: if N=100, MAX_C=20, then v >= 80.\n\nBut at i=20, we can have at most v=20 (visiting every point), but 20 < 80 → impossible.\n\nSo we cannot enforce `v >= N - MAX_C` for all i; we can only enforce it at the end.\n\nBut then intermediate v can be small.\n\nBut total state: v ranges from 1 to i, i from 1 to N → O(N^2), which is 10^8 for N=10^4 → too much.\n\nBut MAX_C = 20 → total skips C ≤ 20 → so final v ≥ N - 20.\n\nBut at any point i, the number of visited checkpoints v must satisfy:\n- v ≥ 1\n- and, to be extendable to a path with total visited ≥ N - 20, we need that the maximum additional checkpoints we can visit from i+1 to N is (N - i), so maximum final v = v_current + (N - i) ≥ N - 20\n  → v_current ≥ N - 20 - (N - i) = i - 20\n\nSo at index i, we must have v_current ≥ i - 20\n\nSimilarly, v_current ≤ i\n\nSo for each i, v ranges from max(1, i - 20) to min(i, N) — only about 20 states per i.\n\nYes!\n\nBecause if at position i, we have visited v checkpoints, then to eventually reach v_final ≥ N - 20, and we can visit at most (N - i) more checkpoints, we need:\n- v + (N - i) ≥ N - 20  ⇒ v ≥ i - 20\n\nSo we only keep states at i with v ≥ i - MAX_C, where MAX_C=20.\n\nSimilarly, v ≤ i\n\nSo number of v values per i is at most 21.\n\nState: `dp[i][v]` = minimum total distance to reach checkpoint i with exactly v visited checkpoints.\n\nWe can use a list of dictionaries or a 2D array where second dimension is small.\n\nBut we can do:\n\n- `dp[i] = a list of values for each v in [i - MAX_C, i]`, but store only possible v.\n\nAlternatively, use a dictionary per i: `dp[i][v] = min_dist`\n\nBut for speed, we can use a list of fixed size.\n\nLet MAX_SKIP = 20\n\nFor i from 1 to N:\n  for v from max(1, i - MAX_SKIP) to min(i, N):\n      `dp[i][v] = min over j from max(1, (v-1) - MAX_SKIP ???) to i-1 such that j >= (v-1) - MAX_SKIP`\n\nWait, better: iterate for each possible previous checkpoint j < i, and for each possible state `dp[j][v_prev]` such that:\n- v_prev = v - 1 (because we visit i)\n- j has v_prev visited, and j < i\n- and v_prev >= j - MAX_SKIP  (automatically satisfied if stored)\n- and v_prev <= j\n- and v_prev = v - 1\n- and v must be >= i - MAX_SKIP (but we enforce that when storing)\n\nAlgorithm:\n\nLet MAX_SKIP = 20\n\nWe’ll maintain for each i, a dictionary or list: `dp[i]` mapping `v` -> min distance to reach i with v visited.\n\nInitialize:\n  `dp[1][1] = 0.0`   (at checkpoint 1, visited 1 checkpoint, cost 0)\n\nFor i from 2 to N:\n  for j from 1 to i-1:\n      if j not in [1, i-1] with dp[j] not empty: continue\n      for each (v_prev, dist_prev) in dp[j].items():\n          v_new = v_prev + 1\n          if v_new < i - MAX_SKIP:  # cannot reach final v_final >= N-MAX_SKIP from here\n              continue  # or skip\n          # But we are only concerned about path feasibility to end\n          # we require v_new >= i - MAX_SKIP\n          if v_new < i - MAX_SKIP:\n              continue\n          # Also, v_new cannot exceed i\n          # compute distance from j to i\n          d = dist(checkpoint[j], checkpoint[i])\n          new_dist = dist_prev + d\n          # update dp[i][v_new] = min( existing, new_dist )\n\n  Also, we can go directly from 1 to i? Covered in j loop.\n\nBut we must include all j < i.\n\nAfter processing all i, at i = N:\n  for each v_final in dp[N]:\n      total_skips = N - v_final\n      if total_skips == 0:\n          penalty = 0\n      else:\n          penalty = 2**(total_skips - 1)\n      total_cost = dp[N][v_final] + penalty\n      best = min(best, total_cost)\n\nBut we must also consider that we might have not stored some paths, but due to the pruning, if a path has total_skips > MAX_SKIP, we skip it.\n\nSo we should also compute the cost of not skipping any: i.e., the total distance of the full path, cost_full, and compare.\n\nBut if we set MAX_SKIP = 20, we cover all total_skips <= 20.\n\nAnd for total_skips > 20, penalty >= 2^20 = 1e6, which is huge, and unless the full path is longer than 1e6, it's not better.\n\nBut max total distance: worst-case, N=10000, each step ~14142, total ~141e6 — and 2^27 = 134e6, so in theory, skipping 28 checkpoints could be beneficial if we save nearly 134e6 in distance, but that's unrealistic — you’d have to skip a huge detour.\n\nBut to be safe, we can compute the full path cost and use it as initial best answer.\n\nThen run the DP for paths with at most MAX_SKIP skips.\n\nWe can set MAX_SKIP = 25 or 30.\n\nBut for N=10000, the inner loop:\n- i from 2 to N: ~10000\n- for each i, j from 1 to i-1: ~i\n- for each j, number of states in dp[j]: at most MAX_SKIP+1 = 26\n- so total operations: sum_{i=2}^N (i * 26) = 26 * sum_{i=2}^N (i) = 26 * O(N^2) = 26 * 50e6 = 1.3e9 for N=10000.\n\nThis is **too slow in Python**.\n\nWe need to optimize.\n\nWe can reverse: for fixed i, we want to compute:\n  for each j < i:\n      for each state in dp[j] with v_prev such that v_new = v_prev+1 >= i - MAX_SKIP ⇒ v_prev >= i - MAX_SKIP - 1\n\nBut we can precompute for each j, store dp[j] as a list indexed by offset.\n\nBut the real problem is O(N^2) time.\n\nBut N=10^4 ⇒ N^2 = 100e6, times 20 = 2e9 operations — too slow in Python.\n\nWe need a better approach.\n\n### Alternative Insight: \nSince we only care about paths that skip at most MAX_SKIP checkpoints, then the number of skipped checkpoints between consecutive visited checkpoints is limited? Not really — you can skip many in one jump.\n\nBut the total number of visited checkpoints is at least N - MAX_SKIP.\n\nBut the path consists of a subsequence of length K >= N - MAX_SKIP.\n\nSo we are looking for a subsequence of the points of length K in [N - MAX_SKIP, N], starting at 1, ending at N.\n\nNumber of such subsequences: sum_{K=N-MAX_SKIP}^{N} \\binom{N-2}{K-2} — but for K close to N, this is small? No — for K = N - 10, \\binom{N-2}{N-12} = \\binom{N-2}{10} which is huge for N=10^4.\n\nSo we must use dynamic programming.\n\nBut can we reduce the number of states?\n\nNote: we can do:\n\nLet MAX_SKIP = 20\nWe use a DP where `dp[i]` = list of pairs `(v, cost)` for states at i with v visited and cost = travel distance, but only keep states where `v >= i - MAX_SKIP`\n\nFor i=1: v=1 >= 1 - 20 = -19 → valid. So `dp[1] = {1: 0.0}`\n\nFor i=2:\n  j=1: v_prev=1, v_new=2, 2 >= 2-20 = -18 → valid\n  distance = dist(1,2)\n  dp[2] = {2: dist}\n\nFor i=3:\n  from j=1: v_prev=1, v_new=2, require 2 >= 3-20=-17 → valid\n  from j=2: v_prev=2, v_new=3, 3 >= -17 → valid\n  so update from both\n\n...\n\nThe number of states per i is at most MAX_SKIP+1, so about 21.\n\nFor each i, we iterate over j from 1 to i-1, and for each j, we consider at most 21 states.\n\nSo inner loop: for j in 1..i-1: constant work per j (because only 21 states)\n\nTotal work: sum_{i=2}^N (i-1) = O(N^2)\n\nN=10^4 → N^2 = 100e6, which is **acceptable in C++ but borderline in Python**.\n\nBut 100e6 iterations in Python might take 10 seconds, and we have 21 * 100e6 = 2.1e9 state checks? No.\n\nWait:\n- For each i (10^4 values)\n- For each j from 1 to i-1 (about i-1 values)\n- For each state in dp[j] (at most 21)\n\nSo total operations = sum_{i=2}^N sum_{j=1}^{i-1} |dp[j]| ≤ sum_{i=2}^N sum_{j=1}^{i-1} 21 = 21 * sum_{i=2}^N (i-1) = 21 * (N-1)*N/2 ≈ 21 * 50e6 = 1.05e9\n\nThis is **too slow in Python**\n\nWe need to optimize the transitions.\n\n### Optimization: \nFor a fixed i, we want to compute for each possible v_prev in a narrow band, the minimum cost over all j < i such that:\n- j has a state with visited count = v_prev\n- and then at i, we would have v_new = v_prev + 1\n- and we require v_new >= i - MAX_SKIP → v_prev >= i - MAX_SKIP - 1\n\nBut note: the cost to reach i with v_new is:\n```\ncost = min_{j < i} { dp[j][v_prev] + dist(j, i) }\n```\nfor fixed v_prev, then v_new = v_prev + 1.\n\nSo we can precompute, for each possible `v` (number of visited), the best cost to reach any j with that v, and maybe use spatial indexing? But dist is Euclidean.\n\nAlternatively, we can maintain for each `v` (global v), a list of points (x_j, y_j, cost), and for new point i, compute min over j with `v_prev` of cost + dist(j,i).\n\nBut still O(N) per i.\n\nBut total number of (j,v) states is only about N * 21 = 210000, so we can store:\n\nLet’s maintain a 2D array:\n- `best[j][v]` is not possible, j up to 10000.\n\nInstead, for each v_count from 1 to MAX_V (but v_count can be up to N), but we only have v_count >= j - MAX_SKIP, and we only care about v_count up to N.\n\nBut the total number of states is sum over i of number of states at i ≈ N * (MAX_SKIP+1) = 10^4 * 21 = 210000\n\nWe can store all active states globally.\n\nBut then for each i, we need to consider all previous states.\n\nTotal work: for each i, we look at up to 21*(i-1) states, so total about 21 * N^2 / 2 = 1.05e9 as above.\n\nIn Pyton, 1e9 operations might take 10-100 seconds, not acceptable.\n\nWe need to set MAX_SKIP smaller?\n\nBut in sample, skipping 2 or more is used.\n\nOr accept and hope that for smaller N it works, but N=10^4 is worst-case.\n\nAnother idea: since the number of visited checkpoints is high (at least N - 20), then the path visits most checkpoints. The decisions are only whether to skip a few.\n\nSo the visited sequence is almost the full sequence.\n\nThen we can try: start with the full path cost, and consider skipping a few checkpoints.\n\nBut not necessarily contiguous.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a different approach: state = last and second last checkpoint, and the number of visited checkpoints, but then we need to know v.\n\nBut still.\n\nGiven time, and that 1.05e9 might be borderline in C++/PyPy but not in Python, we might need a smarter method.\n\nBut let's try to reduce MAX_SKIP.\n\nFrom the samples, the number of skipped checkpoints is small (2 in sample 1).\n\nIn sample 2 and 3, we don't know, but the penalty is at most the cost, which is 24 and 110, so penalty < 110, so 2^{C-1} < 110 → C-1 <= 6 → C<=7.\n\nSo MAX_SKIP = 20 is safe, but we can set MAX_SKIP = 20 and hope that N is small in practice? But constraints say N=10^4.\n\nBut 1.05e9 iterations might be acceptable in Pyton in online judges if implemented in PyPy or C++.\n\nBut we must do in Python.\n\nAlternative: use a different state: let’s let state be `i` and `s = i - v`, the number of skips **before and up to i**.\n\nThen v = i - s\nThen the total final skips = N - v_final = N - (v_path)\nAt the end, v_final = v_path, so skip = N - v_path\n\nBut during the path, at index i, if we have taken v checkpoints, then s = i - v = number of skipped checkpoints in [1..i]\n\nThen total final skips = s + (N - i - (number of future skips))\n\nNo, future we can skip more.\n\nBut the total final skips = s + (number of skipped from i+1 to N)\n\nAnd we haven't decided.\n\nBut we want to minimize travel distance now, and add penalty later.\n\nBut we can still use: state `dp[i][s]`, where s = number of checkpoint skipped in [1..i] (including up to i), but s can be from 0 to i-1, and we only care about s <= MAX_SKIP (20)\n\nThen at i, s in [0, min(MAX_SKIP, i-1)]\n\nNumber of s per i: at most 21\n\nThen `dp[i][s]` = minimum distance to reach i with s skips in [1..i]\n\nHow to transition?\nTo reach i with s skips, then among the first i checkpoints, we skipped s, so visited v = i - s\n\nThen the previous visited checkpoint j < i, and between j and i, we skipped (i - j - 1) checkpoints, which is i - j - 1.\n\nLet s_prev = number of skips in [1..j] = at state j with s_prev\n\nThen total skips in [1..i] = s_prev + (i - j - 1) = s\n→ s_prev = s - (i - j - 1)\n\nSo s_prev = s - (i - j - 1) = s - i + j + 1\n\ns_prev must be >=0 and <= j-1 and <= MAX_SKIP\n\nSo for fixed i and s, j must be such that:\n- j < i\n- s_prev = s - i + j + 1 >= 0\n- s_prev <= min(MAX_SKIP, j-1)\n- s_prev must be in dp[j]\n\nThen try this j.\n\n for i in 2..N:\n   for s in 0..min(MAX_SKIP, i-1):\n      dp[i][s] = inf\n      for j in 1..i-1:\n         s_prev = s - (i - j - 1)  = s - i + j + 1\n         if s_prev < 0 or s_prev > min(MAX_SKIP, j-1): continue\n         if s_prev not in dp[j]: continue\n         dist_ji = dist(j,i)\n         new_cost = dp[j][s_prev] + dist_ji\n         update dp[i][s] = min(dp[i][s], new_cost)\n\n then after, at i=N:\n   for s in 0..min(MAX_SKIP, N-1):\n      total_skips = s + (N - j - 1) for j=i? \n      no.\n\n actually, at i=N, s = number of skips in [1..N] = C\n\n so total skips = s\n\n penalty = 0 if s=0 else 2^(s-1)\n\n travel dist = dp[N][s]\n\n cost = dist + penalty\n\n and also consider paths with s > MAX_SKIP? then cost might be large.\n\n so we can initialize best = cost of full path (s=0)\n\n and update with s=1..MAX_SKIP\n\n state count: per i, s in 0..min(MAX_SKIP, i-1) -> at most 21 states\n\n for each i, we do i-1 * 21 work? no, for fixed i and s, we do i-1 work.\n\n total work: sum_{i=2}^N sum_{s=0}^{min(MAX_SKIP,i-1)} (i-1)\n           <= sum_{i=2}^N (MAX_SKIP+1) * (i-1)\n           = (MAX_SKIP+1) * sum_{i=2}^N (i-1)\n           = (MAX_SKIP+1) * N*(N-1)/2\n           = 21 * 50e6 = 1.05e9  -> same as before.\n\n still 1.05e9.\n\n So both methods have the same complexity.\n\n We must implement and hope that it passes in Pyton for the given constraints, or use Pypy.\n\n But perhaps the constant factor is better because inner loop is not accessing a dict.\n\n Let's implement the second method using a 2D list.\n\n Let MAX_SKIP = 20\n\n dp[i][s] for i in range(1, N+1), s in range(0, MAX_SKIP+1)\n\n But s cannot exceed i-1.\n\n Initialize with inf.\n\n dp[1][0] = 0.0   (no skips in [1..1], one point)\n\n for i in range(2, N+1):\n   for s in range(0, min(MAX_SKIP+1, i)):  # s from 0 to min(MAX_SKIP, i-1)\n      for j in range(1, i):\n         # number of skips between j and i: positions j+1 to i-1: count = i - j - 1\n         # skips in [1..i] = skips in [1..j] + (i - j - 1)\n         # so s = s_prev + ( i - j - 1)\n         s_prev = s - (i - j - 1)\n         if s_prev < 0:\n             continue\n         if s_prev > MAX_SKIP:\n             continue\n         # s_prev must be valid at j: only if s_prev <= j-1, and we have dp[j][s_prev] not inf\n         if s_prev >= j:  # because at j, max skips is j-1\n             continue\n         if we had dp[j][s_prev] is Inf, skip\n         else:\n            d = dist(checkpoint[j], checkpoint[i])\n            new_cost = dp[j][s_prev] + d\n            if new_cost < dp[i][s]:\n                dp[i][s] = new_cost\n\n also, the full path cost is known: let full_cost = sum_{i=1}^{N-1} dist(i,i+1)\n best_answer = full_cost  ( penalty=0)\n\n for s in range(0, min(MAX_SKIP+1, N)):\n   if s == 0:\n      penalty = 0\n   else:\n      penalty = 2**(s-1)\n   total_cost = dp[N][s] + penalty\n   if total_cost < best_answer:\n      best_answer = total_cost\n\n print with high precision.\n\n But wait: can we have s > MAX_SKIP? yes, but we set MAX_SKIP=20, and for s>20, penalty>=2^19=524288, so if best_answer < 524288, we are safe.\n\n In samples, best_answer is < 111, so any path with s>=20 has penalty>=2^19=524288, which is worse.\n\n so we can safely ignore s>20.\n\n But in some input, full_path_cost might be large, but then we have best_answer = full_path_cost, and any path with high penalty will be more.\n\n so it's safe.\n\n Let's code accordingly.\n\n Coordinates are given as integers.\n\n We'll use math.isqrt or just **0.5\n\n Let INF = float('inf')\n\n But might use a large number.\n\n Implementation:\n\n Steps:\n\n 1. Read N\n 2. Read N lines of (x,y)\n 3. Precompute full_path cost: sum of dist(i,i+1) for i=1 to N-1\n 4. Set MAX_SKIP = 20\n 5. dp[i][s] for i in range(N), for s in range(MAX_SKIP+1)\n    We can use a 2D list: dp = 2D array of size [N][MAX_SKIP+1], initialize with inf\n\n    Let dp[i][s] = minimum distance to reach checkpoint i (0-indexed) with exactly s skipped in [0..i] (in the first i+1 checkpoints)\n\n    But easier to 1-index the checkpoints.\n\n    So let points[1..N]\n\n    dp[i][s] for i=1 to N, s=0 to MAX_SKIP\n\n 6. But in code, use 0-indexed list.\n\n    Let:\n        points = []\n        for i in range(N):\n            x,y = map(int, input().split())\n            points.append((x,y))\n\n        full_cost = 0.0\n        for i in range(N-1):\n            dx = points[i+1][0] - points[i][0]\n            dy = points[i+1][1] - points[i][1]\n            full_cost += (dx*dx + dy*dy)**0.5\n\n        MAX_SKIP_VAL = 20\n        # dp[i][s] for i in range(N), s in range(MAX_SKIP_VAL+1)\n        dp = [[10**18] * (MAX_SKIP_VAL+1) for _ in range(N)]\n\n        # base: at i=0, no skip in [0..0] -> s=0\n        dp[0][0] = 0.0\n\n        for i in range(1, N):  # i from 1 to N-1 (0-indexed)\n            for s in range(0, min(MAX_SKIP_VAL+1, i+1)):  # s in [0, min(20, i)] because at index i, there are i+1 points, max skips i\n                for j in range(0, i):  # j from 0 to i-1\n                    # number of checkpoints between j and i (exclusive) = i - j - 1\n                    num_skip_between = i - j - 1\n                    s_prev = s - num_skip_between\n                    if s_prev < 0 or s_prev > MAX_SKIP_VAL:\n                        continue\n                    # at j, the number of skips in [0..j] must be s_prev, and s_prev must be <= j (because at j, there are j+1 points, so max skip=j)\n                    if s_prev > j:  # wait: at index j, the earliest point is 0, so number of points = j+1, so max skip = j\n                        continue\n                    if dp[j][s_prev] >= 10**18:\n                        continue\n                    # compute distance from j to i\n                    x1,y1 = points[j]\n                    x2,y2 = points[i]\n                    dx = x2 - x1\n                    dy = y2 - y1\n                    d = (dx*dx + dy*dy)**0.5\n                    new_cost = dp[j][s_prev] + d\n                    if new_cost < dp[i][s]:\n                        dp[i][s] = new_cost\n\n        best_answer = full_cost  # full path has no penalty\n\n        # now at i = N-1 (last point)\n        i = N-1\n        for s in range(0, min(MAX_SKIP_VAL+1, N)):\