ing is invalid if at least one.\n\n   so you can't use that.\n\nGiven the complexity, and since this is a known type, I look for a solution in a similar style.\n\n In fact, a solution is to use the complement: \n   total = 52^(q)\n   answer = total - (number of assignments that contain the subsequence)\n\n but inclusion-exclusion over subsequences is hard.\n\n Given the time, I'll implement the DP with states representing the number of ways to have a partial subsequence, even if overcounts, and then the completion event is: when three > 0 and we add an uppercase, the number of ways to for it is three * 1 ( for that choice), but then these are the number of (string, subsequence) pairs that are completed.\n\n but that doesn't give the number of strings.\n\n This is a well-known problem in string and automata.\n\nAfter research, a correct method is to use a and of DP with states that are the maximal progress for any letter, and since the final state is only whether you have a and then an uppercase, you can use:\n\n We maintain:\n   dp = a tuple of ( for each c, the state of c: 0, 1, or 2 ( for have seen first c, or have seen second c), and a flag for if you have seen a lowercase after state 2 for any c.\n\n but state space is 3^26 * 2, too big.\n\nAnother idea from a similar code: \n\n  instead, you can use: the only thing that matters for future invalidation is whether you have the triple c-c-lowercase.\n\n  so let's maintain:\n     no_up_to = [0]*26, [0]*26, and a flag.\n\n but the only thing we need is: after we have any triple (c-c and a lowercase letter after), then the next uppercase we see will cause one ( at least) bad subsequences.\n\n so to for at the end, the number of strings that at some point after a triple see an uppercase.\n\n so you can use: \n\n   dp[i][0] = number of ways that have not seen c-c-l for any c\n   dp[i][1] = number of ways that have seen c-c-l but have not seen any uppercase after\n   dp[i][2] = number of ways that have been invalidated\