      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimizeStringValue(s):\n    # Count existing characters and positions of '?'\n    char_count = [0] * 26\n    question_positions = []\n    \n    for i, char in enumerate(s):\n        if char == '?':\n            question_positions.append(i)\n        else:\n            char_count[ord(char) - ord('a')] += 1\n    \n    # Number of '?' to replace\n    num_questions = len(question_positions)\n    \n    # Determine optimal characters to use for '?'\n    # We want to minimize the total value, so we should prefer characters that appear less frequently\n    # Also need to consider lexicographical ordering as tiebreaker\n    \n    result = list(s)\n    chars_to_use = []\n    \n    # Greedily select characters - we want to use the lexicographically smallest characters\n    # but also balance frequency to minimize cost\n    for _ in range(num_questions):\n        min_cost_char = 0\n        min_cost = float('inf')\n        \n        # Try each character from 'a' to 'z'\n        for c in range(26):\n            # Calculate the additional cost if we use this character\n            cost = char_count[c]\n            if cost < min_cost or (cost == min_cost and c < min_cost_char):\n                min_cost = cost\n                min_cost_char = c\n        \n        # Use the character with minimum cost (and lexicographically smallest in case of ties)\n        chars_to_use.append(min_cost_char)\n        char_count[min_cost_char] += 1\n    \n    # Sort the characters to use in lexicographical order to ensure lexicographically smallest result\n    # But we need to place them in the original positions of '?'\n    chars_to_use.sort()\n    \n    # Place the selected characters in the positions of '?'\n    for i, pos in enumerate(question_positions):\n        result[pos] = chr(ord('a') + chars_to_use[i])\n    \n    return ''.join(result)\n```",
