    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef find_tak_codes():\n    import sys\n    \n    # Read input\n    first_line = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    N, M = map(int, first_line.split())\n    \n    grid = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        grid.append(sys.stdin.readline().strip())\n    \n    # Define what a valid Tak Code should look like\n    def is_valid_tak_code(top_row, left_col):\n        # Check if 9x9 region fits in the grid\n        if top_row + 8 >= N or left_col + 8 >= M:\n            return False\n        \n        # Check top-left 3x3 region (must be all black)\n        for i in range(3):\n            for j in range(3):\n                if grid[top_row + i][left_col + j] != '#':\n                    return False\n        \n        # Check bottom-right 3x3 region (must be all black)\n        for i in range(3):\n            for j in range(3):\n                if grid[top_row + 6 + i][left_col + 6 + j] != '#':\n                    return False\n        \n        # Check the 14 adjacent cells around top-left 3x3 that should be white\n        # These are: right of the 3x3 (3 cells), below the 3x3 (3 cells), and diagonally below-right (1 cell)\n        # Actually, per description: all cells adjacent (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally) to the 3x3 regions\n        # For top-left 3x3: check the surrounding cells in a 5x5 area excluding the 3x3 itself\n        \n        # Adjacent cells to top-left 3x3 region\n        for i in range(-1, 4):  # -1 to 3 to cover adjacent\n            for j in range(-1, 4):\n                # Skip the 3x3 region itself (i=0,1,2 and j=0,1,2)\n                if 0 <= i <= 2 and 0 <= j <= 2:\n                    continue\n                # Check if this adjacent position is within the 9x9 region\n                if 0 <= top_row + i < top_row + 9 and 0 <= left_col + j < left_col + 9:\n                    # But we need to ensure this cell is in the Tak Code 9x9\n                    if grid[top_row + i][left_col + j] != '.':\n                        return False\n        \n        # Adjacent cells to bottom-right 3x3 region\n        br_start_row = top_row + 6\n        br_start_col = left_col + 6\n        for i in range(-1, 4):\n            for j in range(-1, 4):\n                # Skip the 3x3 region itself (i=6,7,8 and j=6,7,8 in the 9x9)\n                if 6 <= br_start_row - top_row + i <= 8 and 6 <= br_start_col - left_col + j <= 8:\n                    continue\n                # Check if this adjacent position is within the 9x9 region\n                r = br_start_row + i\n                c = br_start_col + j\n                if top_row <= r < top_row + 9 and left_col <= c < left_col + 9:\n                    if grid[r][c] != '.':\n                        return False\n        \n        return True\n    \n    # Find all valid Tak Code regions\n    results = []\n    for i in range(N - 8):  # top row can be from 0 to N-9\n        for j in range(M - 8):  # left col can be from 0 to M-9\n            if is_valid_tak_code(i, j):\n                results.append((i + 1, j + 1))  # Convert to 1-indexed\n    \n    # Output results in lexicographical order (already sorted by the loop)\n    for i, j in results:\n        print(i, j)\n\nfind_tak_codes()\n```",
