    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_460",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of integers nums of length n, and a positive odd integer k.\nThe strength of x subarrays is defined as strength = sum[1] * x - sum[2] * (x - 1) + sum[3] * (x - 2) - sum[4] * (x - 3) + ... + sum[x] * 1 where sum[i] is the sum of the elements in the i^th subarray. Formally, strength is sum of (-1)^i+1 * sum[i] * (x - i + 1) over all i's such that 1 <= i <= x.\nYou need to select k disjoint subarrays from nums, such that their strength is maximum.\nReturn the maximum possible strength that can be obtained.\nNote that the selected subarrays don't need to cover the entire array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,-1,2], k = 3\nOutput: 22\nExplanation: The best possible way to select 3 subarrays is: nums[0..2], nums[3..3], and nums[4..4]. The strength is (1 + 2 + 3) * 3 - (-1) * 2 + 2 * 1 = 22.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [12,-2,-2,-2,-2], k = 5\nOutput: 64\nExplanation: The only possible way to select 5 disjoint subarrays is: nums[0..0], nums[1..1], nums[2..2], nums[3..3], and nums[4..4]. The strength is 12 * 5 - (-2) * 4 + (-2) * 3 - (-2) * 2 + (-2) * 1 = 64.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [-1,-2,-3], k = 1\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: The best possible way to select 1 subarray is: nums[0..0]. The strength is -1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n <= 10^4\n-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n1 <= k <= n\n1 <= n * k <= 10^6\nk is odd.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maximumStrength(nums, k):\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # dp[i][j][0] = maximum strength using first i elements, j subarrays, last subarray not ending at i-1\n    # dp[i][j][1] = maximum strength using first i elements, j subarrays, last subarray ending at i-1\n    # We use two states because we need to track whether we're continuing a subarray or starting a new one\n    \n    # Initialize with negative infinity\n    NEG_INF = float('-inf')\n    \n    # Use 2D DP where dp[j][ended] represents max strength using j subarrays \n    # ended=0 means we're not in middle of a subarray, ended=1 means we are\n    prev_dp = [[NEG_INF, NEG_INF] for _ in range(k + 1)]\n    prev_dp[0][0] = 0  # Base case: 0 subarrays, not in middle of one, strength = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        curr_dp = [[NEG_INF, NEG_INF] for _ in range(k + 1)]\n        \n        for j in range(k + 1):\n            # Copy previous state (skip current element)\n            if j <= i:\n                curr_dp[j][0] = max(prev_dp[j][0], prev_dp[j][1])\n            \n            if j > 0:\n                # Calculate the multiplier for the j-th subarray\n                # For subarray j: multiplier = (k - j + 1) with alternating signs\n                multiplier = k - j + 1\n                if j % 2 == 0:  # Even indexed subarray (2nd, 4th, etc.)\n                    multiplier = -multiplier\n                \n                # Start new subarray at current position\n                if prev_dp[j-1][0] != NEG_INF:\n                    curr_dp[j][1] = max(curr_dp[j][1], prev_dp[j-1][0] + nums[i] * multiplier)\n                \n                # Continue existing subarray\n                if prev_dp[j][1] != NEG_INF:\n                    curr_dp[j][1] = max(curr_dp[j][1], prev_dp[j][1] + nums[i] * multiplier)\n                \n                # Also can start new subarray from nothing\n                if j == 1:\n                    curr_dp[j][1] = max(curr_dp[j][1], nums[i] * multiplier)\n        \n        prev_dp = curr_dp\n    \n    # Answer is the maximum of using exactly k subarrays (either ending or not ending at last element)\n    result = max(prev_dp[k][0], prev_dp[k][1])\n    return result\n```",
