15\n16 17 18 19 20\n1 3 2 5 4\n11 13 12 15 14\n6 8 7 10 9\n16 18 17 20 19\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3\n\nSwapping the fourth and fifth columns of the initial grid A yields the following grid:\n1 2 3 5 4\n6 7 8 10 9\n11 12 13 15 14\n16 17 18 20 19\n\nThen, swapping the second and third rows yields the following grid:\n1 2 3 5 4\n11 12 13 15 14\n6 7 8 10 9\n16 17 18 20 19\n\nFinally, swapping the second and third columns yields the following grid, which is identical to grid B:\n1 3 2 5 4\n11 13 12 15 14\n6 8 7 10 9\n16 18 17 20 19\n\nYou can make grid A identical to grid B with the three operations above and cannot do so with fewer operations, so print 3.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n2 2\n1 1\n1 1\n1 1\n1 1000000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\n\nThere is no way to perform the operation to make grid A match grid B, so print -1.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n3 3\n8 1 6\n3 5 7\n4 9 2\n8 1 6\n3 5 7\n4 9 2\n\nSample Output 3\n\n0\n\nGrid A is already identical to grid B at the beginning.\n\nSample Input 4\n\n5 5\n710511029 136397527 763027379 644706927 447672230\n979861204 57882493 442931589 951053644 152300688\n43971370 126515475 962139996 541282303 834022578\n312523039 506696497 664922712 414720753 304621362\n325269832 191410838 286751784 732741849 806602693\n806602693 732741849 286751784 191410838 325269832\n304621362 414720753 664922712 506696497 312523039\n834022578 541282303 962139996 126515475 43971370\n152300688 951053644 442931589 57882493 979861204\n447672230 644706927 763027379 136397527 710511029\n\nSample Output 4\n\n20",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom itertools import permutations\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    H = int(data[0])\n    W = int(data[1])\n    \n    # Read grid A\n    A = []\n    index = 2\n    for i in range(H):\n        row = list(map(int, data[index:index+W]))\n        A.append(row)\n        index += W\n    \n    # Read grid B\n    B = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        row = list(map(int, data[index:index+W]))\n        B.append(row)\n        index += W\n    \n    # If A is already equal to B\n    if A == B:\n        print(0)\n        return\n    \n    # We can swap rows and columns arbitrarily. This means we can rearrange\n    # the rows and columns of A in any order. We want to find if there's a \n    # permutation of rows and columns that transforms A into B, and minimize \n    # the number of adjacent swaps needed.\n    \n    # The minimum number of adjacent swaps to achieve a permutation is the \n    # number of inversions in that permutation.\n    def count_inversions(perm):\n        inversions = 0\n        for i in range(len(perm)):\n            for j in range(i+1, len(perm)):\n                if perm[i] > perm[j]:\n                    inversions += 1\n        return inversions\n    \n    min_operations = float('inf')\n    \n    # Try all possible row permutations and column permutations\n    for row_perm in permutations(range(H)):\n        for col_perm in permutations(range(W)):\n            # Apply this permutation to A\n            A_transformed = [[A[row_perm[i]][col_perm[j]] for j in range(W)] for i in range(H)]\n            \n            # Check if it matches B\n            if A_transformed == B:\n                # Count operations (adjacent swaps) needed\n                row_ops = count_inversions(row_perm)\n                col_ops = count_inversions(col_perm)\n                total_ops = row_ops + col_ops\n                min_operations = min(min_operations, total_ops)\n    \n    if min_operations == float('inf'):\n        print(-1)\n    else:\n        print(min_operations)\n\nsolve()\n```",
