    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_167",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of positive integers nums.\nA subarray of nums is called incremovable if nums becomes strictly increasing on removing the subarray. For example, the subarray [3, 4] is an incremovable subarray of [5, 3, 4, 6, 7] because removing this subarray changes the array [5, 3, 4, 6, 7] to [5, 6, 7] which is strictly increasing.\nReturn the total number of incremovable subarrays of nums.\nNote that an empty array is considered strictly increasing.\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,4]\nOutput: 10\nExplanation: The 10 incremovable subarrays are: [1], [2], [3], [4], [1,2], [2,3], [3,4], [1,2,3], [2,3,4], and [1,2,3,4], because on removing any one of these subarrays nums becomes strictly increasing. Note that you cannot select an empty subarray.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [6,5,7,8]\nOutput: 7\nExplanation: The 7 incremovable subarrays are: [5], [6], [5,7], [6,5], [5,7,8], [6,5,7] and [6,5,7,8].\nIt can be shown that there are only 7 incremovable subarrays in nums.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [8,7,6,6]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The 3 incremovable subarrays are: [8,7,6], [7,6,6], and [8,7,6,6]. Note that [8,7] is not an incremovable subarray because after removing [8,7] nums becomes [6,6], which is sorted in ascending order but not strictly increasing.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 50\n1 <= nums[i] <= 50",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this step-by-step.\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\n1. We need to find subarrays that, when removed, leave the remaining elements in strictly increasing order\n2. A strictly increasing sequence means each element is greater than the previous one (no equals allowed)\n3. We're counting all possible contiguous subarrays that satisfy this condition\n\n## Approach\n\n1. First, understand when removal creates a strictly increasing array:\n   - After removing a subarray from index i to j, we have elements [0, i-1] and [j+1, n-1] remaining\n   - These two parts need to form a strictly increasing sequence when concatenated\n   - This means:\n     - The left part [0, i-1] must be strictly increasing\n     - The right part [j+1, n-1] must be strictly increasing\n     - If both parts exist, the last element of left part < first element of right part\n\n2. Strategy:\n   - For each possible subarray (defined by start and end indices), check if removing it results in a strictly increasing array\n   - We can do this by:\n     - Getting the remaining elements after removal\n     - Checking if they form a strictly increasing sequence\n\n## Step-by-step Solution\n\n```python\ndef incremovableSubarrayCount(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    count = 0\n    \n    # Try all possible subarrays (start, end) inclusive\n    for start in range(n):\n        for end in range(start, n):\n            # Create array after removing subarray [start, end]\n            remaining = []\n            \n            # Add elements before start\n            for i in range(start):\n                remaining.append(nums[i])\n            \n            # Add elements after end\n            for i in range(end + 1, n):\n                remaining.append(nums[i])\n            \n            # Check if remaining array is strictly increasing\n            is_strictly_increasing = True\n            \n            # Empty array or single element is strictly increasing\n            if len(remaining) <= 1:\n                is_strictly_increasing = True\n            else:\n                # Check each adjacent pair\n                for i in range(1, len(remaining)):\n                    if remaining[i] <= remaining[i-1]:\n                        is_strictly_increasing = False\n                        break\n            \n            if is_strictly_increasing:\n                count += 1\n    \n    return count\n```",
