    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_60",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nGiven three strings a, b, and c, your task is to find a string that has the minimum length and contains all three strings as substrings.\nIf there are multiple such strings, return the lexicographically smallest one.\nReturn a string denoting the answer to the problem.\nNotes\n\nA string a is lexicographically smaller than a string b (of the same length) if in the first position where a and b differ, string a has a letter that appears earlier in the alphabet than the corresponding letter in b.\nA substring is a contiguous sequence of characters within a string.\n\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: a = \"abc\", b = \"bca\", c = \"aaa\"\nOutput: \"aaabca\"\nExplanation:  We show that \"aaabca\" contains all the given strings: a = ans[2...4], b = ans[3..5], c = ans[0..2]. It can be shown that the length of the resulting string would be at least 6 and \"aaabca\" is the lexicographically smallest one.\nExample 2:\n\nInput: a = \"ab\", b = \"ba\", c = \"aba\"\nOutput: \"aba\"\nExplanation: We show that the string \"aba\" contains all the given strings: a = ans[0..1], b = ans[1..2], c = ans[0..2]. Since the length of c is 3, the length of the resulting string would be at least 3. It can be shown that \"aba\" is the lexicographically smallest one.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= a.length, b.length, c.length <= 100\na, b, c consist only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the shortest string that contains all three given strings as substrings, and if there are multiple such strings, return the lexicographically smallest one.\n\nThe key insight is that I need to consider all possible orderings of the three strings and try to merge them optimally by maximizing overlap between consecutive strings.\n\nHere's my approach:\n1. Create a function to merge two strings by finding the maximum overlap\n2. Try all 6 possible permutations of the three strings\n3. For each permutation, merge the strings in order\n4. Return the shortest result, and if tied, the lexicographically smallest one\n\n```python\ndef minimumString(a, b, c):\n    def merge(s1, s2):\n        # If s2 is already in s1, return s1\n        if s2 in s1:\n            return s1\n        \n        # Try to find maximum overlap where suffix of s1 matches prefix of s2\n        max_overlap = 0\n        for i in range(1, min(len(s1), len(s2)) + 1):\n            if s1[-i:] == s2[:i]:\n                max_overlap = i\n        \n        return s1 + s2[max_overlap:]\n    \n    # Generate all permutations of the three strings\n    permutations = [\n        [a, b, c],\n        [a, c, b],\n        [b, a, c],\n        [b, c, a],\n        [c, a, b],\n        [c, b, a]\n    ]\n    \n    results = []\n    \n    for perm in permutations:\n        # Merge the three strings in the given order\n        merged = perm[0]\n        merged = merge(merged, perm[1])\n        merged = merge(merged, perm[2])\n        results.append(merged)\n    \n    # Find the result with minimum length, and lexicographically smallest if tied\n    min_length = min(len(r) for r in results)\n    candidates = [r for r in results if len(r) == min_length]\n    \n    return min(candidates)\n```",
