he subset of vertices in mask\n    dp = [0] * (1 << n)\n\n    # Iterate over all masks\n    for mask in range(1 << n):\n        # Try to pick any two unused vertices i, j and add edge (i, j)\n        for i in range(n):\n            if mask & (1 << i):\n                continue\n            for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                if mask & (1 << j):\n                    continue\n                new_mask = mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j)\n                dp[new_mask] = max(dp[new_mask], dp[mask] + D[i][j])\n    \n    print(dp[(1 << n) - 1])\n\nmain()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- We read input and build a full adjacency matrix.\n- `dp[mask]` stores the maximum total weight of edges selected so far using only vertices in the mask.\n- For each state (mask), we consider adding an edge between any two unused vertices (i, j), update the new state.\n- The final answer is `dp[(1<<n)-1]` — all vertices used (though note: when `n` is odd, we cannot use all vertices; but our DP naturally handles that — it only uses disjoint edges and may leave one out if needed, but since we're maximizing, it doesn't force all vertices to be used — wait: is this correct?).\n\nWait! Our DP only updates states by adding edges, but note: we are not forcing to use all vertices. The above loop will consider all possible matchings.\n\nHowever, in our updates, we only set `dp[new_mask] = max(...)`, but `dp` values propagate from smaller masks. The way the loops are structured, every matching will be considered, and the maximum over **all** matchings is stored in `dp` array.\n\nBut we are printing `dp[(1<<n)-1]` — which is the full mask. That's only valid if n is even. But in problem, **n can be odd** — then full mask won't have all vertices matched. And indeed, for n=3, full mask has 3 bits set — odd — cannot have a perfect matching.\n\nSo: we must not return only `dp[full]`. Instead, answer is the maximum value across **all** `dp[mask]` — but actually no: we are building up matchings, and it's possible 