checked is sum over all type-2 queries of (R-L+1)\n\nIn worst-case, it could be 5e5 * 5e5 = 250e9, which is too many.\n\nBut we cannot avoid this if we have to check each one.\n\n unless we can use a segment tree that can check if a node's segment is alternating, and handle lazy flip.\n\n### Heavy solution: Segment tree with lazy propagation for:\n\n- Each node stores:\n  - `l`, `r`: range\n  - `left_val`, `right_val`: the value at the leftmost and rightmost position in the segment\n  - `is_good`: whether the segment is good (alternating)\n  - `lazy` : whether the segment is flipped (XOR pending)\n\nAnd metadata to combine: when merging two segments, we need to check if the combined string is good: \n- left.is_good and right.is_good and (left.right_val != right.left_val)\n\nBut also, if the segment is flipped, then values are bit-flipped, but the alternating property is preserved flipping (0101 -> 1010, still good), so `is_good` is unchanged, but `left_val`, `right_val` are flipped.\n\nSo we can design a segment tree.\n\nBut implementation is long in Python, but possible.\n\nGiven the time, and the fact that this is a standard approach for such problems, we'll go with the segment tree.\n\n### Segment Tree Design:\n\n- Each node covers [l, r]\n- We store:\n  - `size = r - l + 1`\n  - `l_val`: the current (effective) bit at position l\n  - `r_val`: at position r\n  - `ok`: whether this segment is good (alternating internally)\n  - `lazy`: the number of flip toggles pending (0 or 1) — XOR flag\n\n- Build:\n  - Leaf: l_val = r_val = initial_bit, ok = True (single char is good), lazy = 0\n\n- Merge:\n  - If left.size == 0: return right\n  - If right.size == 0: return left\n  - Otherwise: `ok = left.ok and right.ok and (left.r_val != right.l_val)`\n  - `l_val = left.l_val`\n  - `r_val = right.r_val`\n  - (size is sum)\n\n- Lazy propagation:\n  - When apply flip to a node: \n    - if lazy is 1, then flip means: \n      - `l_val = 1 - l_val` (or `l_val ^= 1`)\n      - `r_val = 1 - r_val`\n      - `ok` remains the same, because flip doesn't change alternating property\n      - propagate lazy to children if any\n  - So we can store lazy as a boolean.\n\n- Range update: flip [L,R] — standard segment tree range update with lazy\n\n- Query: for [L,R], return a node for that segment, then we can reply \"Yes\" if `ok` is True, else \"No\"\n\nBut the query should return a node so we can check `ok`.\n\nWe don't need to print the string, only whether it's good.\n\n### Implementation:\n\nWe'll implement a segment tree with:\n\n- `__init__(s)`\n- `update(l, r)` -> flip [l,r] (1-indexed)\n- `query(l, r)` -> returns (l_val, r_val, ok, size) for the segment [l,r]\n\nBut easier to return a node.\n\nLet's do it.\n\nNote: string is 1-indexed in input, so we use 1..N.\n\nWe'll use 0-indexed internally.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nComplexity: O(log N) per update and query.\n\nTotal: O(Q * log N) ~ 5e5 * 19 = 9.5e6, acceptable in Python.\n\nLet's implement.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(1 << 25)\n\nclass SegmentTree:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.n = len(data)\n        self.data = data  # 0-indexed list of integers (0 or 1)\n        self.size = 1\n        while self.size < self.n:\n            self.size *= 2\n        # tree: l_val, r_val, ok, lazy, size\n        # We'll use arrays for tree\n        self.l_val = [0] * (2 * self.size)\n        self.r_val = [0] * (2 * self.size)\n        self.ok = [True] * (2 * self.size)\n        self.lazy = [0] * (2 * self.size)  # XOR lazy\n        self._build()\n    \n    def _build(self):\n        # build leaves\n        for i in range(self.n):\n            val = self.data[i]\n            idx = i + self.size\n            self.l_val[idx] = val\n            self.r_val[idx] = val\n            self.ok[idx] = True\n        for i in range(self.size):\n            idx = i + self.size\n            if i >= self.n:\n                # out of range, mark as invalid\n                self.l_val[idx] = 0\n                self.r_val[idx] = 0\n                self.ok[idx] = True\n        # build internal nodes\n        for i in range(self.size - 1, 0, -1):\n            self._pull(i)\n    \n    def _pull(self, i):\n        l = 2 * i\n        r = 2 * i + 1\n        # merge left and right\n        l_val_l = self.l_val[l]\n        r_val_l = self.r_val[l]\n        ok_l = self.ok[l]\n        l_val_r = self.l_val[r]\n        r_val_r = self.r_val[r]\n        ok_r = self.ok[r]\n        size_l = (self.size // (i*2)) * ?  # we don't have size stored per node\n```\n\nWe see that we need the size of child segments.\n\nInstead, let's make a node have: l_val, r_val, ok, and also we can infer size if we know the segment.\n\nBetter: store the tree with explicit ranges in functions, or store an array `node_size` or compute by segment.\n\nAlternatively, we can do a recursive segment tree.\n\nBut iterative is faster, but recursive is simpler for this.\n\nLet's do recursive.\n\n### Recursive Segment Tree:\n\n```python\nclass SegmentTree:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.n = len(data)\n        self.data = data\n        # tree will be built for [0, n-1]\n        self.tree = [None] * (4 * self.n)\n        self.lazy = [0] * (4 * self.n)  # 0 for no flip, 1 for need flip\n        self._build(1, 0, self.n-1)\n    \n    def _build(self, node, l, r):\n        if l == r:\n            val = self.data[l]\n            self.tree[node] = {'l_val': val, 'r_val': val, 'ok': True, 'size': 1}\n        else:\n            mid = (l+r)//2\n            self._build(2*node, l, mid)\n            self._build(2*node+1, mid+1, r)\n            self._pull(node, l, r, mid)\n    \n    def _pull(self, node, l, r, mid):\n        left = self.tree[2*node]\n        right = self.tree[2* node+1]\n        l_val = left['l_val']\n        r_val = right['r_val']\n        size = left['size'] + right['size']\n        if not left['ok'] or not right['ok']:\n            ok = False\n        else:\n            if left['r_val'] != right['l_val']:\n                ok = True\n            else:\n                ok = False\n        self.tree[node] = {'l_val': l_val, 'r_val': r_val, 'ok': ok, 'size': size}\n    \n    def _push(self, node, l, r):\n        if self.lazy[node]:\n            # Apply flip to this node\n            self.tree[node]['l_val'] ^= 1\n            self.tree[node]['r_val'] ^= 1\n            # 'ok' remains the same\n            if l != r:\n                self.lazy[2*node] ^= 1\n                self.lazy[2*node+1] ^= 1\n            self.lazy[node] = 0\n    \n    def update(self, ul, ur, l=0, r=None, node=1):\n        if r is None:\n            r = self.n-1\n        self._push(node, l, r)\n        if ur < l or r < ul:\n            return\n        if ul <= l and r <= ur:\n            self.lazy[node] ^= 1\n            self._push(node, l, r)\n            return\n        mid = (l+r)//2\n        self.update(ul, ur, l, mid, 2*node)\n        self.update(ul, ur, mid+1, r, 2*node+1)\n        self._pull(node, l, r, mid)\n    \n    def query(self, ql, qr, l=0, r=None, node=1):\n        if r is None:\n            r = self.n-1\n        self._push(node, l, r)\n        if qr < l or r < ql:\n            return None\n        if ql <= l and r <= qr:\n            return self.tree[node].copy()\n        mid = (l+r)//2\n        left_res = self.query(ql, qr, l, mid, 2*node)\n        right_res = self.query(ql, qr, mid+1, r, 2*node+1)\n        if left_res is None:\n            return right_res\n        if right_res is None:\n            return left_res\n        # merge\n        if left_res['r_val'] != right_res['l_val'] and left_res['ok'] and right_res['ok']:\n            ok = True\n        else:\n            ok = False\n        l_val = left_res['l_val']\n        r_val = right_res['r_val']\n        size = left_res['size'] + right_res['size']\n        return {'l_val': l_val, 'r_val': r_val, 'ok': ok, 'size': size}\n```\n\nNow, we use it.\n\nSteps:\n\n- Read N, Q\n- Read S\n- Convert S to list of int: [int(c) for c in S]\n- Build segment tree\n- For each query:\n  - If type 1: 1 L R -> update(L-1, R-1) (0-indexed)\n  - If type 2: 2 L R -> query(L-1, R-1), then print \"Yes\" if result['ok'] is True, else \"No\"\n\nNote: a single char has 'ok' = True.\n\nLet's test with sample.\n\nSample: \"10100\", N=5\n\n- Initially:\n  - [1,0,1,0,0]\n  - query [0,2]: \"101\" -> should be good: \n    - 1,0: good, 0,1: good -> yes\n  - query [0,4]: \"10100\" -> ...0 and next 0 -> not good\n\nBuild tree manually would be complex, but code should work.\n\nLet's write the function accordingly.\n\nNote: sys.setrecursionlimit may be needed.\n\nWe'll write it.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nNote: we are not allowed to output anything except the answers.\n\nWe'll now write the complete code.",
