 — all generated sizes are larger.\n\nBut: consider:\n- We have size 4 and size 6.\n- Process 4: produces 8.\n- Later, process 3: produces 6 → adds to 6 → now 6 has more.\n- But 6 < 8, so if we process 8 before 6, we might miss.\n\nAh! Here's the problem.\n\nExample:\n- Initially: size 3 with count 4, size 8 with count 1.\n- 3 -> merge: 4//2=2 of size 6.\n- 6 < 8, so we need to process 6 before 8.\n- But if we don’t push 6 into heap early, we won’t.\n\nBut in our case, when processing 3, we do:\n- q = 2 > 0 → new_size = 6\n- count[6] += 2\n- push 6 into heap.\n\nThen heap has 6 and 8. Pop 6 next, then 8.\n\nSo as long as we **push every new_size** when we create it, and also **all original sizes are in heap**, we are good.\n\nBut now: what if **we update an existing size**, like adding to size 6 which was already in the initial data?\n\nWe still need to process it again (after adding new slimes from merge), because the total count has changed.\n\nBut we already processed 6 earlier? Then it's too late.\n\nExample:\n- Initial: size 6 with count 1 (pushed to heap)\n- size 3 with count 6 (pushed)\n\nSuppose heap pops 3 first:\n- 6 of size 3 → 6//2=3 of size 6 → count[6] becomes 1+3 = 4\n- we push 6 (again) into heap.\n\nThen when we later pop 6 (the first time), we process:\n- c = 4 → r = 0, q = 2 → send 2 to 12\n\nBut we might have already processed 6 earlier.\n\nBut if the heap has [3,6], we pop 3 first (because 3<6), update 6, push 6 again.\n\nThen we pop 6 (the original entry), get count[6]=4, process it.\n\nSo it works.\n\nBut we might have multiple entries for same size — but we check `if c == 0: continue`, and after processing, we set count[size] = r.\n\nBut what if we process the same size twice?\n\nFor example:\n- Two heap entries for size 6.\n- First time: c = 4 → do r=0, q=2 → set count[6]=0\n- Then propagate to 12\n- Later: pop second 6 → c = count[6] = 0 → skip.\n\nSo safe.\n\nBut what if **after** we process 6, we get more slimes of size 6 from elsewhere?\n\nBut merging only produces larger sizes (double), so no process produces 6 from merging 12 or above. And from smaller: only 3 → 6.\n\nBut if we process 6 before 3, then:\n- pop 6: c=1 → r=1, q=0 → set count[6]=1, done\n- later: pop 3: c=6 → merge to 3 of size 6 → count[6] = 1 + 3 = 4 → push 6\n- now 6 is in heap again\n- then we pop 6, process: c=4 → r=0, q=2 → propagate\n\nSo we **must process 6 again** — and we do because we push it.\n\nAnd that’s correct.\n\nSo algorithm is:\n\n- Maintain a min-heap of sizes that need to be considered.\n- Initially, all input sizes.\n- Also, whenever we **add** to any size (either initial or via merging), push it to heap (we do this only in `count[new_size] += q`, then push new_size)\n- But: when we read input, we push each $ S_i $\n- When we merge and produce $ q $ slimes of $ 2s $, we:\n   - update count[2s] += q\n   - push 2s into heap\n- Then we pop from heap:\n   - if current count for that size is zero, skip\n   - otherwise, process: merge as many as possible\n   - set count[size] = c % 2\n   - carry c//2 to 2*size (update and push)\n\nNote: we are not removing the original count until we process.\n\nBut when we process, we set it to remainder.\n\nImportant: we must **not** assume we process each size only once.\n\n---\n\n### Final Code\n\nLet’s implement accordingly.\n\nWe’ll:\n- Use `heapq` for min-heap\n- `count` dictionary\n- Push every size we initialize or extend\n- Process until heap empty\n\n```python\nimport heapq\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    n = int(data[0])\n    count = {}\n    heap = []\n    \n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        s = int(data[index]); c = int(data[index+1])\n        index += 2\n        count[s] = c\n        heapq.heappush(heap, s)\n    \n    # Process all sizes in order\n    while heap:\n        size = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        c = count.get(size, 0)\n        if c == 0:\n            continue\n        # Remove all possible pairs\n        remainder = c % 2\n        carry = c // 2\n        # Leave remainder at this size\n        count[size] = remainder\n        if carry > 0:\n            new_size = size * 2\n            count[new_size] = count.get(new_size, 0) + carry\n            heapq.heappush(heap, new_size)\n    \n    # Sum all remaining slimes\n    result = sum(count[s] for s in count if count[s] > 0)\n    print(result)\n\nmain()\n```\n\n---\n\n### Test with Sample 1\n\nInput:\n```\n3\n3 3\n5 1\n6 1\n```\n\n- count: {3:3, 5:1, 6:1}\n- heap: [3,5,6] (min-heap: 3,5,6)\n\n1. pop 3: c=3 → r=1, carry=1 → count[3]=1, add count[6] +=1 → count[6]=2, push 6\n2. pop 5: c=1 → r=1, carry=0 → count[5]=1\n3. pop 6: c=2 → r=0, carry=1 → count[6]=0, add count[12]=1, push 12\n4. pop 12: c=1 → r=1, carry=0 → count[12]=1\n\nFinal counts:\n- 3:1, 5:1, 6:0, 12:1\n- Total slimes: 1+1+1 = 3 → correct\n\n---\n\n### Sample 2\n```\n1 1\n2 1\n3 1\n```\n\nAll counts=1 → remain 1, no carry\nTotal: 1+1+1 = 3 → correct\n\n---\n\n### Sample 3\n```\n1\n1000000000 1000000000\n```\n\nWe have one size: 1e9, count=1e9\n\nWe simulate:\n- c = 1e9\n- each step: c = c // 2, propagate to next size\n- the number of slimes left at each level is c % 2\n- total slimes = number of ones in binary representation of 1e9\n\nLet’s compute popcount(1000000000)\n\n1000000000 in binary:\n\nWe know:\n- 2^30 ≈ 1.07e9\n- 1000000000 = 10^9\n\nLet me compute:\n\n```python\nbin(1000000000).count('1')\n```\n\nActually:\n\n10^9 = 1000000000\n\n2^29 = 536870912  \n1000000000 - 536870912 = 463129088  \n2^28 = 268435456  \n463129088 - 268435456 = 194693632  \n2^27 = 134217728  \n194693632 - 134217728 = 60475904  \n2^25 = 33554432  \n60475904 - 33554432 = 26921472  \n2^24 = 16777216  \n26921472 - 16777216 = 10144256  \n2^23 = 8388608  \n10144256 - 8388608 = 1755648  \n2^20 = 1048576  \n1755648 - 1048576 = 707072  \n2^19 = 524288  \n707072 - 524288 = 182784  \n2^17 = 131072  \n182784 - 131072 = 51712  \n2^15 = 32768  \n51712 - 32768 = 18944  \n2^14 = 16384  \n18944 - 16384 = 2560  \n2^11 = 2048  \n2560 - 2048 = 512  \n2^9 = 512  \n512 - 512 = 0\n\nPowers: 29,28,27,25,24,23,20,19,17,15,14,11,9 → 13 bits.\n\nSo answer is 13.\n\nOur algorithm will:\n- process 1000000000 -> carry 500000000 to 2e9, remain 0 (since 1e9 is even)\n- then 2e9: carry 250000000 to 4e9, remain 0\n- ... until carry becomes odd number\n\nAnd the total number of 1s in binary of 10^9 is 13 → our sum of remainders is 13.\n\nSo output 13 → correct.\n\n---\n\n### Final Code\n\n```python\nimport heapq\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    count = {}\n    heap = []\n    \n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        s = int(data[index])\n        c = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        count[s] = c\n        heapq.heappush(heap, s)\n    \n    while heap:\n        size = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        c = count.get(size, 0)\n        if c == 0:\n            continue\n        remainder = c % 2\n        carry = c // 2\n        count[size] = remainder\n        if carry > 0:\n            new_size = size * 2\n            count[new_size] = count.get(new_size, 0) + carry\n            heapq.heappush(heap, new_size)\n    \n    result = sum(count[s] for s in count if count[s] > 0)\n    print(result)\n\nmain()\n```",
