      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxSumOfSquares(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # The key insight is that the sum of all numbers remains constant\n    # and we can redistribute bits to maximize the sum of squares.\n    # We want to maximize the largest numbers to get the maximum sum of squares.\n    \n    # Count the number of set bits at each position across all numbers\n    bit_counts = [0] * 32  # 32 bits for numbers up to 10^9\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        for i in range(32):\n            if num & (1 << i):\n                bit_counts[i] += 1\n    \n    # Reconstruct the optimal array\n    # We want to create k large numbers and make the rest as small as possible\n    # The sum of all numbers remains the same, but we want to maximize the sum of squares\n    total_sum = sum(nums)\n    \n    # Create the k numbers that will contribute to our answer\n    # We should pack the bits as efficiently as possible into k numbers\n    result_nums = [0] * k\n    \n    # For each bit position from highest to lowest, distribute the bits among the k numbers\n    for bit_pos in range(31, -1, -1):\n        bits_available = bit_counts[bit_pos]\n        # Distribute these bits among the k numbers\n        for i in range(min(bits_available, k)):\n            result_nums[i] |= (1 << bit_pos)\n    \n    # Calculate the sum of squares\n    sum_of_squares = 0\n    for num in result_nums:\n        sum_of_squares = (sum_of_squares + (num * num) % MOD) % MOD\n    \n    return sum_of_squares\n```",
