nLet me verify with examples:\n\nExample 1: N=13, M=3, P=5\n- (13 - 3) // 5 + 1 = 10 // 5 + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3 ✓\n\nExample 2: N=5, M=6, P=6\n- (5 - 6) // 6 + 1 = (-1) // 6 + 1 = -1 + 1 = 0 ✓\n\nExample 3: N=200000, M=314, P=318\n- (200000 - 314) // 318 + 1 = 199686 // 318 + 1\n- 199686 // 318 = let's calculate: 318 * 627 = 318 * 600 + 318 * 27 = 190800 + 8586 = 199386\n  - 199686 - 199386 = 300, so 627 + (300 // 318) = 627\n- So 627 + 1 = 628 ✓\n\nPerfect!\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    line = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    N, M, P = map(int, line.split())\n    \n    # Calculate the number of full moon days between day 1 and day N inclusive\n    # Full moon days are M, M+P, M+2P, ...\n    # We need M + kP <= N, so k <= (N - M) / P\n    # k ranges from 0 to floor((N - M) / P)\n    # Number of values = floor((N - M) / P) + 1, or 0 if negative\n    \n    if N < M:\n        result = 0\n    else:\n        # k goes from 0 to (N-M)//P (inclusive)\n        # Number of days = (N-M)//P + 1\n        result = (N - M) // P + 1\n    \n    print(result)\n\nsolve()\n```",
