ighbors: 1 (9>=2), 3 (3>=2) → no neighbor with W<2 → dp[2]=1\n3. vertex 3: W=3 → neighbors: 1(9>=3), 2(2<3), 4(1<3)\n   items: vertex2: W=2, dp[2]=1; vertex4: W=1, dp[4]=1\n   capacity = 2\n   knapsack: items: (2,1), (1,1)\n   possible: take (1,1) → sum W=1<3 → value=1; or take (2,1) → value=1; or both: sum W=3 not <3 → invalid\n   max value = 1 → dp[3] = 1+1 = 2\n4. vertex 5: W=4 → neighbors: 1(9>=4), 6(4==4) → no neighbor with W<4 → dp[5]=1\n5. vertex 6: W=4 → neighbors: 5(4==4) → no neighbor with W<4 → dp[6]=1\n6. vertex 1: W=9 → neighbors: 2(2<9), 3(3<9), 5(4<9) → all W<9\n   items: (2, dp[2]=1), (3, dp[3]=2), (4, dp[5]=1)\n   weights: 2,3,4; values:1,2,1\n   capacity = 8\n   knapsack:\n      We want max value with sum W < 9 → capacity=8\n      Check combinations:\n        2+3+4=9 → >8 → invalid\n        2+3=5 → value=1+2=3\n        2+4=6 → value=1+1=2\n        3+4=7 → value=2+1=3\n        2:1, 3:2, 4:1\n        max value = 3\n      So dp[1] = 1+3 = 4\n\nThen total operations = A[1]*dp[1] + A[2]*dp[2] + ... + A[6]*dp[6]\n= 1*4 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 1*1 = 4 + 1 = 5 → matches.\n\nPerfect.\n\nSample 2: A = [0,0] → 0.\n\nSo we code accordingly.\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        print(0)\n        return\n    \n    # Parse first line\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n    \n    graph = [[] for _ in range(N+1)]\n    \n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n        \n    W = [0] * (N+1)\n    for i in range(1, N+1):\n        W[i] = int(next(it))\n        \n    A = [0] * (N+1)\n    for i in range(1, N+1):\n        A[i] = int(next(it))\n        \n    # If no pieces, answer 0\n    if sum(A[1:]) == 0:\n        print(0)\n        return\n        \n    # Sort vertices by W descending\n    vertices = list(range(1, N+1))\n    vertices.sort(key=lambda x