id, `[4,3]` valid → so best starting at index 1.\n\nSo algorithm:\n```python\nfor each test case:\n    n = ...\n    a = list of n numbers\n\n    best = -inf\n    # We'll keep current subarray sum that ends at i, and last element\n    # But we don't need to store full, just current running sum if valid\n\n    # We'll simulate: let dp[i] = max sum of valid subarray ending at i\n    # But transitions: we can only come from i-1 if parity(a[i]) != parity(a[i-1])\n    # Otherwise, start fresh\n\n    So:\n        dp[0] = a[0]\n        for i from 1 to n-1:\n            if a[i] % 2 != a[i-1] % 2:\n                dp[i] = max(a[i], dp[i-1] + a[i])\n            else:\n                dp[i] = a[i]  # can't extend, so start new\n\n        answer = max(dp[i] for all i)\n\n    But wait: is `dp[i-1] + a[i]` always safe?\n    Yes, because if the last two have alternating parity, then the full subarray alternating?\n    Only depends on last two? The earlier part was already alternating (by induction). The only potential break is between i-1 and i.\n\n    So yes: if we maintain that each `dp[i]` represents the maximum sum of an alternating subarray ending at `i`, then:\n\n        if a[i] and a[i-1] have different parity:\n            we can extend the subarray ending at i-1\n            so: candidate = dp[i-1] + a[i]\n        else:\n            we can't extend — must start new at i\n\n        dp[i] = max(a[i], dp[i-1] + a[i])   if can extend\n                = a[i]                      otherwise\n\n    BUT: wait — why `max(a[i], dp[i-1]+a[i])` when we can extend? Shouldn’t we always consider extending or starting?\n\n    Actually, standard Kadane style: we want to extend only if it improves.\n\n    But: `dp[i-1]` could be negative. So better to start fresh?\n\n    YES! For example: `[3, -5, 4]`, both odd, even, odd — alternating.\n\n    But suppose `dp[1] = -5`, and now at `4`, -5 and 4: odd even → different? 4 is even, -5 is odd → yes.\n\n    But adding -5 may reduce our sum. So we s