99\n        -99(odd),101(odd): same → dp[2] = 101\n        ans = max(101, -99, 101) = 101 ✅\n\n    But wait, can we have just 101 at end? yes.\n\n    Another: `[2, -1, 2]`\n        2(even), -1(odd): different → dp[1] = max(-1, 2-1)=max(-1,1)=1\n        -1(odd), 2(even): diff → dp[2] = max(2, 1+2)=3\n        So answer=3\n\n    But subarray [2,-1,2] is valid (even,odd,even) → sum=3 → ok.\n\n    But what about: `[2, -3, 4]`\n        2(even), -3(odd): diff → dp[1]=max(-3, 2-3)=max(-3,-1)=-1\n        -3(odd),4(even): diff → dp[2]=max(4, -1+4)=4 → so sum=4\n        But [2,-3,4] = 3 → but we get 4? wait no: we do:\n            dp[0]=2\n            dp[1]=max(-3, 2-3)=max(-3,-1)=-1\n            dp[2]=max(4, -1+4)=max(4,3)=4 → so we are choosing starting at 4? yes → but [4] is valid.\n\n        But wait, is it possible that skipping -3 leads to better sum?\n        However, we are not skipping — we are forced to consider alternating. From 2 to 4: even to even → can't take both. So [2,4] invalid.\n\n        So allowed: [2], [-3], [4], [2,-3], [-3,4], [2,-3,4]\n\n        [2,-3,4]: even,odd,even → valid → sum=3\n        But our dp[2]=4, which means we started at 4 → so we take single 4 → sum=4\n\n        That's better.\n\n        So answer 4 is correct.\n\n    So algorithm seems solid.\n\n    However: what if best subarray skips more than one? But subarray must be contiguous. We consider every ending index, and build best ending there.\n\n    So we need to consider every index as end, and take best.\n\n---\n\n### But one more edge: could we extend from earlier than i-1?\n\nNo, because:\n- We can only extend if the **immediate neighbor** has different parity.\n- So transition is only from `i-1` if possible.\n- But what if `a[i-1]` has same parity as `a[i]`, but `a[i-2]` has different?\n   - But then `a[i-2]` and `a[i-1]` might not alternate — we require a continuous valid chain.\n\nWe are already handling by building `dp[i]` from `dp[i-1]` only when possible.\n\nSo the recurrence is correct.\n\n---\n\nLet's write the code accordingly.\n\n**Important**: we must handle multiple test cases. Total n over test cases <= 200,000 → O(n) per test case is acceptable.\n\n---\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    t = int(data[0])\n    index = 1\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(data[index]); index += 1\n        a = list(map(int, data[index:index+n]))\n        index += n\n        \n        if n == 1:\n            results.append(str(a[0]))\n            continue\n            \n        # dp[i] = maximum sum of valid alternating subarray ending at i\n        dp = [0] * n\n        dp[0] = a[0]\n        best = a[0]\n        \n        for i in range(1, n):\n            # Check parity of a[i] and a[i-1]\n            if (a[i] % 2) != (a[i-1] % 2):\n                # Can extend\n                dp[i] = max(a[i], dp[i-1] + a[i])\n            else:\n                dp[i] = a[i]\n            if dp[i] > best:\n                best = dp[i]\n                \n        results.append(str(best))\n    \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nLet’s test on the provided example.\n\nSample Input:\n```\n7\n5\n1 2 3 4 5\n4\n9 9 8 8\n6\n-1 4 -1 0 5 -4\n4\n-1 2 4 -3\n1\n-1000\n3\n101 -99 101\n20\n-10 5 -8 10 6 -10 7 9 -2 -6 7 2 -4 6 -1 7 -6 -7 4 1\n```\n\nExpected Output:\n```\n15\n17\n8\n4\n-1000\n101\n10\n```\n\nOur code:\n\n- First: [1,2,3,4,5] → dp: [1,3,6,10,15] → 15 ✅\n- Second: [9,9,8,8]\n    - dp[0]=9\n    - dp[1]=9 (same parity)\n    - dp[2]=max(8,9+8)=17\n    - dp[3]=8 → best=17 ✅\n- Third: [-1,4,-1,0,5,-4]\n    - dp: -1, max(4, -1+4)=4 → 4\n    - then 4 and -1: even/odd → diff → max(-1,4-1)=3 → 3\n    - then -1 to 0: odd/even → diff → max(0,3+0)=3\n    - 0 to 5: even/odd → diff → max(5,3+5)=8\n    - 5 to -4: odd/even → diff → max(-4,8-4)=4\n    - best = max(-1,4,3,3,8,4)=8 ✅\n- Fourth: [-1,2,4,-3]\n    - -1 → -1\n    - -1 to 2: odd/even → diff → 