      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum element-sum of a \"complete\" subset where the product of every pair of elements is a perfect square.\n\nLet me analyze what makes a subset \"complete\":\n- For any two indices i and j in the subset, the product i×j must be a perfect square\n- This means √(i×j) must be an integer\n\nKey insight: Two numbers i and j have a product that is a perfect square if and only if when we remove all square factors from both i and j, we're left with the same number.\n\nFor example:\n- For i=2: 2 = 2 × 1², so the \"square-free part\" is 2\n- For j=8: 8 = 2 × 2², so the \"square-free part\" is 2\n- Since both have the same square-free part (2), their product 2×8=16 is a perfect square\n\nThe algorithm:\n1. For each index (1 to n), find its square-free part by removing all square factors\n2. Group indices by their square-free part\n3. For each group, the sum of nums values gives a valid complete subset\n4. Return the maximum sum among all groups\n\n```python\ndef maximumSum(nums):\n    def get_square_free_part(n):\n        i = 2\n        result = n\n        while i * i <= result:\n            while result % (i * i) == 0:\n                result //= (i * i)\n            i += 1\n        return result\n    \n    n = len(nums)\n    groups = {}\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        square_free = get_square_free_part(i)\n        if square_free not in groups:\n            groups[square_free] = 0\n        groups[square_free] += nums[i - 1]  # convert to 0-indexed for array access\n    \n    return max(groups.values())\n```",
