      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    \n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    \n    cards = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[1 + 2*i])\n        c = int(data[1 + 2*i + 1])\n        cards.append((a, c, i+1))  # (strength, cost, original_index)\n    \n    # Sort cards by strength in ascending order\n    cards.sort()\n    \n    # We'll use a stack to maintain the \"dominant\" cards\n    # A card should be kept if there's no other card that has both higher strength and lower cost\n    # When processing cards in increasing order of strength:\n    # - If current card has lower cost than the last card in our result, the last card is dominated\n    # - We need to remove all cards in our result that have higher cost than current card\n    \n    remaining = []  # Will store (cost, original_index)\n    \n    for strength, cost, idx in cards:\n        # Remove all cards from the end of remaining that have higher cost than current\n        # because current card has higher strength (due to sorting) and lower cost\n        while remaining and remaining[-1][0] > cost:\n            remaining.pop()\n        \n        # Add current card\n        remaining.append((cost, idx))\n    \n    # Extract the original indices and sort them (they should already be in increasing order of index, but let's be safe)\n    result_indices = sorted([idx for cost, idx in remaining])\n    \n    # Output\n    print(len(result_indices))\n    print(' '.join(map(str, result_indices)))\n\nsolve()\n```",
