 can return to same `n`.\n\nThen same state repeats.\n\nAfter 10 moves? The game ends and Vova wins.\n\nBut can Vanya force a win in 5 moves?\n\nAfter:\n\n- Move 1 (Vanya): n → n+1\n- Move 2 (Vova): n+1 → n (if she plays back)\n- Move 3 (Vanya): n → n+1\n- Move 4 (Vova): n+1 → n\n- ... repeats\n\nNever divisible by 3 after Vanya’s move.\n\nSo Vova can always mirror back → Vanya never wins → Vova wins.\n\nSo if Vova can mirror, she wins.\n\nBut what if Vanya chooses differently?\n\nNo — at each of his turns, from `n`, his only moves are to ±1 → both not divisible by 3. Then Vova can always revert.\n\nSo if `n % 3 == 0`, and both play optimally, Vova can force that **after every Vanya move**, the number is not divisible by 3, for all 5 of his moves.\n\nTherefore, Vova wins.\n\nNow, if `n % 3 != 0`, then Vanya can win on **first move**:\n- if `n ≡ 1 mod 3`: then `n - 1 = 0 mod 3` → subtract → win.\n- if `n ≡ 2 mod 3`: then `n + 1 = 0 mod 3` → add → win.\n\nSo he wins immediately.\n\nBut wait — is there any case where `n % 3 != 0` but Vova can prevent the win? No, because Vanya wins **immediately** on his first move.\n\nThe only way Vanya doesn’t win immediately is if **both** n+1 and n-1 are not divisible by 3 → which only happens if `n ≡ 0 mod 3`.\n\nOtherwise, he can choose one move to make it divisible by 3.\n\nSo conclusion:\n\n- If `n % 3 == 0` → Vanya **cannot** win on first move, and Vova can keep forcing the number back to multiples of 3 or avoid giving Vanya a chance? But more simply: Vova can always mirror or prevent.\n\nBut can Vanya win **later** if `n % 3 != 0`? He can already win **now**, so yes.\n\nBut what if `n % 3 != 0` → Vanya can win on first move → so he wins.\n\nOnly if `n % 3 == 0` → he cannot win on first move.\n\nAnd as shown, Vova can always respond to prevent it for the full 10 moves.\n\nBut is there a deeper strategy?\n\nSuppose `n = 3` (≡0 mod 3).\n\nVanya: 3 → 4 or 2.\n\nSuppose 4.\n\nVova: 4 → 5 or 3.\n\nShe shouldn’t go back to 3? She can go to 5.\n\nNow Vanya: 5 → 4 or 6.\n\n6 % 3 == 0 → Vanya can go to 6 → and win!\n\nOh! So if Vova naively goes to 5, Vanya can go 5 → 6 → wins.\n\nSo Vova **must not** go to 5.\n\nIf after Vanya: 3 → 4, then Vova **must** go to 3 (back), not to 5.\n\nSimilarly, if Vanya goes 3 → 2, Vova should go back to 3, not to 1.\n\nBecause:\n- 2 → 1: then Vanya can 1 → 0 → 0 % 3 == 0 → win.\n\nSo Vova must return to 3.\n\nSimilarly, she must always return.\n\nSo the key is: **Vova must return to the original value** (or at least avoid progressing).\n\nBut if Vova always returns to `n` (which is divisible by 3), then after 10 moves, Vanya has never had a winning move → Vova wins.\n\nSo **optimal** play for Vova is to undo Vanya’s move.\n\nThen game repeats, no progress, Vanya never gets the number divisible by 3 after his move (except possibly if he moves to multiple of 3, which he can't from multiple of 3), so Vova wins.\n\nBut in our earlier example, if Vova had gone forward (e.g. 4→5), then Vanya could win.\n\nSo optimal for Vova: **always revert**.\n\nTherefore, if `n % 3 == 0`, Vova can force no win for Vanya → Second wins.\n\nIf `n % 3 != 0`, Vanya wins immediately → First wins.\n\nSo the answer is simply:\n\n```python\nif n % 3 == 0:\n    print(\"Second\")\nelse:\n    print(\"First\")\n```\n\nLet’s verify with sample:\n\n- n=1 → 1%3=1 ≠0 → First → correct\n- n=3 → 0 → Second → correct\n- n=5 → 5%3=2 → First → correct\n- n=100 → 100%3 = 1 → First → correct\n- n=999 → 0 → Second → correct\n- n=1000 → 1000%3 = 1 → First → correct\n\nPerfect.\n\nSo the program is simple.\n\n### Final Code:\n\n```python\nt = int(input())\nfor _ in range(t):\n    n = int(input())\n    if n % 3 == 0:\n        print(\"Second\")\n    else:\n        print(\"First\")\n```",
