      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef paintWalls(cost, time):\n    n = len(cost)\n    \n    # dp[i][j] represents minimum cost to paint first i walls with at most j units of time available\n    # We'll use a different approach: dp[j] = minimum cost to achieve at least j units of painting time\n    # from paid painter, which allows free painter to work during that time\n    \n    # Maximum possible time is sum of all times in time array\n    max_time = sum(time)\n    \n    # Initialize dp array with infinity\n    # dp[t] = minimum cost to get at least t units of paid painting time\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (max_time + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0  # Zero cost for zero time\n    \n    # Process each wall\n    for i in range(n):\n        # We need to iterate backwards to avoid using updated values in the same iteration\n        new_dp = dp[:]\n        for t in range(max_time + 1):\n            if dp[t] != float('inf'):\n                # If we use paid painter for wall i\n                new_time = t + time[i]\n                if new_time <= max_time:\n                    new_dp[new_time] = min(new_dp[new_time], dp[t] + cost[i])\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n    \n    # Now find the minimum cost where paid time >= number of walls not painted by paid painter\n    # If paid painter works for 't' units of time, free painter can paint 't' walls\n    # So we need: t >= (n - number of walls painted by paid painter)\n    # But we don't know how many walls paid painter painted, so let's think differently\n    \n    # Actually, let's use a different DP approach\n    # dp[i][t] = minimum cost to paint first i walls with paid painter having worked for t units of time\n    # The free painter can paint min(t, i) walls (limited by time or number of walls)\n    \n    # Let's use a simpler approach: try all combinations with dynamic programming\n    \n    # We'll use state: dp[i][t] = minimum cost to process first i walls \n    # where paid painter has accumulated t units of time\n    \n    dp2 = {}\n    \n    def solve(i, paid_time):\n        if i == n:\n            # All walls processed\n            # We can use free painter for min(paid_time, n - paid_walls_count)\n            # But we don't track paid_walls_count directly\n            # Instead: if paid_time >= number of unpainted walls, we're good\n            # Since free painter can paint min(paid_time, remaining_walls)\n            # remaining_walls = 0 since i == n\n            return 0 if paid_time >= 0 else float('inf')\n        \n        if (i, paid_time) in dp2:\n            return dp2[(i, paid_time)]\n        \n        # Option 1: Use paid painter for wall i\n        option1 = cost[i] + solve(i + 1, paid_time + time[i])\n        \n        # Option 2: Use free painter for wall i\n        # Free painter can only work if paid painter is occupied\n        # So we need paid_time > 0 to use free painter\n        option2 = float('inf')\n        if paid_time > 0:\n            option2 = solve(i + 1, paid_time - 1)\n        \n        result = min(option1, option2)\n        dp2[(i, paid_time)] = result\n        return result\n    \n    return solve(0, 0)\n```",
