ical order from Kahn’s algorithm, we **omit book 1** from output.\n\nBut note: **we must not read book 1 until the end**, but the problem asks **only to list books read before it**.\n\nSo: we generate the full order of all books needed (including 1), then **remove 1** and print the others.\n\nBut: is it possible that book 1 is not last? Yes, but we don’t care — we just don’t print it.\n\nBut the prerequisites of book 1 must come before it — but after removing it, we just output the rest in the order they were processed.\n\nBut: **what if book 1 has in_degree 0?** → but problem says C_1 >= 1 → so book 1 requires at least one book → `in_degree[1] >= 1` → it won’t be first.\n\n---\n\nLet’s test with sample 1:\n\nInput:\n```\n6\n3 2 3 4   → book 1 needs 2,3,4\n2 3 5     → book 2 needs 3,5\n0         → book 3 needs none\n1 5       → book 4 needs 5\n0         → book 5\n0         → book 6\n```\n\nWe want to make book 1 readable.\n\nNeeded set: start from 1 → must have 2,3,4 → to have 2: need 3,5 → to have 4: need 5 → so needed: {1,2,3,4,5}\n\nNow build in_degrees for each in {1,2,3,4,5}:\n\n- 1: prereq = [2,3,4] → all in set → in_degree[1] = 3\n- 2: prereq = [3,5] → both in set → in_degree[2] = 2\n- 3: prereq = [] → 0\n- 4: prereq = [5] → in set → in_degree[4] = 1\n- 5: prereq = [] → 0\n\nin_degree:\n- 1:3, 2:2, 3:0, 4:1, 5:0\n\nQueue: all with in_degree 0 → [3,5]\n\nWe can enqueue in any order (problem says any order is acceptable).\n\nSuppose we use sorted? But problem doesn’t require — we can use arbitrary (like natural order).\n\nBut to pass sample outputs, let’s see.\n\nSample output: `5 3 4 2` or `3 5 4 2` etc.\n\nIn Kahn’s:\n- Start with 3 and 5.\n\nLet’s choose **5 first**, then 3.\n\nProcess 5:\n- Look at all books that depend on 5 → which books have 5 as prerequisite?\n   - From data: book 2 (has 3,5), book 4 (has 5)\n- So when 5 is processed, we reduce in_degree[2] by 1 → becomes 1, in_degree[4] by 1 → becomes 0 → enqueue 4\n\nNow queue: [3,4]\nProcess 3:\n- 3 i