      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the problem:\n\nWe are given an N×N grid of 0s and 1s. We need to **rotate the outer border squares clockwise by one position**, leaving the inner squares (if any) unchanged.\n\n### Steps:\n1. Read the grid.\n2. Extract the values on the **outer ring** in **clockwise order**, starting from (0,0) → (0,1) → ... → (0, N-1) → (1, N-1) → ... → (N-1, N-1) → ... back to (1,0).\n3. Rotate this list **clockwise by one**, meaning each element moves to the next position, and the last element goes to the front.\n4. Put the rotated values back onto the outer ring.\n5. Print the resulting grid.\n\nWe must be careful to handle grids of size N=2 (no inner part) and larger.\n\n---\n\n### Plan:\nWe'll collect the outer ring coordinates in clockwise order:\n\n- Top row: (0, 0) → (0, N-1) [left to right]\n- Right column: (1, N-1) → (N-2, N-1) [top to bottom, excluding corners]\n- Bottom row: (N-1, N-1) → (N-1, 0) [right to left]\n- Left column: (N-2, 0) → (1, 0) [bottom to top, excluding corners]\n\nThen shift the values clockwise (each value moves forward one step, last one wraps to first), and write them back.\n\n---\n\nHere is the implementation:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        grid.append([int(c) for c in data[i].strip()])\n    \n    if n == 1:\n        print(grid[0][0])\n        return\n\n    # Collect outer ring coordinates in clockwise order\n    coords = []\n    \n    # Top: left to right\n    for j in range(n):\n        coords.append((0, j))\n    \n    # Right: top to bottom (excluding top-right corner)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        coords.append((i, n-1))\n    \n    # Bottom: right to left (excluding bottom-right corner)\n    for j in range(n-2, -1, -1):\n        coords.append((n-1, j))\n    \n    # Left: bottom to top (excluding bottom-left and top-left corners)\n    for i in range(n-2, 0, -1):\n        coords.append((i, 0))\n    \n    # Now collect values from original grid on outer ring\n    values = [grid[i][j] for i, j in coords]\n    \n    # Rotate clockwise by one: last element becomes first\n    rotated = [values[-1]] + values[:-1]\n    \n    # Put back rotated values\n    for (i, j), val in zip(coords, rotated):\n        grid[i][j] = val\n    \n    # Print the grid\n    for row in grid:\n        print(''.join(map(str, row)))\n\nsolve()\n```",
