      "generated_answer": "We need to determine whether we can **exactly** cover an `H x W` grid using **some subset** of `N` rectangular tiles (each of size `A_i x B_i`), possibly rotating (flipping) them, without overlap and without going outside the grid.\n\n### Key Observations:\n- Each tile can be placed as `(a, b)` or `(b, a)` (due to rotation).\n- We can only use **each tile once**.\n- We **don't have to use all tiles** — only a subset.\n- The **total area** covered must be exactly `H * W`.\n- Since `N <= 7` and `H, W <= 10`, we can use **backtracking (DFS)** to try placing tiles in the grid.\n\nApproach:\n1. Read input.\n2. Use a recursive backtracking algorithm:\n   - Represent the grid as a 2D boolean (or integer) array to track which cells are filled.\n   - Find the first unfilled cell.\n   - Try every unused tile (in both orientations) that fits at that position.\n   - Mark the area as occupied, recurse.\n   - Backtrack if needed.\n3. If we succeed in filling the entire grid, output `\"Yes\"`, else `\"No\"`.\n\nWe will now implement this strategy.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    def can_place(grid, r, c, h, w):\n        if r + h > H or c + w > W:\n            return False\n        for i in range(h):\n            for j in range(w):\n                if grid[r+i][c+j]:\n                    return False\n        return True\n\n    def place(grid, r, c, h, w, marker):\n        for i in range(h):\n            for j in range(w):\n                grid[r+i][c+j] = marker\n\n    def dfs(grid, tiles):\n        if all(grid[i][j] for i in range(H) for j in range(W)):\n            return True\n        # Find the first empty cell\n        r, c = -1, -1\n        for i in range(H):\n            for j in range(W):\n                if not grid[i][j]:\n                    r, c = i, j\n                    break\n            if r != -1:\n                break\n        # Try every tile\n        for idx in range(len(tiles)):\n            if tiles[idx] is None:\n                continue\n            a, b = tiles[idx]\n            # Remove tile\n            tile = tiles[idx]\n            tiles[idx] = None\n            # Try both orientations\n            orientations = [(a, b)]\n            if a != b:\n                orientations.append((b, a))\n            for h, w in orientations:\n                if can_place(grid, r, c, h, w):\n                    place(grid, r, c, h, w, True)\n                    if dfs(grid, tiles):\n                        tiles[idx] = tile  # restore\n                        return True\n                    place(grid, r, c, h, w, False)  # backtrack\n            # backtrack tile\n            tiles[idx] = tile\n        return False\n\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n\n    n = int(data[0])\n    H = int(data[1])\n    W = int(data[2])\n    tiles = []\n    index = 3\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[index]); b = int(data[index+1]); index += 2\n        tiles.append((a, b))\n\n    # The total area of used tiles must equal H*W\n    total_area = H * W\n    # Check if any subset of tiles has total area = total_area\n    # But we also need to pack them, so we try DFS\n    grid = [[False]*W for _ in range(H)]\n    if dfs(grid, tiles):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- `can_place`: checks if a tile of size `h x w` can be placed at `(r, c)`.\n- `place`: marks/unmarks the grid region.\n- `dfs`: recursively tries to fill the grid starting from the first unfilled cell.\n- We try each unused tile in both orientations.\n\n### Why This Works:\n- `N <= 7` — small number of tiles.\n- Backtracking over placements is feasible.\n- We are only placing tiles where needed, and using rotation.\n\n### Sample Validation:\n- **Sample 1**: 5x5 = 25. Tiles: 1, 9, 16, 6, 10 → Use 9 (3x3) + 6 (2x3) + 10 (2x5) = 25 → Possible → \"Yes\".\n- **Sample 2**: 1x2 = 2. Tile: 2x3 = 6 → even if rotated,