    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array nums and an integer target.\nA 0-indexed array infinite_nums is generated by infinitely appending the elements of nums to itself.\nReturn the length of the shortest subarray of the array infinite_nums with a sum equal to target. If there is no such subarray return -1.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3], target = 5\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [1,2,3,1,2,3,1,2,...].\nThe subarray in the range [1,2], has the sum equal to target = 5 and length = 2.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the shortest length of a subarray with sum equal to target = 5.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,1,1,2,3], target = 4\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [1,1,1,2,3,1,1,1,2,3,1,1,...].\nThe subarray in the range [4,5], has the sum equal to target = 4 and length = 2.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the shortest length of a subarray with sum equal to target = 4.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [2,4,6,8], target = 3\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: In this example infinite_nums = [2,4,6,8,2,4,6,8,...].\nIt can be proven that there is no subarray with sum equal to target = 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5\n1 <= target <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the shortest subarray in the infinitely repeated array that sums to target.\n\nKey insights:\n1. If target is very large, the optimal solution might involve taking entire copies of the original array\n2. I can use a sliding window approach on a \"doubled\" version of the array to simulate the circular/infinite nature\n3. The maximum length I need to consider is at most 2 * len(nums) because if a solution wraps around, it can be found in two copies\n\nMy approach:\n1. First, handle the case where target is a multiple of the total sum - I might need complete repetitions\n2. Use a sliding window on two concatenated copies of nums to find subarrays that sum to the remainder\n3. Consider combinations of complete repetitions plus a partial subarray\n\n```python\ndef minSizeSubarray(nums, target):\n    n = len(nums)\n    total_sum = sum(nums)\n    \n    # If target is larger than total_sum, we might need complete repetitions\n    full_cycles = target // total_sum\n    remainder = target % total_sum\n    \n    # If remainder is 0, we just need full_cycles * n\n    if remainder == 0:\n        return full_cycles * n\n    \n    # Now we need to find the shortest subarray that sums to remainder\n    # We'll use sliding window on two copies of nums\n    double_nums = nums + nums\n    min_length = float('inf')\n    \n    # Sliding window to find subarray with sum = remainder\n    left = 0\n    current_sum = 0\n    \n    for right in range(len(double_nums)):\n        current_sum += double_nums[right]\n        \n        while current_sum > remainder and left <= right:\n            current_sum -= double_nums[left]\n            left += 1\n            \n        if current_sum == remainder:\n            min_length = min(min_length, right - left + 1)\n    \n    # If we couldn't find a subarray for the remainder\n    if min_length == float('inf'):\n        return -1\n    \n    # Total length is full_cycles * n + length of subarray for remainder\n    return full_cycles * n + min_length\n```",
