less we have some constraint\n            # However, looking at the problem again, we need to maximize servings\n            \n            # Actually, in real context, if a dish costs nothing, we could make infinite servings\n            # But the sample constraints and expected answers suggest we need to be more careful\n            \n            # Let's go back to our original approach but handle zero coefficients properly\n            pass\n    \n    # We'll use a reasonable upper bound for servings\n    # Maximum servings of either dish cannot exceed the maximum available ingredient divided by minimum non-zero requirement\n    max_possible_servings = 0\n    for i in range(N):\n        if A[i] > 0:\n            max_possible_servings = max(max_possible_servings, Q[i] // A[i])\n        if B[i] > 0:\n            max_possible_servings = max(max_possible_servings, Q[i] // B[i])\n    \n    # Add some buffer\n    max_possible_servings = min(max_possible_servings + 10, 10**6)\n    \n    # Try all possible servings of dish A from 0 to max_possible_servings\n    for servings_A in range(max_possible_servings + 1):\n        # Check if we can make 'servings_A' of dish A\n        valid_A = True\n        remaining_Q = Q[:]\n        \n        for i in range(N):\n            if A[i] * servings_A > Q[i]:\n                valid_A = False\n                break\n            remaining_Q[i] = Q[i] - A[i] * servings_A\n        \n        if not valid_A:\n            break\n            \n        # Calculate maximum servings of dish B possible with remaining ingredients\n        servings_B = float('inf')\n        for i in range(N):\n            if B[i] > 0:\n                servings_B = min(servings_B, remaining_Q[i] // B[i])\n        \n        if servings_B == float('inf'):\n            # This means B[i] = 0 for all i, so we can make unlimited B\n            # But combined with the fact that we can make A servings, we need to check\n            # Actually, if B[i] = 0 for all i and we can make dish B, then we can make infinite B\n            # But this would mean infinite total servings, which isn't practical\n            # Looking at constraints, there must be at least one i such that B[i] >= 1\n            # So this case shouldn't happen\n            servings_B = 0\n            \n        total_servings = servings_A + servings_B\n        max_servings = max(max_servings, total_servings)\n    \n    print(max_servings)\n\nsolve()\n```",
