      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the number of integer points that are covered by at least one car, where each car occupies a continuous range from start_i to end_i (inclusive).\n\nThis is a classic interval merging problem. The approach is:\n1. Sort the intervals by their start points\n2. Merge overlapping or adjacent intervals\n3. Sum up the length of all merged intervals\n\nTwo intervals [a,b] and [c,d] overlap or are adjacent if b >= c-1 (since we're dealing with integer points).\n\n```python\ndef solution(nums):\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Sort intervals by start point\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    # Merge overlapping intervals\n    merged = []\n    for start, end in nums:\n        # If merged is empty or current interval doesn't overlap with the last merged interval\n        if not merged or merged[-1][1] < start:\n            merged.append([start, end])\n        else:\n            # Merge with the last interval by extending the end point\n            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)\n    \n    # Calculate total covered points\n    total_points = 0\n    for start, end in merged:\n        total_points += end - start + 1\n    \n    return total_points\n```",
