less of team labels – by forcing new teams to be assigned the next integer, we avoid permuting labels.\n\nYes, this is standard.\n\nNow DFS termination:\n\nWhen i == N+1:\n- if max_team_used == T → valid, return 1\n- else: return 0\n\nAt each step i (current player i to assign), try:\n\n- for each team t in 1 .. max_team_used:\n   if no player j (assigned to team t) such that `bad[i][j]` is True → safe\n- if max_team_used < T: try team t = max_team_used + 1 → always safe (no members initially)\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nNote: players are numbered 1 to N.\n\nWe'll index teams from 1.\n\nInitialize: assign player 1 to team 1.\n\nBut we can do DFS starting at i=2, with team[1]=1, max_team=1.\n\nBut to make it clean, start at i=1, and if i=1, only option is team 1.\n\nAlternatively, in the loop, if i==1: force team 1.\n\nBut we can handle it:\n\n```python\ndef dfs(i, teams, max_team):\n    if i > N:\n        return 1 if max_team == T else 0\n    total = 0\n    # try existing teams 1 to max_team\n    for t in range(1, max_team+1):\n        # check if player i can join team t\n        conflict = False\n        for j in range(1, i):  # players already assigned\n            if teams[j] == t and bad[i][j]:\n                conflict = True\n                break\n        if not conflict:\n            teams[i] = t\n            total += dfs(i+1, teams, max_team)\n    # try new team\n    new_team = max_team + 1\n    if new_team <= T:\n        teams[i] = new_team\n        total += dfs(i+1, teams, new_team)\n    return total\n```\n\nBut we must initialize `teams` as list of zeros of length N+1.\n\nCall: `dfs(1, teams, 0)` → but at i=1, we try teams 1 to 0 → no existing, then try new_team=1 if <=T.\n\nSo it will work.\n\nBut at i=1: max_team=0 → no loop over existing teams → then try new_team=1 → assign.\n\nSo okay.\n\nBut we can optimize: at i=1, we must assign to team 1.\n\nAlternatively, it naturally assigns to new_team=1.\n\nSo we're good.\n\nNow, run the sample:\n\nSample 1