n of first i players? But we only need to pass team assignments, which we do via mutable list.\n\nWe’re not memoizing, but N=10, and the recursion tree is about the number of set partitions up to 10, which is around 10^5 worst, acceptable.\n\nBut in worst-case, T=10, N=10: S(10,10)=1, so it's fine.\n\nAnother: T=5, N=10: S(10,5)=42525, and we do checks at each node — but we traverse each full partition once, and each internal node corresponds to partial partition.\n\nThe total number of recursive calls is the number of partial set partitions (prefixes), which is manageable.\n\nSo we’re good.\n\nLet’s run on the provided examples:\n\nSample 4: 8001 → we’ll see.\n\nWe’ll write the code as described.\n\nFinal code:\n\nNote: we used `sys.setrecursionlimit` just in case, but depth is at most N+1=11, so not needed.\n\nBut keep it.\n\nNow, print the code.\n\nBut the problem says: \"Generate an executable Python function... Simply call the function after the definition.\"\n\nSo we don't need `main()` wrapper? Actually, we can define `main` and call it.\n\nOr just write the code.\n\nWe'll write a function? Or just a script.\n\nThe question says: \"generate an executable Python function\" — but then \"call the function\".\n\nBut we can do:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().splitlines()\n    if not data:\n        print(0)\n        return\n    # Parse first line\n    N, T, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    \n    # Incompatibility matrix\n    bad = [[False] * (N+1) for _ in range(N+1)]\n    \n    for i in range(1, M+1):\n        a, b = map(int, data[i].split())\n        bad[a][b] = True\n        bad[b][a] = True\n    \n    # teams[i] for player i\n    team_assignment = [0] * (N+1)  # 1-indexed, 0 unused\n    total_ways = 0\n    \n    def dfs(player, max_used_team):\n        nonlocal total_ways\n        if player > N:\n            if max_used_team == T:\n                total_ways += 1\n            return\n        \n        # Try joining an existing team (1 to max_used_team)\n        for t in range(1, max_used_team + 1):\n            # Check conflict with any player already in team t\n            conflict = False\n            for other in range(1, player):\n                if team_assignment[other] == t and bad[player][other]:\n                    conflict = True\n                    break\n            if not conflict:\n                team_assignment[player] = t\n                dfs(player + 1, max_used_team)\n        \n        # Try a new team\n        new_team = max_used_team + 1\n        if new_team <= T:\n            team_assignment[player] = new_team\n            dfs(player + 1, new_team)\n    \n    dfs(1, 0)\n    print(total_ways)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nBut using `nonlocal` might be less clear. Instead, we can return count as before.\n\nBetter to return value.\n\nRevised:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not data:\n        print(0)\n        return\n    N, T, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    \n    # Build bad matrix\n    bad = [[False] * (N+1) for _ in range(N+1)]\n    for i in range(M):\n        a, b = map(int, data[1+i].split())\n        bad[a][b] = True\n        bad[b][a] = True\n    \n    teams = [0] * (N+1)  # team of player i\n    \n    def dfs(i, max_team):\n        if i == N + 1:\n            return 1 if max_team == T else 0\n        count = 0\n        # Try existing teams 1 to max_team\n        for t in range(1, max_team + 1):\n            ok = True\n            for j in range(1, i):\n                if teams[j] == t and bad[i][j]:\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n            if ok:\n                teams[i] = t\n                count += dfs(i + 1, max_team)\n        # Try new team\n        new_t = max_team + 1\n        if new_t <= T:\n            teams[i] = new_t\n            count += dfs(i + 1, new_t)\n        return count\n    \n    result = dfs(1, 0)\n    print(result)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nThis should work.\n\nLet’s hope.\n\nBut sample 1: N=5, T=2, M=2, pairs (1,3) and (3,4)\n\nWe’ll get 4.\n\nSample 2: T=1, so must put all in one team → 1 and 3 together → conflict → 0.\n\nSample 3: no constraints, S(6,4)=65.\n\nSample 4: 8001 → must be correct.\n\nSo we output the code.\n\nNote: the `range(1, M+1)` in data: lines 1 to M are data[1:1+M]\n\nYes.\n\nFinal code:",
