    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum number of coins to add so that all integers from 1 to target are obtainable as subsequences.\n\nThe key insight is to use a greedy approach. I'll track the maximum value I can obtain so far (let's call it `max_obtainable`), and for each value from 1 to target, I'll ensure I can reach it.\n\nThe strategy:\n1. Sort the coins to process them in order\n2. Keep track of the maximum value that can be obtained with the current coins\n3. When there's a gap (max_obtainable + 1 can't be reached), add a coin of value (max_obtainable + 1)\n4. When I add such a coin, the new max_obtainable becomes max_obtainable + (max_obtainable + 1) = 2 * max_obtainable + 1\n\nThis works because if I can make all values from 1 to max_obtainable, and I add a coin of value (max_obtainable + 1), then I can make all values from 1 to (2 * max_obtainable + 1) by either:\n- Using the existing coins (values 1 to max_obtainable)\n- Using the new coin plus some combination of existing coins (values (max_obtainable + 1) to (2 * max_obtainable + 1))\n\n```python\ndef minimumAddedCoins(coins, target):\n    coins.sort()\n    max_obtainable = 0\n    added_coins = 0\n    coin_idx = 0\n    \n    while max_obtainable < target:\n        # If we have coins that are small enough to extend our range\n        if coin_idx < len(coins) and coins[coin_idx] <= max_obtainable + 1:\n            max_obtainable += coins[coin_idx]\n            coin_idx += 1\n        else:\n            # Need to add a coin of value (max_obtainable + 1)\n            added_coins += 1\n            max_obtainable += max_obtainable + 1\n    \n    return added_coins\n```",
