tion j >= i\n- Find max in subarray nums[i:j+1]\n- Check if nums[i] == max and nums[j] == max\n\nBut this is O(n³) and with n up to 10^5, it will time out.\n\nBetter approach:\n- For each i, I can iterate j from i to n-1, keeping track of current max\n- If current max equals nums[i] and current max equals nums[j], increment counter\n\nThis is O(n²) which might still be too slow for 10^5.\n\nLet me optimize further. Key insight: In a valid subarray, the first and last elements must equal the maximum. So if I fix the maximum value, I can find all positions with that value and count valid pairs.\n\nActually, let me implement the O(n²) solution first and see if it passes:\n\n```python\ndef solution(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    count = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        current_max = nums[i]\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            current_max = max(current_max, nums[j])\n            if nums[i] == current_max and nums[j] == current_max:\n                count += 1\n                \n    return count\n```",
