    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_142",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of positive integers nums and a positive integer limit.\nIn one operation, you can choose any two indices i and j and swap nums[i] and nums[j] if |nums[i] - nums[j]| <= limit.\nReturn the lexicographically smallest array that can be obtained by performing the operation any number of times.\nAn array a is lexicographically smaller than an array b if in the first position where a and b differ, array a has an element that is less than the corresponding element in b. For example, the array [2,10,3] is lexicographically smaller than the array [10,2,3] because they differ at index 0 and 2 < 10.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,5,3,9,8], limit = 2\nOutput: [1,3,5,8,9]\nExplanation: Apply the operation 2 times:\n- Swap nums[1] with nums[2]. The array becomes [1,3,5,9,8]\n- Swap nums[3] with nums[4]. The array becomes [1,3,5,8,9]\nWe cannot obtain a lexicographically smaller array by applying any more operations.\nNote that it may be possible to get the same result by doing different operations.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,7,6,18,2,1], limit = 3\nOutput: [1,6,7,18,1,2]\nExplanation: Apply the operation 3 times:\n- Swap nums[1] with nums[2]. The array becomes [1,6,7,18,2,1]\n- Swap nums[0] with nums[4]. The array becomes [2,6,7,18,1,1]\n- Swap nums[0] with nums[5]. The array becomes [1,6,7,18,1,2]\nWe cannot obtain a lexicographically smaller array by applying any more operations.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [1,7,28,19,10], limit = 3\nOutput: [1,7,28,19,10]\nExplanation: [1,7,28,19,10] is the lexicographically smallest array we can obtain because we cannot apply the operation on any two indices.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n1 <= limit <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the lexicographically smallest array by swapping elements when their absolute difference is <= limit.\n\nThe key insight is that if two elements can be swapped (|a-b| <= limit), and a third element can be swapped with one of them, then all three elements are in the same \"connected component\" and can be rearranged among themselves. Elements within a connected component can be sorted to achieve the lexicographically smallest arrangement.\n\nTwo elements belong to the same component if there's a chain of swaps connecting them, where each consecutive pair has difference <= limit.\n\nHere's my approach:\n1. Sort the array while keeping track of original indices\n2. Group elements into components where elements in the same component can reach each other through a chain of valid swaps\n3. Within each component, arrange elements in ascending order at their original positions to get the lexicographically smallest result\n\n```python\ndef lexicographicallySmallestArray(nums, limit):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 1:\n        return nums[:]\n    \n    # Create array of (value, original_index) pairs and sort by value\n    indexed_nums = [(nums[i], i) for i in range(n)]\n    indexed_nums.sort()\n    \n    # Group indices into components\n    components = []\n    current_component = [indexed_nums[0][1]]  # Store original indices\n    \n    for i in range(1, n):\n        curr_val, curr_idx = indexed_nums[i]\n        prev_val, _ = indexed_nums[i-1]\n        \n        # If current element can be swapped with previous element, they're in same component\n        if curr_val - prev_val <= limit:\n            current_component.append(curr_idx)\n        else:\n            # Start new component\n            components.append(current_component)\n            current_component = [curr_idx]\n    \n    # Add the last component\n    components.append(current_component)\n    \n    # Create result array\n    result = [0] * n\n    \n    # For each component, sort the original indices and assign sorted values\n    for component in components:\n        # Get the values at these positions in the original array\n        values = [nums[i] for i in component]\n        values.sort()\n        \n        # Sort the component indices to place smaller values at smaller indices\n        component.sort()\n        \n        # Assign sorted values to sorted positions\n        for idx, val in zip(component, values):\n            result[idx] = val\n    \n    return result\n```",
