    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of alternating subarrays in a binary array.\n\nAn alternating subarray has no two adjacent elements with the same value. Single elements are always alternating.\n\nMy approach:\n1. For each position, I'll extend the current alternating subarray as long as the alternating property holds\n2. When I find an alternating subarray of length `k`, it contributes `k * (k + 1) / 2` subarrays (all possible contiguous subarrays within it)\n3. I need to be careful about overlapping - better to use a sliding window approach\n\nActually, let me think differently. I can use a dynamic programming approach where I track the length of the current alternating subarray ending at each position.\n\nIf the current element is different from the previous one, I can extend the previous alternating subarray. Otherwise, I start a new one of length 1.\n\nFor each position, the number of alternating subarrays ending at that position equals the length of the current alternating subarray.\n\n```python\ndef countAlternatingSubarrays(nums):\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n    \n    total = 0\n    current_length = 1  # Every single element forms an alternating subarray\n    \n    # First element contributes 1 subarray\n    total += 1\n    \n    # Process from second element onwards\n    for i in range(1, len(nums)):\n        if nums[i] != nums[i-1]:\n            # Can extend the previous alternating subarray\n            current_length += 1\n        else:\n            # Same as previous, start fresh\n            current_length = 1\n        \n        # Add the number of alternating subarrays ending at position i\n        total += current_length\n    \n    return total\n```",
