ribution).\n\nActually, let me think differently. If we have `k` groups, then the size of each group will be either `s` or `s+1` for some `s`, and we need to check if we can assign the frequencies to achieve this.\n\nBut the constraint is that indices with the same value must be together in groups, but a value can span multiple groups.\n\nFor a value with frequency `f`, if we distribute it across some of the `k` groups, those groups will contain some number of this value. The constraint is that within a group, all values must be the same.\n\nSo each value with frequency `f` needs to be partitioned into some number of groups, each containing only that value.\n\nIf we have `k` total groups, and we want group sizes to differ by at most 1, then group sizes are either `floor(total/k)` or `ceil(total/k)`.\n\nFor each value with frequency `f`, we need to determine if it can be partitioned into parts where each part goes to a different group, and the part sizes are constrained by the final group sizes.\n\nLet me implement a solution:\n\n```python\ndef minGroupsForValidAssignment(nums):\n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    # Count frequency of each value\n    freq_counter = Counter(nums)\n    frequencies = list(freq_counter.values())\n    \n    total_elements = len(nums)\n    \n    # Try different numbers of groups\n    # The answer must be at least 1 and at most total_elements\n    # We want the minimum number of groups\n    \n    for k in range(1, total_elements + 1):\n        # For k groups, ideal sizes are either s or s+1 where s = total_elements // k\n        s = total_elements // k\n        remainder = total_elements % k\n        # remainder groups will have size s+1, k - remainder groups will have size s\n        \n        # Check if we can distribute all frequencies into k groups\n        # such that no group exceeds the max size of s+1\n        # and we use exactly the right number of groups of each size\n        \n        # We'll try to be greedy: for each frequency, we need to split it\n        # into chunks to fill groups. Each chunk in a group can be at most s+1,\n        # but since a group can only have one value, the chunk size in a group is limited\n        \n        # Actually, let's think: for k groups, we have `remainder` groups of size s+1\n        # and `k - remainder` groups of size s.\n        \n        # For each frequency f, we need to assign it to cover some number of group-slots\n        # We want to minimize the number of groups used or check feasibility\n        \n        # For a frequency f, the minimum number of groups needed is ceil(f / (s+1))\n        # because each group can take at most s+1 elements of the same value\n        \n        min_groups_needed = 0\n        for f in frequencies:\n            min_groups_needed += (f + s) // (s + 1)  # This is ceil(f / (s+1))\n        \n        if min_groups_needed <= k:\n            return k\n    \n    return total_elements\n```",
