n merge **subset of sets**, and leave others aside.\n\nThe problem says: \"you may perform the operation any number of times\", and each time you replace two sets with their union. We don’t have to merge **all** into one. We just want **one set** at the end (among possibly others) that contains both 1 and 8.\n\nSo we want to merge **a subset** of the sets such that:\n- The subset is connected (directly or indirectly via overlaps)\n- Their union contains both 1 and 8\n- And we want to **minimize the number of operations**, which for merging k sets is k-1\n\nBut we can **choose which sets to merge**, as long as their merging sequence is valid (i.e., only merge overlapping sets).\n\nSo we want the **smallest k** such that there exists a connected group of **k sets** that together contain both 1 and 8, where \"connected\" means the sets can be merged via overlapping steps.\n\nBut note: in union-find approach, we connected sets that are in same component via shared values — but if sets are in same component, it means they can be merged via a sequence — so **any subset of a connected component cannot necessarily be merged in isolation** unless the subset by itself is also connected.\n\nBut here’s a better idea:\n\nWe can think of this as a **graph problem on sets**, but instead of grouping all sets in a component, we want the **smallest connected subcollection** of sets that:\n\n- Contains at least one set with element 1\n- Contains at least one set with element 8\n- All sets in the group can be merged into one set (so they form a connected subgraph in the set intersection graph)\n\nBut the **set intersection graph** is huge: N up to 200,000 → we can't build adjacency list of sets.\n\nAlternative idea: use **elements as nodes** and **sets as hyperedges**, and we want the minimum number of hyperedges to connect node 1 and node M.\n\nAh! This is key.\n\nWe can model this as:\n\n- The integers from 1 to M are **nodes**.\n- Each set is a **hyperedge** connecting all the elements in it.\n- Two elements are connected if they are in the same set (directly), and we can propagate through multiple sets.\n- So the **universe** is the graph where elements are connected if in same set, and we take transitive closure over sets as hyperedges.\n\nBut we want: can we **connect element 1 and element M** via a chain of sets?\n\nBut more than that: we also want to **count** the **minimum number of sets** needed to connect 1 and M in this hypergraph, because:\n- If we use `k` sets that together make 1 and M in the same connected component (of elements), then we can merge those `k` sets into one (if they are \"connected\"?), but are they?\n\nWait — consider: we can merge sets if they share an element. So to merge multiple sets, they must be connected via shared elements.\n\nSo if we think of **sets as nodes**, and connect two sets if they share an element, then a group of sets can be merged into one if they are in the same **connected component in the set graph**.\n\nBut we don't want the whole component — we want the **smallest number of sets** we can use to connect element 1 and element M.\n\nBut — in order for the resulting union to contain both 1 and M, and be a single set, we must merge a collection of sets that:\n- One contains 1\n- One contains M\n- And the entire collection is **connected** (via shared elements) so they can be merged.\n\nWe want to **minimize** the number of operations = number_of_sets_merged - 1.\n\nSo we want the **minimum number of sets** that can be connected (in the set graph) such that their element union contains both 1 and M.\n\nBut: the sets must be **connected as a subgraph** in the intersection graph (two sets connected if they share an element).\n\nThis is equivalent to: find the **smallest connected subgraph** (in the set intersection graph) that includes at least one set with 1 and one set with M.\n\nBut \"connected subgraph\" and minimizing number of sets → **shortest path between two sets (one containing 1, one containing M)**, where each edge is between sets that intersect.\n\nBut then:\n- We can build a graph:\n  - Nodes: sets (0 to N-1)\n  - For every element, all sets containing it form a clique.\n- But adding edge for every pair in clique is too expensive (e.g., if 1000 sets contain element 1, that's ~1e6 edges).\n\nInstead, use **common trick**: **use element-nodes as intermediaries**.\n\nIntroduce **two types of nodes**: set nodes and element nodes.\n\nBuild a **bipartite graph**:\n- Left side: sets (0 to N-1)\n- Right side: elements (1 to M)\n- An edge between set i and element x if x ∈ S_i\n\nNow, a path from set A to set B is: A — x — B, meaning they share x.\n\nBut more importantly: a **path from a set containing 1 to a set containing M**?\n\nActually, we want: **shortest path (in terms of number of sets used)** from a set containing 1 to a set containing M?\n\nWait, but we can merge multiple sets. We want the **minimum number of sets** to include so that:\n- They form a **connected** subgraph in the set intersection graph\n- And one has 1, one has M\n\nThat is equivalent to: **shortest path in the set graph** between a set containing 1 and a set containing M.\n\nBut with the bipartite trick, we can do a **BFS in the bipartite graph** to compute shortest path between two elements? Or between two sets?\n\nLet’s define:\n\nWe want the **minimum number of sets** needed along a path that connects element 1 to element M.\n\nActually, consider:\n\nWe can move:\n- From an element to any set that contains it,\n- From a set to any element in it.\n\nSo to go from element 1 to element M:\n- Start at element 1\n- Go to a set that contains 1\n- Then go to an element in that set\n- Then to a set containing that element, etc.\n- Until we reach element M.\n\nThe **number of sets used** in such a path is the number of set-nodes we pass.\n\nWe want the **shortest path (in number of set nodes)** from element 1 to element M.\n\nBut note: each \"use\" of a set counts as 1, and we can use a set only once? But we can use same set multiple times? No — we don't want cycles.\n\nBut we are only counting path, so we can avoid revisiting.\n\nSo do BFS on the bipartite graph from element 1 to element M, where:\n- Nodes: either element or set\n- Distance: number of **set nodes** visited in the path\n\nBut the path: `1 (element) -> set_i -> element_x -> set_j -> ... -> M`\n\nThe number of sets on the path is what we care about.\n\nBut each set is used only once. And we want the **minimum number of sets** needed to connect 1 and M.\n\nYes: so the **shortest path in the bipartite graph from element 1 to element M**, counting **only the number of set nodes** on the path.\n\nBut in BFS, each step from element to set or set to element.\n\nSo we can do:\n\nLet dist[node] be the number of **sets used so far** in the path to reach this node.\n\nBut when we go from element to set: if going to a new set, then increment count?\n\nActually, **each time we enter a set node from an element, that counts as using the set**.\n\nSo in BFS:\n\n- Start from element 1, initialize dist[1] = 0 (it's an element)\n- Then from element 1, go to all sets that contain it → for each such set, dist[set] = dist[el] + 1\n- Then from a set, go to all elements in it → dist[el in set] = dist[set] (no extra cost)\n- We want dist[M] (when we reach element M)\n\nBut we never use a node twice.\n\nWe do BFS over both types of nodes.\n\nWe'll maintain:\n- A queue: (node_type, node_id, distance)\n- Two arrays: dist_element[1..M], dist_set[0..N-1], initialized to -1 or INF\n\nThen:\n- Enqueue element 1 with dist 0.\n- Then:\n  - From an element `e`, for every set `s` that contains `e`, if we haven't visited `s`, set dist_set[s] = dist_element[e] + 1, and enqueue s.\n  - From a set `s`, for every element `e` in `s`, if we haven't visited `e`, set dist_element[e] = dist_set[s], and enqueue e.\n\nStop when we reach element M.\n\nThen, if we reach element M, the distance is `dist_element[M]`, which is the **number of sets used** in the path.\n\nThen, the number of **operations** is: `(number of sets used) - 1`\n\nBut wait: we used `k` sets in the path, and we need `k - 1` operations to merge them into one.\n\nBut is it guaranteed that these `k` sets can be merged? Yes: because they are connected by shared elements along the path.\n\nFor example:\n- Set1 contains 1 and x\n- Set2 contains x and y\n- Set3 contains y and M\nThen path: 1 → S1 → x → S2 → y → S3 → M\nNumber of sets = 3, operations = 2.\n\nBut can we merge S1, S2, S3?\n\n- S1 and S2 share x → merge to S12\n- S12 and S3 share y → merge → final set contains 1 and M.\n\nYes.\n\nBut what if one set contains both 1 and M? Then path: 1 → S0 → M → distance = 1 (one set), operations = 0 → correct.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Read input.\n2. Build:\n   - For each set i, store its elements.\n   - For each element x, store the list of sets that contain x.\n3. Use BFS:\n   - State: we have two kinds: element and set.\n   - We maintain:\n     - `dist_elements` array of size (M+1), initialize -1\n     - `dist_sets` array of size N, initialize -1\n4. Queue (collections.deque), start: element 1, dist 0.\n5. Set `dist_elements[1] = 0`.\n6. While queue:\n   - Pop node.\n   - If it's an element `e`:\n     - For each set `s` that contains `e`:\n       - If `dist_sets[s] == -1`, then `dist_sets[s] = dist_elements[e] + 1`, push set `s`.\n   - If it's a set `s`:\n     - For each `e` in `S_s`:\n       - If `dist_elements[e] == -1`, then `dist_elements[e] = dist_sets[s]`, push `e`.\n7. If `dist_elements[M] == -1`, print -1.\n   Else, number of sets used = `dist_elements[M]`, operations = `dist_elements[M] - 1`.\n\nBut wait: `dist_elements[M]` is the number of sets used to reach M? Yes.\n\nBut in the path of 1 → S0 → M, then:\n- dist_elements[1] = 0\n- from 1, go to S0: dist_sets[S0] = 1\n- from S0, go to M: dist_elements[M] = 1\n- then operations = 1 - 1 = 0 → correct.\n\nIn the 3-set path: 1 → S1 → x → S2 → y → S3 → M\n- dist[1] = 0\n- S1: 1\n- x: 1 (from S1)\n- S2: 2\n- y: 2\n- S3: 3\n- M: 3\n- operations = 3-1 = 2 → but the distance to M is 3? That can't be.\n\nWait: from S1, we get x → then x leads to S2 → S2 gets distance = dist[x] + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2\nThen S2 → y → y: dist = 2\nThen y → S3: dist = 2+1=3\nS3 → M: dist = 3\nThen operations = 3-1=2 → correct.\n\nBut the **number of sets used is 3**, so operations = 2. But we got dist_elements[M] = 3? That’s not the number of sets.\n\nWait: in the BFS, when we go from x to S2, we set `dist_sets[S2] = dist_elements[x] + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2`\n\nBut we are using the **same** unit for both? But the count is the number of sets used.\n\nSo yes, `dist_elements[M]` is the number of sets used along the path.\n\nBut then we need to subtract 1.\n\nBut wait: in the one-set case: dist_elements[M] = 1 → 1-1=0 → correct.\n\nSo answer is: `dist_elements[M] - 1` if `dist_elements[M] > 0`, else?\n\nBut what if M == 1? But constraint says M>=2, so 1 != M.\n\nBut what if set contains both 1 and we start at 1, so we reach M via one set → so yes.\n\nBut is there a possibility that we reach M without going through any set? No.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        print(-1)\n        return\n\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n\n    sets = []  # list of sets\n    for i in range(N):\n        a = int(next(it))\n        s = []\n        for j in range(a):\n            s.append(int(next(it)))\n        sets.append(set(s))\n\n    # Precompute: for each element x in [1, M], list of sets that contain it\n    element_to_sets = [[] for _ in range(M+1)]  # index 1..M\n    for i in range(N):\n        for x in sets[i]:\n            if 1 <= x <= M:\n                element_to_sets[x].append(i)\n\n    # BFS in bipartite graph: nodes are elements (1..M) and sets (0..N-1)\n    dist_element = [-1] * (M+1)   # dist_element[x] = number of sets used to reach x\n    dist_set = [-1] * N           # dist_set[i] = number of sets used to reach set i\n\n    q = deque()\n\n    # Start from element 1\n    dist_element[1] = 0\n    q.append(('element', 1))\n\n    while q:\n        typ, id = q.popleft()\n        if typ == 'element':\n            e = id\n            d = dist_element[e]\n            # Go to every set that contains e\n            for s_index in element_to_sets[e]:\n                if dist_set[s_index] == -1:\n                    dist_set[s_index] = d + 1\n                    q.append(('set', s grinding)\n                    q.append(('set', s_index))\n        else:  # typ == 'set'\n            s_index = id\n            d = dist_set[s_index]\n            # Go to every element in this set\n            for e in sets[s_index]:\n                if dist_element[e] == -1:\n                    dist_element[e] = d\n                    q.append(('element', e))\n\n    if dist_element[M] == -1:\n        print(-1)\n    else:\n        # number of operations = number of sets used - 1\n        print(dist_element[M] - 1)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nWait: one typo: `q.append(('set', s grinding)` → cut off.\n\nFixed.\n\nLet’s test on sample 1:\n\n- Element 1: goes to set0 → set0: dist[0] = 1\n- Set0: has {1,2} → 1 already visited, 2 → dist_element[2] = 1 → add element 2\n- From element 2: goes to sets that contain 2: set0 (visited), set1 → set1: dist[1] = 1+1 = 2\n- Set1: {2,3} → 2 visited, so e=3 → dist_element[3] = 2\n- From element 3: goes to sets: set1 (visited), set2 → set2: dist[2] = 2+1 = 3\n- Set2: {3,4,5} → 3 visited, 4,5 → dist_element[4]=3, dist_element[5]=3\n- So dist_element[5] = 3 → operations = 3-1 = 2 → correct.\n\nSample 2:\n- 1 → set0 → dist_set[0]=1\n- set0 → 1 and 2: dist_element[2]=1\n- so dist_element[2] = 1 → M=2 → operations = 1-1=0 → correct.\n\nSample 3:\n- 1: go to set0 → set0: dist=1\n- set0: {1,3} → 3: dist_element[3]=1\n- 3: go to set0 only → no new set\n- 2: not reached yet\n- So element 2 never gets visit