    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_53",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a binary array nums.\nA subarray of an array is good if it contains exactly one element with the value 1.\nReturn an integer denoting the number of ways to split the array nums into good subarrays. As the number may be too large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [0,1,0,0,1]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: There are 3 ways to split nums into good subarrays:\n- [0,1] [0,0,1]\n- [0,1,0] [0,1]\n- [0,1,0,0] [1]\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [0,1,0]\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: There is 1 way to split nums into good subarrays:\n- [0,1,0]\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n0 <= nums[i] <= 1",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of ways to split a binary array into good subarrays, where each good subarray contains exactly one element with value 1.\n\nLet me analyze the pattern:\n- Each good subarray must contain exactly one 1\n- If there are no 1s in the array, it's impossible to create good subarrays (return 0)\n- If there's exactly one 1, there's exactly one way to split (the entire array)\n- If there are multiple 1s, I need to split between consecutive 1s\n\nThe key insight is that between two consecutive 1s, if there are `k` zeros, I can make the split at any of the `k+1` positions (before the first zero, between zeros, or after the last zero).\n\nFor example, with `[0,1,0,0,1]`:\n- First 1 at index 1, second 1 at index 4\n- Between them: 2 zeros (at indices 2,3)\n- I can split at 3 possible positions: after index 1, after index 2, or after index 3\n- This gives us 3 ways\n\nThe total number of ways is the product of the number of splitting positions between each consecutive pair of 1s.\n\n```python\ndef numberOfGoodSubarraySplits(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Find all positions of 1s\n    ones_positions = []\n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        if num == 1:\n            ones_positions.append(i)\n    \n    # If no 1s, no good subarrays possible\n    if len(ones_positions) == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # If only one 1, there's exactly one way (whole array)\n    if len(ones_positions) == 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    # For multiple 1s, the answer is the product of \n    # (number of zeros between consecutive 1s + 1)\n    result = 1\n    for i in range(1, len(ones_positions)):\n        # Number of positions to split between consecutive 1s\n        # equals number of zeros between them + 1\n        zeros_between = ones_positions[i] - ones_positions[i-1] - 1\n        split_positions = zeros_between + 1\n        result = (result * split_positions) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
