    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The degree sequence (6, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2) has one vertex of degree 6 and six vertices of degree 2. However, in a simple graph with 7 vertices, no vertex can have degree 6 unless it connects to all other six vertices, meaning every other vertex must have degree at least 1 from that connection. But since all other vertices have degree exactly 2, each must connect to the degree-6 vertex and one other. This forms a cycle among the six degree-2 vertices with the high-degree vertex connected to all. However, such a configuration is impossible because the vertex with degree 6 is connected to all others, but then the remaining edges to fulfill degree 2 on each must form a 6-cycle among them, but that gives each of them degree 2 — total degree sequence (6,2,2,2,2,2,2) is graphical. Counting labeled graphs: the degree-6 vertex is unique (only one choice). The remaining 6 vertices form a cycle — there are (6−1)!/2 = 60/2 = 30 distinct cycles (number of labeled cyclic orderings). But since one vertex connects to all, and the rest form a cycle, each such graph corresponds to a cyclic arrangement. So, number of such graphs is the number of distinct 6-cycles on 6 labeled vertices: (6-1)! / 2 = 60, no: (6-1)! = 120 → /2 = 60? Wait: Number of cycles on 6 labeled vertices is (6-1)! / 2 = 60 / 2 = 30. But we fix the labels.\n\nActually, the number of distinct cycles on 6 labeled vertices is (6−1)! / 2 = 60 / 2 = 30. Each such cycle, together with the universal vertex connected to all six, gives the degree sequence (6,2,2,2,2,2,2): the universal vertex has degree 6, each other has degree 1 from the universal vertex and 1 from the cycle — wait, no: degree 2 from cycle? No: if a vertex is in a cycle of 6, it has degree 2 from the cycle. But if it's also connected to the universal vertex, its total degree is 3. Contradiction.\n\nSo that can't be.\n\nWait: the degree sequence is (6,2,2,2,2,2,2) — sum is 6+12=18 → total edges = 9.\n\nBut in a 7-vertex graph, max degree is 6, and degree 6 vertex is connected to all others. So each other vertex already has degree at least 1.\n\nBut they must have degree exactly 2, so each must have exactly one additional edge.\n\nSo we need to add 6 edges (since sum of degrees is 18 → 9 edges; one vertex connected to 6 others → 6 edges; remaining 3 edges must account for the additional degrees).\n\nEach of the six vertices needs one more edge to go from degree 1 to degree 2. So each needs one more incident edge.\n\nSo we need to add 6/2 = 3 edges among the six vertices, such that each has degree 1 in this subgraph (since each already has degree 1 from connection to the hub, and needs one more).\n\nSo we need a 3-edge graph on 6 vertices, where each vertex has degree 1 — that is, a perfect matching.\n\nNumber of perfect matchings on 6 labeled vertices: number of ways to partition 6 vertices into 3 pairs.\n\nThat is: (6 choose 2) * (4 choose 2) * (2 choose 2) / 3! = (15 * 6 * 1) / 6 = 90 / 6 = 15.\n\nSo there are 15 such graphs.\n\nThe vertex of degree 6 is uniquely determined (only one can have degree 6), so we don't divide or multiply by symmetry.\n\nThus, the number of labeled graphs is 15.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
