      "generated_answer": "For jointly Gaussian random variables forming a Markov chain $X \\rightarrow Y \\rightarrow Z$, the mutual information $I(X;Z)$ is bounded by the product of the correlation coefficients. Using the data processing inequality and properties of Gaussian mutual information, $I(X;Z) = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_2(1 - \\rho_{XY}^2 \\rho_{YZ}^2)$, but more accurately, for a Markov chain, $I(X;Z) \\leq I(X;Y)$ and $I(X;Z) = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_2\\left(1 + \\frac{\\rho_{XZ}^2}{1 - \\rho_{XZ}^2}\\right)$, where $\\rho_{XZ} = \\rho_{XY} \\rho_{YZ} = 0.1 \\cdot 0.9 = 0.09$. Then $I(X;Z) = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_2(1 + \\frac{(0.09)^2}{1 - (0.09)^2}) \\approx \\frac{1}{2} \\log_2(1 + 0.0081) \\approx \\frac{0.0081}{2 \\ln 2} \\approx 0.00587$ bits.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{E}",
