    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ m = 2^n $, so there are $ m $ possible values of $ X $, and we are asking $ n + \\sqrt{n} $ random questions of the form \"Is $ X \\in S_i $?\", where each subset $ S_i $ is chosen uniformly at random from all $ 2^m $ subsets. Each question reveals 1 bit of information on average, but since the subsets are random, not optimized like in binary search, the information gained per question is less efficient.\n\nThe expected number of elements consistent with the answers decreases multiplicatively; however, after $ n + \\sqrt{n} $ questions, the expected number of remaining elements is still greater than 1 by a margin that diminishes slowly. Using Markov’s inequality, the probability that more than one element remains (i.e., error) tends to 0 as $ n \\to \\infty $, because $ \\mathbb{E}[\\text{number of remaining elements}] \\to 1 $, and the excess $ \\sqrt{n} $ questions helps suppress the variance asymptotically.\n\nThus, the probability of error goes to 0 as $ n \\to \\infty $.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
