ay to get yzy. D → Ey → zy; E → z — so only one z. So zy only. So xyzy = x + yzy? No. Or x + something → no.\n\nBut let’s derive xyz:\n\nCan we get \"xyz\"? That is x y z.\n\nFrom A → B C.\n\n- B must generate x (minimal)\n- C must generate yz — but C → B (x's), or D → y or zy → no rule produces yz. So \"yz\" not possible. So C cannot generate \"yz\" → so \"xyz\" = x + yz → not possible.\n\nSimilarly, \"xz\" = x + z → but C must generate z → no rule D or E gives z alone → E → z only through E → z, but D → Ey → zy → so z must be followed by y.\n\nSo z cannot appear alone — only as \"zy\". So \"xz\" ends in z → invalid.\n\nThus both C (xz) and E (xyz) seem invalid.\n\nBut wait: can E appear alone? E → z, but D → Ey → so Ey, and E → z → so zy.\n\nNo rule produces E alone.\n\nSo only way to get z is through D → Ey → zy.\n\nSo any z must be followed by y.\n\nSo strings ending in z are impossible.\n\nThus:\n- C. xz → ends in z → impossible\n- Other strings: xy (ends y), xxzy (ends y), xxxxy (y), xyz (ends z), xyzy (ends y)\n\nxyz ends in z → cannot be.\n\nBut wait — \"xyz\" is x y z — last character is z → but no string can end in z → so invalid.\n\nSimilarly, xz ends in z — invalid.\n\nBut only one answer is to be selected.\n\nNow look at the question: \"Which of the following is not a sentence\"\n\nSo only one correct answer expected.\n\nNow examine B: xxzy — is this possible?\n\nB → xx (x⁺), C → D → Ey → zy → so B → xx, C → zy → A → xxzy → yes.\n\nF. xyzy — x y z y — can we get that?\n\nIs there a way? C → D → Ey → zy → so C → zy → then B → x → so A → x zy → xzy\n\nNot xyzy.\n\nIs D recursive? D → y | Ey → E → z — no recursion.\n\nSo D → y or zy\n\nC → B or D → so C → x⁺ or y or zy\n\nSo C never produces yzy or anything with two y's.\n\nSo xyzy = x y z y — would require C to produce yzy or something — impossible.\n\nSo xyzy not possible.\n\nBut wait — could C → D → y → and then something? No — A → BC only once.\n\nSo A → B C — B gives some x's, then C gives one of: x⁺,