      "generated_answer": "In a strictly binary tree, every non-leaf node has two children, so the minimum height for N nodes is ⌈log₂(N+1)⌉ − 1 ≈ log₂(N). The condition that the ratio of any two root-to-leaf path lengths is bounded by c implies the tree is **height-balanced** in a weak sense: no path is more than c times longer than another. This ensures that the height h is at most a constant factor of the minimum possible height, so h = O(log N), specifically h < c log₂(N) for sufficiently large N.\n\nThus, the best characterization is:  \n\\boxed{E}",
