      "generated_answer": "Thomson's point in the short-term violinist case is to distinguish between moral obligations and rights. She argues that while you may be morally generous in allowing the violinist to use your kidneys for one hour, this does not entail that the violinist has a right to them. Therefore, the correct answer is that you ought to allow it, but the violinist does not have a right to it.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
