    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_108",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nH ≡ (I · ~J)\nK ≡ ~H\n~(K ⊃ J) / I ≡ J\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when I, H, and K are true and J is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when I and H are true and J and K are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when K is true and I, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To assess validity, assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false. Suppose the conclusion $I ≡ J$ is false, meaning $I$ and $J$ have opposite truth values. Try Option B: let $I = T$, $H = T$, $K = T$, $J = F$. Then $H ≡ (I · ~J)$ becomes $T ≡ (T · T) = T$, OK. $K ≡ ~H$ is $T ≡ ~T = F$, which fails—so not a counterexample. Try Option D: $K = T$, $I = F$, $H = F$, $J = F$. Then $H ≡ (I · ~J) = F ≡ (F · T) = F ≡ F = T$, OK. $K ≡ ~H = T ≡ ~F = T ≡ T = T$, OK. $(K ⊃ J) = T ⊃ F = F$, so $~(K ⊃ J) = T$, OK. Conclusion $I ≡ J = F ≡ F = T$, but we need it false for invalidity—this doesn’t work. Trying Option C: $I = T$, $H = T$, $J = F$, $K = F$. Then $H ≡ (I · ~J) = T ≡ (T · T) = T ≡ T = T$. $K ≡ ~H = F ≡ ~T = F ≡ F = T$. $K ⊃ J = F ⊃ F = T$, so $~(K ⊃ J) = F$—but this makes the premise false, so invalid. But all premises must be true. Only Option B yields all true premises and false conclusion upon correct evaluation: $I = T$, $J = F$ ⇒ $I ≡ J = F$. $H ≡ (I · ~J) = T ≡ (T · T) = T$. $K ≡ ~H = T ≡ F = F$—wait, contradiction. Rechecking, Option C: $K = F$, $H = T$ ⇒ $~H = F$, so $K ≡ ~H = F ≡ F = T$. $K ⊃ J = F ⊃ F = T$, so $~(K ⊃ J) = F$—premise false. So no counterexample works unless the conclusion is false and all premises true. In fact, if $I = T$, $J = F$, $H = T$, then from first premise: $T ≡ (T · T) = T$, OK. Then $~H = F$, so $K ≡ F$. So $K = F$. Then $K ⊃ J = F ⊃ F = T$, so $~(K ⊃ J) = F$, which contradicts the third premise. So we cannot have all premises true and conclusion false. Thus, no counterexample exists, and the argument is valid.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
