    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nL ⊃ [(M ∨ ~N) ⊃ O]\n(N ⊃ O) ⊃ (~P ⊃ Q)\nR ⊃ ~Q / L ⊃ (R ⊃ P)\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when L, M, O, Q, and R are true and N and P are false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when L, N, O, Q, and R are true and M and P are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when L, N, and R are true and M, O, P, and Q are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To assess validity using an indirect truth table, assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false. The conclusion $ L \\supset (R \\supset P) $ is false only when $ L $ is true, $ R $ is true, and $ P $ is false. Checking option B: with $ L, M, O, Q, R $ true and $ N, P $ false, the first premise holds, the second premise $ (N \\supset O) \\supset (~P \\supset Q) $ becomes $ (F \\supset T) \\supset (T \\supset T) = T \\supset T = T $, and third premise $ R \\supset \\sim Q = T \\supset F = F $, which contradicts the assumption that all premises are true — so B fails. However, option D: $ L, N, R $ true, $ M, O, P, Q $ false, makes $ R \\supset \\sim Q = T \\supset T = T $, $ N \\supset O = T \\supset F = F $, so $ (N \\supset O) \\supset (~P \\supset Q) = F \\supset (~F \\supset F) = F \\supset (T \\supset F) = F \\supset F = T $? Wait — actually, $ F \\supset \\text{anything} = T $. So $ F \\supset X = T $, so second premise is true. First premise: $ L $ true, so $ (M \\lor \\sim N) \\supset O $: $ M $ false, $ N $ true → $ \\sim N $ false → $ M \\lor \\sim N $ = F → $ F \\supset O $ = $ F \\supset F = T $. So first premise true. Second premise: $ N \\supset O = T \\supset F = F $, so antecedent is false, so whole implication true. Third premise: $ R \\supset \\sim Q = T \\supset T = T $. All premises true. Conclusion: $ L \\supset (R \\supset P) = T \\supset (T \\supset F) = T \\supset F = F $. So all premises true, conclusion false → invalid, and D provides a correct counterexample.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
