      "generated_answer": "To assess validity using an indirect truth table, assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false. The conclusion ~(E ≡ F) is false when E and F have the same truth value (both true or both false). Suppose E and F are both true; then for the first premise E ⊃ (F ∨ ~G) to be true, (F ∨ ~G) must be true — which it is since F is true. For the second premise F ⊃ (~E ∨ ~G), since F is true, (~E ∨ ~G) must be true; but E is true, so ~E is false, meaning ~G must be true, so G must be false. Thus, when E and F are true and G is false, both premises are true, but the conclusion is false — a counterexample.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
