  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_504",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Your company has just written one million units of a one-year European asset-or-nothing put option on an equity index fund. The equity index fund is currently trading at 1000. It pays dividends continuously at a rate proportional to its price; the dividend yield is 2%. It has a volatility of 20%. The option’s payoff will be made only if the equity index fund is down by more than 40% at the end of one year. The continuously compounded risk-free interest rate is 2.5% Using the Black-Scholes model, determine the price of the asset-or-nothing put options. Give the answer in millions.\n\nOptions: \nA. 7.5\nB. 5.2\nC. 3.6\nD. 2.2\nE. 2.5\nF. 4.1\nG. 4.7\nH. 5.9\nI. 1.9\nJ. 6.8\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The asset-or-nothing put pays one unit of the asset if the price is below the strike at maturity. Here, the strike is implicitly 600 (since a 40% drop from 1000 is 600), and the payoff is the value of the index if $ S_T < 600 $. Using the Black-Scholes formula for an asset-or-nothing put: $ e^{-rT} \\cdot S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_2) $, where $ d_2 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r - q - \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2})T}{\\sigma \\sqrt{T}} $. Plugging in $ S_0 = 1000 $, $ K = 600 $, $ r = 0.025 $, $ q = 0.02 $, $ \\sigma = 0.20 $, $ T = 1 $, we compute $ d_2 \\approx 1.90 $, so $ N(-d_2) \\approx N(-1.90) = 0.0287 $, leading to a price of $ 1000 \\cdot e^{-(0.025+0.02)} \\cdot 0.0287 \\approx 27.95 $ per option, or \\$27.95 million for one million options — but this seems off.\n\nWait — recalculate carefully:  \n$ d_2 = \\frac{\\ln(1000/600) + (0.025 - 0.02 - 0.5 \\cdot 0.2^2)(1)}{0.2} = \\frac{\\ln(1.6667) + (0.005 - 0.02)}{0.2} = \\frac{0.5108 - 0.015}{0.2} = \\frac{0.4958}{0.2} = 2.479 $? No:  \nActually:  \n$ \\ln(1000/600) = \\ln(5/3) \\approx \\ln(1.6667) \\approx 0.5108 $  \n$ r - q - \\sigma^2/2 = 0.025 - 0.02 - 0.02 = -0.015 $  \nSo $ d_2 = (0.5108 - 0.015)/0.2 = 0.4958 / 0.2 = 2.479 $, so $ N(-d_2) = N(-2.479) \\approx 0.0066 $  \nThen price = $ e^{-0.025} \\cdot 1000 \\cdot e^{-0.02} \\cdot 0.0066 \\approx 1000 \\cdot e^{-0.045} \\cdot 0.0066 \\approx 1000 \\cdot 0.956 \\cdot 0.0066 \\approx 6.31 $  \nSo approximately \\$6.31 million — closest to 6.8? But let's double-check.\n\nWait — correction: for asset-or-nothing put, the formula is:  \n$ \\text{Price} = e^{-rT} \\cdot S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_2) $? No: actually, the standard formula for asset-or-nothing put is $ S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_1) $? Wait — no.\n\nWait: Digital (binary) options:  \n- Cash-or-nothing put: $ e^{-rT} N(-d_2) $  \n- Asset-or-nothing put: $ S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_1) $ — actually, no: correction.\n\nActually, correct formula for **asset-or-nothing put** is:  \n$ S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_1) $? Or $ -d_2 $?\n\nLet’s clarify:  \nIn Black-Scholes:\n- $ d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S/K) + (r - q + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma \\sqrt{T}} $\n- $ d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma \\sqrt{T} $\n\n- Cash-or-nothing put: $ e^{-rT} N(-d_2) $\n- Asset-or-nothing put: $ S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_1) $\n\nWait — is that correct?\n\nActually, **asset-or-nothing** depends on whether it's call or put:\n- Asset-or-nothing call: pays $ S_T $ if $ S_T > K $ → value: $ S_0 e^{-qT} N(d_1) $\n- Asset-or-nothing put: pays $ S_T $ if $ S_T < K $ → value: $ S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_1) $\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSo we need $ N(-d_1) $\n\nCompute $ d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(1000/600) + (r - q + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma \\sqrt{T}} $\n\n= $ \\frac{\\ln(1.6667) + (0.025 - 0.02 + 0.02)}{0.2} $  \n= $ \\frac{0.5108 + 0.025}{0.2} = \\frac{0.5358}{0.2} = 2.679 $\n\nSo $ d_1 = 2.679 $, $ N(-d_1) = N(-2.679) \\approx 0.0037 $\n\nThen asset-or-nothing put price = $ S_0 e^{-qT} N(-d_1) = 1000 \\cdot e^{-0.02} \\cdot 0.0037 \\approx 1000 \\cdot 0.9802 \\cdot 0.0037 \\approx 3.626 $\n\nSo price per option ≈ 3.626, so one million options → $3.626 million ≈ **3.6 million**\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 271,
        "output_tokens": 1426,
        "total_tokens": 1697
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
