    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5641",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the main contrast between the short-run and long-run Phillips curve?\n\nOptions: \nA. In the short run there is a negative relationship between inflation and unemployment and in the long run the relationship is positive.\nB. In the short run there is a constant relationship between inflation and unemployment and in the long run the relationship is positive.\nC. In the short run there is a constant relationship between inflation and unemployment and in the long run the relationship is negative.\nD. In the short run there is a negative relationship between inflation and unemployment and in the long run the relationship is constant.\nE. In the short run there is a positive relationship between inflation and unemployment, in the long run the relationship is non-existent.\nF. In the short run, inflation and unemployment are unrelated, while in the long run, there is a positive relationship.\nG. In the short run there is a positive relationship between inflation and unemployment and in the long run the relationship is constant.\nH. In the short run there is a positive relationship between inflation and unemployment and in the long run the relationship is negative.\nI. In the short run, inflation and unemployment are unrelated, while in the long run, there is a negative relationship.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The short-run Phillips curve shows a negative (inverse) relationship between inflation and unemployment, as policymakers can temporarily reduce unemployment at the cost of higher inflation. In the long run, however, this trade-off disappears and the Phillips curve is vertical, meaning there is no relationship between inflation and unemployment—referred to as the natural rate of unemployment. Thus, the relationship is negative in the short run and constant (vertical) in the long run.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
