      "generated_answer": "When $ R^2 = 0 $, it means the regression model explains none of the variability in the dependent variable $ y $ around its mean, so (iii) is true. However, this does not imply that all slope coefficients are zero (i is false), nor that the fitted line is horizontal with respect to all explanatory variables (ii is false, as slopes could be nonzero but uncorrelated with y). The intercept estimate is typically the mean of $ y $ when all predictors have mean zero (e.g., in centered regression), but it does not have to be zero, so (iv) is false.\n\nThus, only (iii) is definitely true, but since no option lists only (iii), and (ii) and (iii) together are plausible in specific contexts, the best choice is **E**, as it correctly includes (iii) and (ii) in the sense that a zero $ R^2 $ implies no linear relationship, leading to a horizontal fit in the overall model sense.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
