      "generated_answer": "The coal company has the right to extract coal from the land, which is a classic example of a *profit-a-prendre*—a nonpossessory interest allowing one to enter and remove resources from another’s land. Unlike an easement, which allows use of land, a profit-a-prendre specifically involves taking something from the land, such as minerals, timber, or in this case, coal. The fact that the company pays royalties and had extraction rights confirms this interest.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
