      "generated_answer": "The covenant against encumbrances in a general warranty deed guarantees that the property is free from any encumbrances at the time of conveyance, including unrecorded interests if the grantee is under a notice statute and had no actual or constructive notice. However, under a notice-type recording statute, a subsequent bona fide purchaser (like the farmer in 1979 and then the buyer in 2009) without notice takes free of unrecorded interests; thus, the 1936 mortgage was effectively extinguished as to later purchasers without notice. Therefore, the covenant was not breached because the property was not subject to enforceable encumbrances at the time of the farmer’s conveyance.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
