      "generated_answer": "The farmer’s use of the road began with the widower’s explicit permission (\"No problem...\"), making the use permissive rather than adverse, which defeats the claim of an easement by prescription. For an easement by prescription to form, the use must be hostile, continuous, open, and without permission. Since the use was initially allowed, it cannot be considered hostile or under a claim of right, and thus cannot mature into a prescriptive easement.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
