      "generated_answer": "We are asked to find the remainder when the sum  \n$$\n\\binom{\\binom{3}{2}}{2} + \\binom{\\binom{4}{2}}{2} + \\dots + \\binom{\\binom{40}{2}}{2}\n$$\nis divided by $1000$.\n\nFirst, note that $\\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$, so $\\binom{\\binom{n}{2}}{2} = \\binom{\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} - 1\\right)$, which simplifies to:\n$$\n\\binom{\\binom{n}{2}}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} - 1\\right) = \\frac{n(n-1)}{4} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{n(n-1) - 2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{n(n-1)[n(n-1) - 2]}{8}\n$$\nThus the sum becomes $\\sum_{n=3}^{40} \\frac{n(n-1)[n(n-1) - 2]}{8}$. Let $x_n = n(n-1)$, so the term becomes $\\frac{x_n(x_n - 2)}{8} = \\frac{x_n^2 - 2x_n}{8}$. So the sum is:\n$$\n\\sum_{n=3}^{40} \\frac{[n(n-1)]^2 - 2n(n-1)}{8}\n$$\nLet’s define $f(n) = n(n-1)$. Then the sum is $\\frac{1}{8} \\sum_{n=3}^{40} [f(n)^2 - 2f(n)]$. Compute $\\sum_{n=3}^{40} f(n) = \\sum_{n=3}^{40} n(n-1) = \\sum_{n=3}^{40} (n^2 - n) = \\sum_{n=1}^{40} (n^2 - n) - (1^2 - 1) - (2^2 - 2) = \\left(\\sum n^2 - \\sum n\\right) - 0 - 2 = \\left[\\frac{40\\cdot41\\cdot81}{6} - \\frac{40\\cdot41}{2}\\right] - 2$, but better to compute directly:\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{k} n(n-1) = \\sum n^2 - n = \\frac{k(k+1)(2k+1)}{6} - \\frac{k(k+1)}{2} = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{2k+1}{3} - 1\\right) = \\frac{k(k+1)(2k-2)}{6} = \\frac{k(k+1)(k-1)}{3}\n$$\nWait, simplify:  \n$\\sum_{n=1}^{k} n(n-1) = 2\\binom{k+1}{3}$, since $n(n-1) = 2\\binom{n}{2}$, and $\\sum_{n=1}^k \\binom{n}{2} = \\binom{k+1}{3}$. So:  \n$$\n\\sum_{n=3}^{40} n(n-1) = 2\\sum_{n=3}^{40} \\binom{n}{2} = 2\\left[\\binom{41}{3} - \\binom{3}{3}\\right] = 2\\left[\\binom{41}{3} - 1\\right]\n$$\nWait — more carefully: $\\sum_{n=2}^{k} \\binom{n}{2} = \\binom{k+1}{3}$, so from $n=3$ to $n=40$, $\\sum_{n=3}^{40} \\binom{n}{2} = \\binom{41}{3} - \\binom{3}{2} = \\binom{41}{3} - 1$, since the sum fro