      "generated_answer": "We start by simplifying each term in the product using the identity $ \\log_a(b) = \\frac{\\log b}{\\log a} $, so $ \\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4})} = \\frac{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\log k}}{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2 - 4})}{\\log(k+1)}} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1)\\log 5 / \\log k}{(k^2 - 4)\\log 5 / \\log(k+1)} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1)\\log(k+1)}{(k^2 - 4)\\log k} $. Noting that $ k^2 - 1 = (k - 1)(k + 1) $ and $ k^2 - 4 = (k - 2)(k + 2) $, the term becomes $ \\frac{(k - 1)(k + 1)\\log(k+1)}{(k - 2)(k + 2)\\log k} $, but a better approach is to use change-of-base to write $ \\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1}) = \\frac{(k^2 - 1)\\log 5}{\\log k} $, so the entire fraction becomes $ \\frac{(k^2 - 1)/\\log k}{(k^2 - 4)/\\log(k+1)} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(k+1)}{\\log k} \\Rightarrow \\frac{(k^2 - 1)\\log(k+1)}{(k^2 - 4)\\log k} $. Then, using $ \\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1}) = (k^2 - 1) \\cdot \\frac{\\log 5}{\\log k} $, and similar for denominator, we get:  \n$$\n\\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4})} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\cdot \\frac{\\log 5}{\\log k}}{(k^2 - 4) \\cdot \\frac{\\log 5}{\\log(k+1)}} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1)}{(k^2 - 4)} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(k+1)}{\\log k}\n= \\frac{(k-1)(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\log(k+1)}{\\log k}\n$$\nThis approach leads to complex telescoping.\n\nInstead, use $ \\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1}) = (k^2 - 1) \\log_k 5 = (k^2 - 1) \\cdot \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln k} $, and $ \\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4}) = (k^2 - 4) \\cdot \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln(k+1)} $, so the ratio becomes:\n$$\n\\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\ln 5 / \\ln k}{(k^2 - 4) \\ln 5 / \\ln(k+1)} = \\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k} = \\frac{(k - 1)(k + 1)}{(k - 2)(k + 2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k}\n$$\nStill messy.\n\nBetter idea: Use identity $ \\log_a b = \\frac{1}{\\log_b a} $, and write $ \\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4})} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\log_k 5}{(k^2 - 4) \\log_{k+1} 5} $.\n\nBut $ \\log_k 5 = \\frac{\\log 5}{\\log k} $, so we can't avoid base logs.\n\nTry to write everything in terms of natural logs:\n$$\n\\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2 - 4})} = \\frac{\\frac{\\ln(5^{k^2 - 1})}{\\ln k}}{\\frac{\\ln(5^{k^2 - 4})}{\\ln(k+1)}} = \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\ln 5 / \\ln k}{(k^2 - 4) \\ln 5 / \\ln(k+1)} = \\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k}\n$$\nSo the term is $ \\frac{k^2 - 1}{k^2 - 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k} = \\frac{(k - 1)(k + 1)}{(k - 2)(k + 2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k} $\n\nNow the product from $ k = 4 $ to $ 63 $ is:\n$$\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\left( \\frac{(k - 1)(k + 1)}{(k - 2)(k + 2)} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k} \\right)\n= \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k - 1)(k + 1)}{(k - 2)(k + 2)} \\right) \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k} \\right)\n$$\n\nNow consider the second product: $ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k} = \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln 6}{\\ln 5} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln 7}{\\ln 6} \\cdots \\frac{\\ln 64}{\\ln 63} = \\frac{\\ln 64}{\\ln 4} $\n\nTelescoping! So this is $ \\frac{\\ln 64}{\\ln 4} = \\log_4 64 = \\log_4 (4^{3/2}) $? Wait: $ 64 = 4^3 $, since $ 4^3 = 64 $? $ 4^1 = 4, 4^2 = 16, 4^3 = 64 $, yes. So $ \\log_4 64 = 3 $. So second part is 3.\n\nNow first product: $ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k - 1)(k + 1)}{(k - 2)(k + 2)} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k - 1)}{(k - 2)} \\cdot \\frac{(k + 1)}{(k + 2)} $\n\nBreak into two separate products:\n$$\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k - 1}{k - 2} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k - 1}{k - 2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\cdots \\frac{62}{61} = \\frac{62}{2} = 31 \\quad \\text{(telescoping)}\n$$\nIndices: when $ k=4 $, $ \\frac{3}{2} $; $ k=5 $, $ \\frac{4}{3} $; ... $ k=63 $, $ \\frac{62}{61} $, so numerator: 3 to 62, denominator: 2 to 61, so result $ \\frac{62}{2} = 31 $.\n\nNow $ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k + 1}{k + 2} = \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7} \\cdot \\frac{7}{8} \\cdots \\frac{64}{65} $ — wait: $ k=4 $: $ \\frac{5}{6} $, $ k=5 $: $ \\frac{6}{7} $, ..., $ k=63 $: $ \\frac{64}{65} $. So it telescopes to $ \\frac{5}{65} = \\frac{1}{13} $.\n\nSo the entire first product is $ 31 \\cdot \\frac{1}{13} = \\frac{31}{13} $.\n\nThen total product is $ \\frac{31}{13} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{93}{13} $.\n\nNow $ m = 93 $, $ n = 13 $, relatively prime? 13 is prime, doesn't divide 93 (13×7=91, 93-91=2), yes. So $ m + n = 93 + 13 = \\boxed{106} $.\n\nBut let's double-check the ranges.\n\nFirst telescoping: $ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} $: k from 4 to 63 → terms $ \\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{4}{3}, \\frac{5}{4}, \\dots, \\frac{62}{61} $. So numerator: 3 to 62, denominator: 2 to 61 → so $ \\frac{3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 62}{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 61} = \\frac{62}{2} = 31 $. Good.\n\nSecond: $ \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k+1}{k+2} = \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7} \\cdot \\frac{7}{8} \\cdots \\frac{64}{65} $. First numerator 5, last denominator 65, all中间 cancel → $ \\frac{5}{65} = \\frac{1}{13} $. Yes.\n\nLog part: $ \\prod \\frac{\\ln(k+1)}{\\ln k} = \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 4} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln 6}{\\ln 5} \\cdots \\frac{\\ln 64}{\\ln 63} = \\frac{\\ln 64}{\\ln 4} = \\log_4 64 $. And $ 4^x = 64 = 2^6, 4^x = (2^2)^x = 2^{2x} $, so $ 2^{2x} = 2^6 \\Rightarrow 2x=6 \\Rightarrow x=3 $. Yes.\n\nSo total: $ \\frac{31}{13} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{93}{13} $, $ m=93, n=13 $, $ m+n=106 $.\n\n\\boxed{106}",
