    "global index": "AIME_113",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are $ n $ values of $ x $ in the interval $ 0 < x < 2\\pi $ where $ f(x) = \\sin(7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)) = 0 $. For $ t $ of these $ n $ values of $ x $, the graph of $ y = f(x) $ is tangent to the $ x $-axis. Find $ n + t $.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The equation $ f(x) = \\sin(7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)) = 0 $ implies $ 7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x) = k\\pi $ for integer $ k $, so $ \\sin(5x) = \\frac{k}{7} $. Valid $ k $ values are $ -7 \\leq k \\leq 7 $, but $ |\\sin(5x)| \\leq 1 $, so $ k = -7, -6, \\dots, 7 $, but only $ k = -7, -6, \\dots, 7 $ such that $ \\left| \\frac{k}{7} \\right| \\leq 1 $, i.e., $ k = -7, -6, \\dots, 7 $ — total 15 values.\n\nFor each $ k $, $ \\sin(5x) = \\frac{k}{7} $ has 10 solutions in $ 0 < x < 2\\pi $ (since $ 5x \\in (0, 10\\pi) $, and $ \\sin(\\theta) = c $ has 2 solutions per period of $ 2\\pi $, and there are 5 periods), unless $ \\frac{k}{7} = \\pm1 $, i.e., $ k = \\pm7 $, where each gives only 5 solutions (at peaks). So total $ n = 13 \\cdot 10 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 130 + 10 = 140 $.\n\nTangency to $ x $-axis occurs when $ f(x) = 0 $ and $ f'(x) = 0 $. $ f'(x) = \\cos(7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)) \\cdot 7\\pi \\cdot \\cos(5x) \\cdot 5 $. When $ f(x) = 0 $, $ \\cos(7\\pi \\cdot \\sin(5x)) = \\cos(k\\pi) = (-1)^k $. So $ f'(x) = 0 $ only if $ \\cos(5x) = 0 $. But if $ \\sin(5x) = \\pm1 $, then $ \\cos(5x) = 0 $. So only when $ k = \\pm7 $, we have $ \\cos(5x) = 0 $, and indeed $ f'(x) = 0 $. So tangency occurs at those points. For $ k = \\pm7 $, each has 5 solutions, so $ t = 10 $. Thus $ n + t = 140 + 10 = \\boxed{{150}} $.",
