 ∈ R_b, or j ∈ C_b.\n\nBut in the cells where i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_0: can add white chip unless prevented.\n\nTo prevent that, we need that this doesn't happen — but it can happen.\n\nTo prevent adding a white chip at every empty cell, for every empty cell (i,j), we must have either:\n\n- i ∈ R_b, or\n- j ∈ C_b, or\n- for black chip: i ∈ R_w or j ∈ C_w\n\nBut also, we cannot add a black chip if i ∈ R_w or j ∈ C_w.\n\nFor a cell (i,j) to be \"unaddable\", it must be that:\n- to add white: either i ∈ R_b or j ∈ C_b or (i,j) occupied\n- to add black: either i ∈ R_w or j ∈ C_w or (i,j) occupied\n\nSince (i,j) is empty, so for white, need i ∈ R_b or j ∈ C_b; for black, need i ∈ R_w or j ∈ C_w.\n\nSo to prevent both, we need:\n(i ∈ R_b or j ∈ C_b) and (i ∈ R_w or j ∈ C_w)\n\nThis must hold for every empty cell (i,j).\n\nThe empty cells are those not in (R_w × C_w) ∪ (R_b × C_b)\n\nThis includes cells like (i,j) with i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_b — but this cell cannot have a white chip because j ∈ C_b, and we may or may not have a black chip — but if it's empty, then to add black: i ∈ R_w — if i ∈ R_w, then we cannot add black chip, good; to add white: j ∈ C_b — cannot add white.\n\nSo for (i,j) with i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_b: cannot add any chip — because row i is white, so cannot add black; column j is black, so cannot add white.\n\nSimilarly for i ∈ R_b, j ∈ C_w.\n\nFor i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_0: can add white (since row i is white, col j empty), cannot add black (row i is white).\n\nSo to prevent adding white, we need j not in C_0, or i not in R_w — but i is in R_w, so we need j not in C_0, i.e., j in C_b or C_w.\n\nBut j in C_0, so not.\n\nSimilarly, only if we can ensure that there are no such cells.\n\nSo the cell (i,j) with i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_0 is empty and we can add a white chip there.\n\nTo prevent this, there must be no such cell — i.e., either no i in R_w, or no j in C_0.\n\nSimilarly, cells (i,j) with i ∈ R_0, j ∈ C_w: can add a white chip.\n\nPrevent by: no i in R_0 or no j in C_w.\n\nSimilarly, cells (i,j) with i ∈ R_b, j ∈ C_0: can add black chip; prevent by no i in R_b or no j in C_0.\nCells (i,j) with i ∈ R_0, j ∈ C_b: can add black; prevent by no i in R_0 or no j in C_b.\nCells (i,j) with i ∈ R_0, j ∈ C_0: can add any color; to prevent, need that for such a cell, the conditions hold, but it's hard.\n\nFor (i,j) in R_0 × C_0: to prevent adding white, need i ∈ R_b or j ∈ C_b; but i not in R_b (since in R_0), similarly j not in C_b, so unless we have that, cannot prevent.\n\nSimilarly for black.\n\nSo only way to prevent adding a chip at (i,j) for i ∈ R_0, j ∈ C_0 is if there are no such cells — i.e., R_0 = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nSimilarly, for the other types:\n\nFor cells in R_w × C_0: can add white; to prevent, need that R_w = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nBut if C_0 = ∅, then good.\n\nSimilarly for R_b × C_0: need R_b = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nFor R_0 × C_w: need R_0 = ∅ or C_w = ∅.\n\nFor R_0 × C_b: need R_0 = ∅ or C_b = ∅.\n\nAnd for R_0 × C_0: need R_0 = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nNow, to have maximality, we need that for every type of cell, the addition is prevented.\n\nSuppose C_0 ≠ ∅ and R_0 ≠ ∅.\n\nThen for cells in R_0 × C_0: need R_0 = ∅ or C_0 = ∅ — not true, so cannot prevent adding a chip there.\n\nTherefore, must have R_0 = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nNow, if R_0 = ∅, then no issue with R_0 × anything.\n\nSimilarly if C_0 = ∅.\n\nBut also, if R_0 = ∅, then cells like R_0 × C_w are empty.\n\nCells like R_w × C_0: could be present.\n\nFor (i,j) in R_w × C_0: can add white chip.\n\nTo prevent, we need R_w = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nBut if R_0 = ∅, then C_0 may not be empty.\n\nSo if R_w ≠ ∅ and C_0 ≠ ∅, then such cells exist and can accept white chip.\n\nTo prevent, we need that either R_w = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nSimilarly, for R_b × C_0: need R_b = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nSimilarly, if C_0 = ∅, then need for R_0 × C_w: need R_0 = ∅ or C_w = ∅, so if R_0 ≠ ∅, need C_w = ∅.\n\nThis is getting messy.\n\nFor maximal configuration, the only possibilities are that either:\n\n- C_0 = ∅, i.e., all columns are used, or\n- R_0 = ∅, i.e., all rows are used.\n\nBut is that sufficient?\n\nSuppose R_0 = ∅, so all rows are used, i.e., each row is in R_w or R_b.\n\nThen consider an empty cell (i,j).\n\nIf i ∈ R_w, then row i is white-used.\n\nNow, if j ∈ C_w, then cell (i,j) should have a white chip — because must be full rectangle.\n\nSimilarly, if i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_b, then as above, cannot add any chip.\n\nIf i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_0, then can add a white chip unless prevented.\n\nBut if the configuration is not full, i ∈ R_w, j ∈ C_0, then we can add a white chip.\n\nTo prevent this, we must ensure that there are no such cells — i.e., if R_w is non-empty, then C_0 must be empty.\n\nBut if R_w is empty, then fine.\n\nSimilarly for R_b.\n\nSo if R_0 = ∅, but R_w and R_b may be non-empty.\n\nTo prevent adding at R_w × C_0, we need that either R_w = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nSimilarly, for R_b × C_0: R_b = ∅ or C_0 = ∅.\n\nSo to have maximality when R_0 = ∅, we need that either C_0 = ∅, or R_w = ∅ and R_b = ∅.\n\nBut R_w and R_b can't both be empty if R_0 = ∅ and there are rows.\n\nIf R_0 = ∅, then R_w ∪ R_b = all rows, so if both empty, no rows, not possible for 5 rows.\n\nSo must have R_w or R_b non-empty.\n\nTherefore, to satisfy, we must have C_0 = ∅.\n\nSimilarly, if C_0 = ∅, then for R_0 × C_w, need R_0 = ∅ or C_w = ∅, so must have R_0 = ∅.\n\nTherefore, the only maximal configurations are those where all rows are used and all columns are used.\n\nThat is, R_0 = ∅ and C_0 = ∅.\n\nThen R_w and R_b partition the 5 rows, C_w and C_b partition the 5 columns.\n\nThen the white chips are in R_w × C_w, black in R_b × C_b, and since the sets are disjoint, the only overlap would be if a cell is in both, but since R_w and R_b disjoint, no cell is in both rectangles.\n\nThe cell (i,j) for i in R_w, j in C_b: in this configuration, is it filled? Only if in one of the rectangles.\n\nR_w × C_w: requires j in C_w, not C_b.\n\nR_b × C_b: requires i in R_b, not R_w.\n\nSo (i,j) is empty.\n\nCan we add a white chip? Row i is in R_w, so white-only, so can place white. Column j is in C_b, so must be black-only — if we place a white chip, it will have a white chip, but it may have a black chip elsewhere (since C_b means there is a black chip in column j), so yes, there is a cell in column j with a black chip (since C_b and C_0=∅, there is a black chip in column j), so if we add a white chip, the column will have both colors — violation.\n\nSimilarly, cannot add a black chip because row i has a white chip.\n\nSo cannot add any chip.\n\nSimilarly for other cells.\n\nSo maximality is satisfied.\n\nAlso, the rectangle is full.\n\nSo only when all rows and all columns are used.\n\nNumber of such configurations:\n\nChoose for each row: assign to R_w or R_b: 2^5 = 32 ways.\n\nChoose for each column: assign to C_w or C_b: 2^5 = 32 ways.\n\nThen white chips: R_w × C_w, black: R_b × C_b.\n\nTotal configurations: 32 × 32 = 1024.\n\nBut is that all?\n\nCould there be configurations where not all rows and columns are used, but still maximal?\n\nFor example, suppose only one row is used, and it is white, and all columns are white-used. But if not all columns are used, but we want C_0=∅.\n\nSuppose we set R_w = {1}, C_w = {1,2,3,4,5}, R_b=∅, C_b=∅.\n\nThen R_0 = {2,3,4,5}, C_0 = ∅.\n\nC_0 = ∅, all columns used.\n\nThen is it maximal?\n\nWhite chips: {1} × {1,2,3,4,5} = 5 cells.\n\nNo black chips.\n\nNow, cell (2,1): i=2 ∈ R_0, j=1 ∈ C_w.\n\nCan we add a black chip? Row 2 has no chips, so can be black; column 1 has only white chips, so if we add a black chip, it will have both colors — cannot.\n\nCan we add a white chip? Row 2 has no chips, so can be white; column 1 is already has white, so if we add a white chip, it's fine — it will have only white chips.\n\nSo we can add a white chip at (2,1).\n\nThus, not maximal.\n\nTo prevent this, since C_0 = ∅, but R_0 ≠ ∅, and C_w ≠ ∅, we need R_0 = ∅ or C_w = ∅ to prevent adding at R_0 × C_w, but C_w ≠ ∅, R_0 ≠ ∅, so cannot prevent.\n\nTherefore, only when both R_0 = ∅ and C_0 = ∅.\n\nAnother try: suppose no white chips, only black in a subgrid.\n\nBut same issue.\n\nSo only when all rows and all columns are used.\n\nTherefore, number is (2^5) for row assignments × (2^5) for column assignments = 32 × 32 = 1024.\n\nBut in this case, the total number of chips is |R_w| |C_w| + |R_b| |C_b|.\n\nSince |R_w| + |R_b| =5, |C_w| + |C_b| =5.\n\nAnd this can be up to, say, if |R_w|=5, |C_w|=5, then white chips=25, black=0.\n\nOr if |R_w|=3, |C_w|=4, then white=12, |R_b|=2, |C_b|=1, black=2, total 14, etc.\n\nAll within 25.\n\nAnd maximality as above.\n\nNow, each such choice gives a unique configuration.\n\nNumber of ways: choose subset R_w of rows: 2^5 = 32 choices (including empty).\n\nSimilarly R_b = complement, so determined.\n\nSimilarly for columns: 2^5 = 32 choices for C_w, C_b complement.\n\nSo 32 × 32 = 1024.\n\nBut is the empty configuration included? If R_w = empty, R_b = all rows, C_w = empty, C_b = all columns.\n\nThen white chips: ∅ × ∅ = empty.\n\nBlack chips: all rows × all columns = 25 cells, all black.\n\nSimilarly, if R_w=all, C_w=all, then all white.\n\nIf R_w=all, C_w=empty, then C_b=all, so black chips: empty (since R_b=empty), white chips: all rows × empty = empty, so no chips.\n\nBut is that allowed? No chips.\n\nCan we add a chip? For any cell (i,j), row i is in R_w or R_b? If R_w=all, R_b=empty, so row i in R_w, so it is white-used.\n\nColumn j in C_w=empty or C_b=all, so j in C_b, black-used.\n\nThen to add a white chip: row i is white-only, but column j is black-only — has black chips, so cannot add white.\n\nSimilarly, cannot add black because row would have black and white.\n\nSo cannot add any chip.\n\nAnd there are no chips, but the condition is satisfied? \"all chips in the same row have the same colour\" — vacuously true.\n\nAnd no cell has a chip, so \"each cell at most one chip\" true.\n\nAnd \"any additional chip would violate\" — yes, as above.\n\nSo the empty grid is also maximal.\n\nIn this case, with R_w=all, R_b=empty, C_w=empty, C_b=all, then white chips: all × empty = empty, black: empty × all = empty, so no chips.\n\nSimilarly, other combinations where one rectangle is empty.\n\nSo yes, included.\n\nTotal number is 32 * 32 = 1024.\n\nBut earlier I thought 14528, but that was wrong because of the model error.\n\nSo correct answer is 1024.\n\nBut let's confirm with small grid.\n\nFor 1x1 grid.\n\nThen R_w, R_b partition rows: two choices: ( {1}, ∅ ) or ( ∅, {1} )\n\nSimilarly for columns: ( {1}, ∅ ) or ( ∅, {1} )\n\nSo 2 * 2 = 4 configurations:\n\n1. R_w=1, R_b=0, C_w=1, C_b=0: white chip at (1,1)\n\n2. R_w=1, R_b=0, C_w=0, C_b=1: no chip (as above)\n\n3. R_w=0, R_b=1, C_w=1, C_b=0: no chip\n\n4. R_w=0, R_b=1, C_w=0, C_b=1: black chip at (1,1)\n\nNow, are there only these?\n\n- All chips: white only, black only, or no chip.\n\nIs no chip maximal? As above, yes, because if you try to add white, row is say in R_w but col in C_b if we use this labeling, but in the actual physical, if no chips, can we add? But in this configuration, the row and column are assigned by the sets.\n\nIn case 2: R_w={1}, so row 1 is designated for white, C_b={1}, so column 1 for black. So cannot add white (column is black), cannot add black (row is white). So cannot add, maximal.\n\nSimilarly for case 3.\n\nAnd for case 1 and 4, have a chip, obviously maximal since single cell.\n\nIs there a configuration with no chip but different assignment? No, only these.\n\nCould we have a configuration where we place no chip, but without assigning? But in our model, the sets are defined by the chip placement, but for maximality, the assignment is determined.\n\nIn this case, for no chips, we could in principle have different assignments, but in our counting, we have two ways to have no chips: one where row is \"reserved\" for white, col for black; other where row for black, col for white.\n\nAnd both are valid and distinct in the sense that the \"row color\" is different, but since no chips, is the row really reserved?\n\nThe problem is that the row doesn't have a color until it has a chip.\n\nBut in this maximal configuration with no chips, why can't we add a white chip? Only if doing so would violate, but it doesn't, unless there's a constraint.\n\nIn the actual problem, with no chips, we can add a white chip at (1,1), and it would be valid: row 1 has a white chip, column 1 has a white chip.\n\nSo the empty grid should not be maximal.\n\nAh! This is the point.\n\nIn the 1x1 grid, if no chip, then we can add a white (or black) chip without violation, so it is not maximal.\n\nTherefore, the empty grid is not allowed.\n\nIn our earlier analysis, for the no-chip configuration in 1x1, like case 2: we have R_w={1}, C_b={1}, but is this consistent?\n\nWe have no chips, so R_w should be the set of rows with at least one white chip — which is empty.\n\nBut we have R_w={1}, which is not consistent.\n\nI think I have it backward.\n\nLet's define properly.\n\nLet R_w be the set of rows that contain at least one white chip.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nThen for a row to be in R_w, it must have at least one white chip.\n\nSimilarly for R_b.\n\nSince no two colors in a row, R_w and R_b are disjoint.\n\nThe set of white chips must be exactly R_w × C_w, with C_w the set of columns with at least one white chip, and for maximality, since if i in R_w, j in C_w, then (i,j) must have a white chip.\n\nSimilarly for black.\n\nAlso, the two rectangles are disjoint.\n\nNow for maximality, cannot add more.\n\nNow for a cell (i,j) not occupied.\n\nSuppose we try to add a white chip.\n\nThis requires that row i has only white chips (if it has chips) and will have only white after; similarly for column.\n\nBut if row i already has a black chip, then it is in R_b, so cannot add white.\n\nSimilarly, if column j has a black chip, in C_b, cannot add white.\n\nSimilarly for black.\n\nAlso, if we add a white chip, it may be that row i has no chips, then after adding, it will 