od{289} $. Then derivative part: $ 4*8^3*17t = 4*512*17t = 34816t $. $ 34816 \\mod 289 $? First, 289*120 = 34680, 34816 - 34680 = 136, so $ \\equiv 136t \\pmod{289} $ in the increment to m^4. So $ m^4 \\equiv 50 + 136t \\pmod{289} $, so $ m^4 + 1 \\equiv 51 + 136t \\equiv 0 \\pmod{289} $. So $ 136t \\equiv -51 \\pmod{289} $. Divide by 17: $ 8t \\equiv -3 \\pmod{17} $, so $ 8t \\equiv 14 \\pmod{17} $, $ t \\equiv 14 * 8^{-1} \\mod 17 $. $ 8*2=16≡-1, so 8*15=120≡120-7*17=120-119=1, so 8^{-1}≡15. So $ t \\equiv 14*15 = 210 \\equiv 210 - 12*17 = 210 - 204 = 6 \\pmod{17} $. So $ t=6 $, $ m = 8 + 17*6 = 8 + 102 = 110 $.\n\nSimilarly, lift $ m \\equiv 15 \\pmod{17} $ (i.e. -2): $ m = 15 + 17t $. $ m^4 \\equiv (-2)^4 + 4*(-2)^3*17t = 16 + 4*(-8)*17t = 16 - 544t \\pmod{289} $. So $ m^4 + 1 \\equiv 17 - 544t \\equiv 0 \\pmod{289} $, so $ -544t \\equiv -17 \\pmod{289} $, same as before: $ 544t \\equiv 17 \\pmod{289} $, divide by 17: $ 32t \\equiv 1 \\pmod{17} $, $ 32≡-2, so -2t≡1 ⇒ 2t≡-1≡16 ⇒ t≡8 \\pmod{17} $. So $ m = 15 + 17*8 = 15+136=151 $.\n\nLift $ m \\equiv 9 \\pmod{17} $ (i.e. -8): $ m = 9 + 17t $. $ m^4 \\equiv (-8)^4 + 4*(-8)^3*17t = 4096 + 4*(-512)*17t = 4096 - 34816t \\equiv 50 - 136t \\pmod{289} $. So $ m^4 + 1 \\equiv 51 - 136t \\equiv 0 \\pmod{289} $, so $ -136t \\equiv -51 \\), so $ 136t = 51 \\pmod{289} $, same as before, $ 8t \\equiv 3 \\pmod{17} $, $ t \\equiv 3*15 = 45 \\equiv 11 \\pmod{17} $. So $ m = 9 + 17*11 = 9+187=196 $.\n\nSo the solutions mod 289 are $ m \\equiv 110, 151, 155, 196 \\pmod{289} $. The smallest positive is $ \\boxed{110} $.\n\nCheck: $ 110^4 = (110^2)^2 = 12100^2 = 146410000 $. Then $ 110^4 + 1 = 146410001 $. Now divide by 289. Or check mod 289. But from construction, it should work. And 110 is less than 151, 155, 196. So least positive $ m $ is 110. But is there a smaller one? Negative values would give larger positives. So yes.\n\nBut is p=17 the smallest possible? Check smaller primes: p=2: n^4+1 is odd, not divisible by 4 unless n odd, n=1: 1+1=2, not div by 4. n=3: 81+1=82, 82/4=20.5, not integer. n=5: 625+1=626, 626/4=156.5, no. n^4+1 ≡ 2 mod 4 for odd n, so never div by 4, so p=2 no. p=5: n^4+1 ≡0 mod 25? n^4≡-1 mod 25. But φ(25)=20, so order divides 20. -1 is not a fourth power? Squares mod 25: 0,1,4,9,16,24,10, etc. fourth powers: squares of squares: 0,1,16,11,21,6, etc. Check if -1=24 is a fourth power. Try n=1:1, n=2:16, n=3:81≡6, n=4:256≡6, n=5:0, n=6:1296≡21, n=7:2401≡1, n=8:4096≡21? 4096 /25: 25*163=4075, 4096-4075=21. n=9:6561, 25*262=6550, 11. n=10:0. None is 24. So no solution mod 25. p=13: n^4≡-1 mod 169. First mod 13: n^4≡12 mod 13. Powers: 1^4=1, 2^4=16≡3, 3^4=81≡3, 4^4=256≡256-19*13=256-247=9, 5^4=625≡625-48*13=625-624=1, 6^4=1296≡1296-99*13=1296-1287=9, 7^4=2401≡2401-184*13=2401-2392=9, better: 12 is quartic residue? 2^4=16≡3, 3^4=81≡3 (13*6=78), 4^4=256≡256-19*13=256-247=9, 5^4=625÷13: 13*48=624, so 1. 6^4≡(-7)^4=7^4=(49)^2=10^2=100≡9, 7^4=(49)^2=10^2=100≡9, 8^2=64≡12, 8^4=144≡1. 9^2=81≡3, 9^4=9, 10^2=100≡9, 10^4=81. 11^2=121≡4, 11^4=16. 12^2=144≡1, 12^4=1. So fourth powers mod 13: 0,1,3,9. 12 not among them. So no solution for p=13. p=17 is indeed smallest.\n\nSo smallest p=17, smallest m=110.\n\nBut earlier I thought 110. But in the list: 110,151,155,196. So 110 is smallest.\n\nBut let me verify $ 110^4 + 1 $ divisible by $ 17^2 = 289 $.\n\nCompute $ 110^2 = 12100 $.\n\nThen $ 110^4 = (12100)^2 $.\n\n$ 12100^2 = (1.21 \\times 10^4)^2 = 1.4641 \\times 10^8 = 146410000 $.\n\nSo $ 110^4 + 1 = 146410001 $.\n\nNow divide by 289.\n\nFirst, 289 * 500,000 = 144,500,000\n\nSubtract: 146,410,001 - 144,500,000 = 1,910,001\n\nNow 289 * 6000 = 289*6*1000=1734*1000=1,734,000\n\nSubtract: 1,910,001 - 1,734,000 = 176,001\n\n289 * 600 = 173,400\n\nSubtract: 176,001 - 173,400 = 2,601\n\n289 * 9 = 2,601\n\nExactly: 289*9=2601.\n\nSo total: 500,000 + 6,000 + 600 + 9 = 506,609\n\nAnd 289 * 506,609 = 146,410,001.\n\nYes, exactly.\n\nSo $ 110^4 + 1 = 289 \\times 506609 $, so divisible by $ 17^2