      "generated_answer": "We are given that $\\omega \\neq 1$ is a 13th root of unity, so $\\omega^{13} = 1$ and $1 + \\omega + \\omega^2 + \\cdots + \\omega^{12} = 0$. Consider the product  \n$$\n\\prod_{k=0}^{12} (2 - 2\\omega^k + \\omega^{2k}).\n$$\nWe simplify the expression inside:  \n$$\n2 - 2\\omega^k + \\omega^{2k} = 2(1 - \\omega^k) + \\omega^{2k}.\n$$\nHowever, a better approach is to factor cleverly. Let us define $f(x) = \\prod_{k=0}^{12} (x - \\omega^k) = x^{13} - 1$, since the $\\omega^k$ are the 13th roots of unity.\n\nBut instead, consider the expression:\n$$\nP = \\prod_{k=0}^{12} (2 - 2\\omega^k + \\omega^{2k}) = \\prod_{k=0}^{12} \\left( (1 - \\omega^k)^2 + 1 \\right).\n$$\nWait — let's try a substitution. Let $z = \\omega^k$, so as $k$ runs from 0 to 12, $z$ runs over all 13th roots of unity. Then the product becomes:\n$$\nP = \\prod_{z^{13}=1} (2 - 2z + z^2) = \\prod_{z^{13}=1} (z^2 - 2z + 2).\n$$\nNow define the polynomial $Q(z) = z^2 - 2z + 2$. Then:\n$$\nP = \\prod_{z^{13} = 1} Q(z).\n$$\nThere is a known result: for a polynomial $f(z)$, the product $\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} f(\\omega^k)$, where $\\omega$ is a primitive $n$th root of unity, equals the resultant of $f(z)$ and $z^n - 1$, or equivalently, the product of $f(\\zeta)$ over all $n$th roots of unity $\\zeta$ is equal to the resultant $\\text{Res}(f(z), z^n - 1)$, but more usefully, it's equal to the product of $(\\alpha^n - 1)$ over roots $\\alpha$ of $f$, with multiplicity.\n\nAlternatively, if $f(z) = a\\prod (z - r_i)$, then $\\prod_{\\zeta^{13}=1} f(\\zeta) = \\prod_{\\zeta} a \\prod_i (\\zeta - r_i) = a^{13} \\prod_i \\left( \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - r_i) \\right) = a^{13} \\prod_i (r_i^{13} - 1)$, since $\\prod_{\\zeta^{13}=1} (z - \\zeta) = z^{13} - 1$, so $\\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - c) = (-1)^{13} (c^{13} - 1)$ when monic? Wait.\n\nActually:\n$$\n\\prod_{\\zeta^{13} = 1} (z - \\zeta) = z^{13} - 1,\n$$\nso\n$$\n\\prod_{\\zeta^{13} = 1} (c - \\zeta) = c^{13} - 1.\n$$\nBut here we have $\\prod_{\\zeta} f(\\zeta)$, and $f(z) = z^2 - 2z + 2$, which factors as $(z - \\alpha)(z - \\beta)$, where $\\alpha, \\beta = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 - 8}}{2} = 1 \\pm i$.\n\nSo,\n$$\n\\prod_{\\zeta^{13} = 1} f(\\zeta) = \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\alpha)(\\zeta - \\beta) = \\left( \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\alpha) \\right) \\left( \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\beta) \\right) = (\\alpha^{13} - 1)(\\beta^{13} - 1),\n$$\nsince $\\prod_{\\zeta^{13}=1} (\\zeta - c) = (-1)^{13} \\prod_{\\zeta} (c - \\zeta) = - (c^{13} - 1)$, but wait — actually:\n$$\n\\prod_{\\zeta^{13}=1} (c - \\zeta) = c^{13} - 1.\n$$\nBut our product is $\\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\alpha)$, which is $(-1)^{13} \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\alpha - \\zeta) = -(\\alpha^{13} - 1)$.\n\nSince there are 13 roots, and $\\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\alpha) = (-1)^{13} \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\alpha - \\zeta) = -(\\alpha^{13} - 1)$.\n\nSo\n$$\n\\prod_{\\zeta} f(\\zeta) = \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\alpha)(\\zeta - \\beta) = \\left( \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\alpha) \\right) \\left( \\prod_{\\zeta} (\\zeta - \\beta) \\right) = [- (\\alpha^{13} - 1)] \\cdot [- (\\beta^{13} - 1)] = (\\alpha^{13} - 1)(\\beta^{13} - 1).\n$$\nSo $P = (\\alpha^{13} - 1)(\\beta^{13} - 1)$, where $\\alpha = 1+i$, $\\beta = 1-i$.\n\nNote that $\\alpha = 1+i = \\sqrt{2} e^{i\\pi/4}$, so $|\\alpha| = \\sqrt{2}$, $\\arg(\\alpha) = \\pi/4$. Then:\n$$\n\\alpha^{13} = (\\sqrt{2})^{13} e^{i 13\\pi/4} = 2^{13/2} e^{i(13\\pi/4 - 4\\pi)} = 2^{13/2} e^{-i3\\pi/4} = 2^{6.5} (\\cos(-135^\\circ) + i\\sin(-135^\\circ)) = 2^{6.5} \\left( -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - i\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)\n$$\nCalculate:\n$$\n2^{6.5} = 2^6 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = 64\\sqrt{2}\n$$\nSo:\n$$\n\\alpha^{13} = 64\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - i\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right) = 64(-1 - i)\n$$\nSimilarly, $\\beta = 1-i$, so $\\beta^{13} = ( \\sqrt{2} e^{-i\\pi/4} )^{13} = 2^{6.5} e^{-i13\\pi/4} = 2^{6.5} e^{-i(13\\pi/4)} = 2^{6.5} e^{i(-13\\pi/4 + 4\\pi)} = 2^{6.5} e^{i3\\pi/4} $\nWait: $-13\\pi/4 + 4\\pi = -13\\pi/4 + 16\\pi/4 = 3\\pi/4$\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\beta^{13} = 64\\sqrt{2} \\left( \\cos(135^\\circ) + i\\sin(135^\\circ) \\right) = 64\\sqrt{2} \\left( -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + i\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right) = 64(-1 + i)\n$$\n\nThus:\n- $\\alpha^{13} = -64 - 64i$\n- $\\beta^{13} = -64 + 64i$\n\nThen:\n- $\\alpha^{13} - 1 = -65 - 64i$\n- $\\beta^{13} - 1 = -65 + 64i$\n\nNow multiply:\n$$\nP = (-65 - 64i)(-65 + 64i) = (-65)^2 - (64i)^2 = 4225 - (-4096) = 4225 + 4096 = 8321\n$$\n\nNow compute $8321 \\mod 1000$ — take the last three digits: $8321 \\mod 1000 = 321$\n\nSo the remainder when $P$ is divided by 1000 is $\\boxed{321}$.",
