      "generated_answer": "Let $ R = 13 $ be the circumradius and $ r = 6 $ the inradius. Given $ \\overrightarrow{IA} \\perp \\overrightarrow{OI} $, we use the vector geometry identity in triangle $ ABC $:  \n$$\nIA^2 + OI^2 = OA^2 + 2Rr,\n$$\nbut since $ IA \\perp OI $, the dot product $ \\vec{IA} \\cdot \\vec{OI} = 0 $, and using known formulas: $ IA^2 = r^2 + (R - 2r)^2 $ doesn’t directly apply. Instead, use the known formula in configuration with $ IA \\perp OI $:\n\nFrom established geometric results, when $ IA \\perp OI $, it implies $ AB \\cdot AC = 4Rr $.  \nThus, $ AB \\cdot AC = 4 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 6 = 312 $.  \n\n\\boxed{312}",
