    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ n + 2 $ divides $ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) $, and we want the sum of all positive integers $ n $ satisfying this condition.\n\nLet $ d = n + 2 $, so $ n = d - 2 $. Substituting, the expression becomes:\n$$\n3((d - 2) + 3)((d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13)\n$$\nSo $ d \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ d $ divides this, and $ d $ divides $ d \\times \\text{something} $, we only need $ d $ to divide the entire expression modulo $ d $. Note that $ d \\mid 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $, and since $ d \\equiv 0 \\pmod{d} $, we simplify modulo $ d $:\n- $ d + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{d} $\n- $ d^2 - 4d + 13 \\equiv 13 \\pmod{d} $\nSo modulo $ d $, the expression $ 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\equiv 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 13 = 39 \\pmod{d} $\n\nThus, $ d \\mid 39 $. So $ d $ is a positive divisor of 39 (since $ n > 0 \\Rightarrow d = n+2 \\geq 3 $), and the positive divisors of 39 are $ 1, 3, 13, 39 $. Since $ d \\geq 3 $, we take $ d = 3, 13, 39 $. Then $ n = d - 2 $, so $ n = 1, 11, 37 $. All are positive.\n\nCheck $ n = 1 $: $ n+2 = 3 $, product: $ 3(4)(1 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 10 = 120 $, $ 120 \\div 3 = 40 $: OK  \n$ n = 11 $: $ n+2 = 13 $, product: $ 3(14)(121 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 130 = 5460 $, $ 5460 \\div 13 = 420 $: OK  \n$ n = 37 $: $ n+2 = 39 $, product: $ 3(40)(1369 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 40 \\cdot 1378 = 165360 $, $ 165360 \\div 39 = 4240 $: OK  \n\nSo all valid. Sum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $\n\n\\boxed{49}",
