1=367.\n\nPreviously I had 368 including A0, so 367 is sum from k=1 to 7.\n\nNow for inclusion-exclusion, we need to subtract sum |A_k ∩ A_m| for k<m, k,m≥1.\n\nThis is still complicated, but perhaps we can use the fact that the group is cyclic and use Burnside's lemma or just accept that this is hard.\n\nI recall that in such problems, the only subsets that satisfy the condition for some k are those that are not their own periodic something.\n\nBut let’s think of the complementary probability: that for all k=1 to 7, S ∩ (S+k) ≠ ∅.\n\nFor example, if S is the entire set, then S ∩ (S+k) = S ≠ ∅.\n\nIf S has size 5, then by pigeonhole, for any k, |S| + |S+k| = 10 > 8, so S ∩ (S+k) ≠ ∅ by pigeonhole? No, S and S+k are both size 5, in set of size 8, so |S ∩ (S+k)| = |S| + |S+k| - |S ∪ (S+k)| ≥ 5+5-8 = 2 > 0, so indeed, for |S| > 4, |S| ≥ 5, then for every k, |S ∩ (S+k)| ≥ 2|S| - 8 > 0 if |S| > 4. 2*5-8=2>0, yes. So if |S| ≥ 5, then for all k, S ∩ (S+k) ≠ ∅, so S not in any A_k for k≠0, and not in A0 unless S= empty.\n\nAlso, S not in A0 if not empty.\n\nSo for |S| ≥ 5, the coloring is not in the union.\n\nSimilarly, for |S| = 4, |S| = 2*4 - 8 = 0, so possibly disjoint.\n\nFor |S| = 4, S ∩ (S+k) = ∅ iff S and S+k are disjoint. Since both size 4, this means S+k = complement of S.\n\nSo for a set S of size 4, it is in A_k iff S+k = \\bar{S}.\n\nSo for each S of size 4, it is in A_k for some k iff \\bar{S} is a rotation of S.\n\nSimilarly, for |S| = 3, it is in A_k if S+k ⊆ \\bar{S}, which has size 5, so possible.\n\nBut perhaps compute by size.\n\nLet’s compute the number of S such that there exists k with S ∩ (S+k) = ∅.\n\nSplit by |S|.\n\nIf |S| = 0: S = empty. Then for any k, S+k = empty, so S ∩ (S+k) = ∅, so in all A_k. So count 1.\n\nIf |S| = 1: say S = {i}. Then S+k = {i+k}. S ∩ (S+k) = ∅ iff i ≠ i+k, iff k ≠ 0. So for any k≠0, they are disjoint. So for each k≠0, in A_k. So such S are in the union. Number of such S: C(8,1) = 8.\n\nIf |S| = 2: S = {i,j}. S+k = {i+k,j+