ot a rotate for this S.\n\nSo for S to have \\bar{S} = S+k, we need sum \\bar{S} = sum S + 4k mod 8.\n\nSum of all elements 0 to 7 is 28 ≡ 4 mod 8.\n\nSo sum S + sum \\bar{S} = 4 mod 8.\n\nLet a = sum S, b = sum \\bar{S}, a+b=4 mod 8.\n\nBut b = a + 4k mod 8.\n\nSo a + (a + 4k) = 4 mod 8, so 2a + 4k ≡ 4 mod 8.\n\nDivide by 2: a + 2k ≡ 2 mod 4.\n\nSo a ≡ 2 - 2k mod 4, i.e., a ≡ 2 mod 4 if k even, a ≡ 0 mod 4 if k odd? 2k mod 4 is 0 if k even, 2 if k odd.\n\nSo a + 0 ≡ 2 mod 4 if k even, so a ≡ 2 mod 4.\n\nIf k odd, a + 2 ≡ 2 mod 4, so a ≡ 0 mod 4.\n\nIn this case, for the equation to have a solution, a must be even, which it is since a integer.\n\nBut specifically, if k even, a ≡ 2 mod 4; if k odd, a ≡ 0 mod 4.\n\nBut this is necessary but not sufficient.\n\nIn our case, for S={0,1,2,3}, sum=6≡6 mod 8? 0+1+2+3=6, 6 mod 8=6, not 7 as I said earlier. 0+1+2+3=6.\n\n\\bar{S} sum=4+5+6+7=22≡6 mod 8.\n\n22-6=16≡0 mod 8, but 4k for k=4 is 16≡0, and 6+0=6, yes.\n\na=6≡6 mod 8, 6 ≡ 2 mod 4? 6 div 4 is 1*4=4, remainder 2, yes.\n\nk=4 even, so a should be ≡2 mod 4, yes.\n\nNow, we need to count the number of 4-element subsets S such that \\bar{S} is a rotation of S.\n\nSince the rotation group acts, we can count the number of S such that \\bar{S} = S + k for some k.\n\nNote that if \\bar{S} = S + k, then the stabilizer, but perhaps list the possible k.\n\nFor each k, how many S such that \\bar{S} = S + k.\n\nBut \\bar{S} = S + k iff S + k = complement of S.\n\nThis is a system that can be solved.\n\nFor fixed k, the condition is that for each i, i in S iff i not in \\bar{S} = S + k, so i in S iff i-k not in S.\n\nSo the condition is that for all i, i in S iff i-k not in S.\n\nThis is a recurrence.\n\nFor example, fix k=4.\n\nThen i in S iff i-4 not in S.\n\nBut i-4+8=i-4, since mod 8.\n\nSo for each i, i in S iff i-4 not in S.\n\nLet a_i = 1 if i in S, else 0.\n\nThen a_i = 1 - a_{i-4}.\n\nThen a_{i-4} = 1 - a_{i-8} = 1 - a_i.\n\nFrom a_i = 1 - a_{i-4}, and a_{i-4} = 1 - a_{i-8} = 1 - a_i, since i-8≡i.\n\nSo a_{i-4} = 1 - a_i.\n\nThen a_i = 1 - a_{i-4} = 1 - (1 - a_i) = a_i.\n\nSo consistent.\n\nSo a_i = 1 - a_{i-4} for all i.\n\nSo pair (i,i+4): if i in S, then i+4 not in S, and if i not in S, then i+4 in S.\n\nSo for each pair {i,i+4}, exactly one is in S.\n\nThere are 4 such pairs: {0,4},{1,5},{2,6},{3,7}.\n\nFor each pair, choose exactly one element.\n\nSo number of such S: 2^4 = 16.\n\nBut this is for fixed k=4.\n\nAnd for each such S, \\bar{S} = S+4, as required.\n\nNow for other k.\n\nFor example, k=0: \\bar{S} = S +0 = S, so S = \\bar{S}, so |S|=4, and S= complement, so S = \\bar{S}, which implies that 2|S| = 8, so S is half, but S = \\bar{S} means that S is equal to its complement, which is impossible unless the set is equal, but in a group, might be possible, but here, if S = \\bar{S}, then for each i, i in S iff i not in S, contradiction. So no such S for k=0.\n\nFor k=2: \\bar{S} = S+2.\n\nThen condition: i in S iff i-2 not in S.\n\nSo a_i = 1 - a_{i-2}.\n\nThen a_{i-2} = 1 - a_{i-4}, a_{i-4} = 1 - a_{i-6}, a_{i-6} = 1 - a_{i-8} = 1 - a_i.\n\nSo a_{i-6} = 1 - a_i.\n\nBut a_{i-6} = 1 - a_{i-4} = 1 - (1 - a_{i-2}) = a_{i-2} = 1 - a_i.\n\nFrom a_i = 1 - a_{i-2}\n\na_{i-2} = 1 - a_{i-4}\n\na_{i-4} = 1 - a_{i-6}\n\na_{i-6} = 1 - a_{i-8} = 1 - a_i\n\nThen a_{i-4} = 1 - a_{i-6} = 1 - (1 - a_i) = a_i\n\nThen a_{i-2} = 1 - a_{i-4} = 1 - a_i\n\nThen a_i = 1 - a_{i-2} = 1 - (1 - a_i) = a_i, consistent.\n\nNow from a_{i-4} = a_i, so the sequence is periodic with period 4.\n\nAnd a_i = 1 - a_{i-2}\n\nFor i=0: a0, a2, a4, a6 are linked.\n\nFrom a0, a2 = 1 - a0, a4 = a0, a6 = 1 - a4 = 1 - a0, and a0 = 1 - a_{0-2} = 1 - a_6? i-2 for i=0 is -2≡6, so a0 = 1 - a6.\n\nBut a6 = 1 - a0, so a0 = 1 - (1 - a0) = a0, ok.\n\nSimilarly for i=1: a1, a3, a5, a7.\n\na1 = 1 - a_{1-2} = 1 - a_{-1} = 1 - a7\n\na3 = 1 - a1\n\na5 = 1 - a3\n\na7 = 1 - a5\n\nAnd a_{i-4} = a_i, so a3 = a_{3-4} = a_{-1} = a7, a7 = a3, etc.\n\na3 = a7, a5 = a1? a5 = a_{5-4} = a1.\n\nFrom a3 = 1 - a1, and a3 = a7, a7 = 1 - a5 = 1 - a1.\n\nSo a3 = 1 - a1, and a7 = 1 - a1, and a3 = a7, so consistent.\n\nAlso a1 = 1 - a7 = 1 - (1 - a1) = a1, ok.\n\nSo for the even indices: a0, a2, a4=a0, a6=1-a0\n\nAnd a0 = 1 - a6 = 1 - (1 - a0) = a0, ok.\n\nSo a0 can be 0 or 1.\n\nIf a0=0, then a2=1-0=1, a4=0, a6=1-0=1\n\nIf a0=1, a2=0, a4=1, a6=0\n\nSimilarly for odds: a1, a3=1-a1, a5=a1, a7=1-a1, and a1 = 1 - a7 = 1 - (1 - a1) = a1.\n\nSo a1 free.\n\nBut now, |S| = sum of a_i = (a0+a2+a4+a6) + (a1+a3+a5+a7)\n\nFor evens: if a0=0: 0+1+0+1=2\n\nIf a0=1: 1+0+1+0=2\n\nFor odds: if a1=0: a1=0,a3=1,a5=0,a7=1, sum=2\n\nIf a1=1:1,0,1,0, sum=2\n\nSo total |S|=4 always.\n\nNumber of such S: a0 has 2 choices, a1 has 2 choices, so 4 such S.\n\nList them:\n\n1. a0=0: evens: 0:0,2:1,4:0,6:1 so {2,6}\n\na1=0: odds:1:0,3:1,5:0,7:1 so {3,7}\n\nS={2,6,3,7}\n\n2. a0=0, a1=1: evens: {2,6}, odds:1:1,3:0,5:1,7:0 so {1,5}, S={1,2,5,6}\n\n3. a0=1, a1=0: evens:0:1,2:0,4:1,6:0 so {0,4}, odds:1:0,3:1,5:0,7:1 so {3,7}, S={0,3,4,7}\n\n4. a0=1, a1=1: evens: {0,4}, odds: {1,5}, S={0,1,4,5}\n\nSo only 4 such S for k=2.\n\nBut is \\bar{S} = S+2?\n\nTake S={2,6,3,7}. S+2={4,0,5,1}\n\n\\bar{S} = not in {2,3,6,7} so {0,1,4,5}, yes same as {0,1,4,5}.\n\nS+2={2+2,6+2,3+2,7+2}={4,8≡0,5,9≡1} = {0,1,4,5}, and \\bar{S} = total minus S = 8 elements minus 4, so {0,1,4,5}, yes.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nSo for k=2, there are 4 such S.\n\nSimilarly for k=6: since -2=6 mod 8, and the condition for k=6: \\bar{S} = S+6.\n\nThen similarly, a_i = 1 - a_{i-6}\n\nBut i-6 ≡ i+2 mod 8, so a_i = 1 - a_{i+2}, which is different.\n\nBut since the group is symmetric, likely the number is the same.\n\nNote that k=6 is inverse of k=2 in some sense.\n\nFor each such S that works for k=2, does it work for k=6?\n\nThe condition is different.\n\nFor example, take S={2,3,6,7} above, \\bar{S}={0,1,4,5}\n\nS+6={2+6=8≡0,3+6=9≡1,6+6=12≡4,7+6=13≡5} = {0,1,4,5} = \\bar{S}, yes!\n\nS+6 = (S+2)+4, but in this case, for this S, it is also true for k=6.\n\nBut is it the same S?\n\nFor this S={2,3,6,7}, S+2={4,5,0,1}, S+6={0,1,4,5}, same set.\n\nSo \\bar{S} = {0,1,4,5} = S+2 = S+6.\n\nSo for this S, it works for both k=2 and k=6.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nBut in general, for k=6, the number might be the same.\n\nLet's compute for k=6.\n\nCondition: \\bar{S} = S+6, so a_i = 1 - a_{i-6} = 1 - a_{i+2} since i-6≡i+2 mod 8.\n\nSo a_i = 1 - a_{i+2}\n\nThen a_{i+2} = 1 - a_{i+4}, etc.\n\nSimilar to before, it will give relations.\n\nBut likely there are 4 such S for k=6 as well.\n\nBut the same S might be counted.\n\nIn fact, from above, the S that work for k=2 also work for k=6 in the examples.\n\nFor S={1,2,5,6}, \\bar{S} = {0,3,4,7}\n\nS+6={1+6=7,2+6=8≡0,5+6=11≡3,6+6=12≡4} = {0,3,4,7} = \\bar{S}, yes.\n\nSimilarly for all.\n\nSo for each of the 4 S, \\bar{S} = S+2 = S+6, since 2 and 6 are both generators, but 2+6=8≡0, and S+2 and S+6 are both equal to \\bar{S}, so S+2 = S+6, so 2≡6 mod period, but in general.\n\n S+2 = S+6 implies 2≡6 mod stabilizer, but here it means the set is periodic with period 4, since (S+6) - (S+2) = S+4, and S+4 should equal S if S+2=S+6, but not necessarily.\n\nIf S+2 = T and S+6 = T, then (S+6) - (S+2) = S+4, and T - T = set, but not helpful.\n\nFrom S+2 = S+6, then adding, but in sets, it means that for the set to be invariant under +4, because (S+6) - (S+2) = +4, so S+4 = S.\n\nFor the S we have: take S={1,2,5,6}, then S+4={5,6,9≡1,10≡2} = {1,2,5,6} = S, so yes, periodic with period 4.\n\nSimilarly, S={0,1,4,5}, S+4={4,5,8≡0,9≡1} = same.\n\nS={0,3,4,7}, S+4={4,7,8≡0,11≡3} = {0,3,4,7}=S.\n\nS={2,3,6,7}, S+4={6,7,10≡2,11≡3}={2,3,6,7}=S.\n\nSo all have period 4.\n\nAnd \\bar{S} = S+2 = S+6.\n\nNow for other k.\n\nFor k=1: \\bar{S} = S+1.\n\nThen a_i = 1 - a_{i-1}\n\nThen a_{i-1} = 1 - a_{i-2}, etc.\n\nThen a_i = 1 - a_{i-1} = 1 - (1 - a_{i-2}) = a_{i-2}\n\nSimilarly, a_{i-2} = a_{i-4}, etc.\n\nSo a_i = a_{i-2} = a_{i-4} = a_{i-6} = a_{i-8} = a_i, so period 2.\n\nAlso a_i =