for r_i=1: 4 positions × 4 colorings = 16 colorings.\n\nBut wait: is there overlap? No, because only one internal edge red.\n\nNow, next case: r_i=3 → r_b=2.\n\nThis is symmetric to r_i=1, because swapping red and blue would transform a valid coloring to another (since each square has 2 red and 2 blue, so swapping gives 2 blue and 2 red → still valid).\n\nSimilarly, the condition r_b + 2 r_i = 8 becomes, after swap: number of blue boundary + 2 × blue internal = 8.\n\nBut total boundary edges: 8, so blue boundary = 8 - r_b; blue internal = 4 - r_i.\n\nThen: (8 - r_b) + 2(4 - r_i) = 8 - r_b + 8 - 2 r_i = 16 - (r_b + 2 r_i) = 16 - 8 = 8.\n\nSo yes, the equation is preserved. And the condition per square (2 red, 2 blue) is unchanged under swap.\n\nThus the set of colorings is closed under red-blue swap.\n\nTherefore, the number of colorings with (r_i, r_b) = (3,2) is equal to that with (r_i, r_b) = (1,6), which is 16.\n\nSimilarly, for r_i=0, r_b=8: all boundary red, internal blue: only one such coloring.\n\nAfter swap: all boundary blue, internal red → which is r_i=4, r_b=0 → also one coloring.\n\nNow the middle case: r_i=2, r_b=4.\n\nThen r_b + 2 r_i = 4 + 4 = 8.\n\nWe need to count the number of colorings with exactly two internal edges red, and four boundary edges red, satisfying the per-square condition.\n\nNow, the internal edges: there are four: H_left, H_right, VM_top, VM_bottom.\n\nWe choose 2 of them to be red.\n\nBut they may be adjacent or opposite.\n\nTypes of pairs:\n\n1. Two horizontal: H_left and H_right both red\n2. Two vertical: VM_top and VM_bottom both red\n3. Two in the same row: e.g., H_left and VM_top (both adjacent to TL)\n4. Two in opposite corners: H_left and VM_bottom\n5. H_right and VM_top\n6. H_left and H_right — already in 1\n7. VM_top and VM_bottom — in 2\n\nBetter: possible pairs:\n\n- Both horizontal: {H_left, H_right}\n- Both vertical: {VM_top, VM_bottom}\n- Two adjacent (sharing a corner): {H_left, VM_top}, {H_left, VM_bottom}, {H_right, VM_top}, {H_right, VM_bottom}\n- Diagonally opposite: but H_left and H_right are horizontal; VM_top and VM_bottom vertical.\n\nBut internal edges: H_left (horizontal, left), H_right (horizontal, right), VM_top (vertical, top), VM_bottom (vertical, bottom).\n\nPairs of them:\n\n- Same direction: \n  - horizontal pair: H_left and H_right\n  - vertical pair: VM_top and VM_bottom\n- Cross (perpendicular):\n  - sharing a square: e.g., H_left and VM_top (both on TL)\n  - H_left and VM_bottom (H_left is on BL and TL? H_left is top of BL, bottom of TL; VM_bottom is right of BL. So both on BL? BL has left: BL_left, top: H_left, right: VM_bottom, bottom: BL_bottom. So yes, H_left and VM_bottom are on BL.\n  - similarly, H_left and VM_top: on TL\n  - H_right and VM_top: on TR\n  - H_right and VM_bottom: on BR\n- And the other diagonals: H_left and VM_bottom, but also H_right and VM_top — wait, these are opposite.\n\nBut H_left and VM_bottom: do they share a square? BL has both: yes, top and right edges.\n\nSimilarly, H_left and VM_top: TL has both.\n\nBut H_left and VM_bottom — both incident to BL? H_left is top of BL, VM_bottom is right of BL — so yes, they meet at the top-right corner of BL.\n\nSimilarly, every pair of one horizontal and one vertical internal edge share a common square if they are in the same \"cell\".\n\nSpecifically:\n- H_left and VM_top → both in TL\n- H_left and VM_bottom → both in BL\n- H_right and VM_top → both in TR\n- H_right and VM_bottom → both in BR\n\nAnd the pairs like H_left and VM_bottom are not \"opposite\" — they are adjacent via BL.\n\nIn fact, any horizontal and vertical internal edge intersect at a corner.\n\nThe four internal edges form a \"plus\" sign in the center, but actually they are not connected at center? Wait: H_left, H_right are in the middle horizontal line, left and right segment; VM_top, VM_bottom are in the middle vertical line, top and bottom segment. They all meet at the center point.\n\nSo they form a cross.\n\nNow, choosing two to be red.\n\nCases:\n\nCase A: both red edges are parallel: either both horizontal or both vertical.\n\nCase B: both red edges are perpendicular and adjacent (i.e., share a common square).\n\nBut all perpendicular pairs share a square.\n\nNumber of ways to choose two internal edges: \\binom{4}{2} = 6.\n\n- Same direction: \n  - horizontal pair: {H_left, H_right} — one such\n  - vertical pair: {VM_top, VM_bottom} — one such\n- Perpendicular: there are 4 such pairs: {H_left, VM_top}, {H_left, VM_bottom}, {H_right, VM_top}, {H_right, VM_bottom}\n\nNote: {H_left, VM_top} corresponds to TL corner, etc.\n\nNow, subcase A1: both horizontal internal edges red: H_left and H_right red; VM_top and VM_bottom blue.\n\nNow, r_i = 2, so need r_b = 4.\n\nNow, look at each square.\n\nTL: bottom: H_left (red), right: VM_top (blue) → so bottom red, right blue.\n\nNeeds total 2 red, so exactly one of top or left to be red: i.e., exactly one of TL_top and TL_left.\n\nSimilarly, TR: bottom: H_right (red), left: VM_top (blue) → bottom red, left blue → so exactly one of top or right: i.e., TR_top or TR_right.\n\nBL: top: H_left (red), right: VM_bottom (blue) → top red, right blue → so exactly one of left or bottom: BL_left or BL_bottom.\n\nBR: top: H_right (red), left: VM_bottom (blue) → top red, left blue → so exactly one of bottom or right: BR_bottom or BR_right.\n\nNow, no square forces two reds.\n\nSo we have independent choices: for each of the four corners, we choose which of its two boundary edges is red (the other blue).\n\nFor TL: choose which of {TL_top, TL_left} is red.\n\nSimilarly for TR, BL, BR.\n\nEach has 2 choices.\n\nSo total: 2^4 = 16 colorings in this subcase.\n\nBut we need to check if this satisfies r_b = 4.\n\nEach choice sets exactly one boundary edge red per square? No.\n\nPer square, we are setting one additional boundary edge red.\n\nFor TL: we set exactly one of its two boundaries red; similarly for each.\n\nSince each boundary edge belongs to only one square, total red boundary edges: 4 (one per square) → r_b=4. Perfect.\n\nSo 16 colorings for this configuration (H_left, H_right red).\n\nBy symmetry, if both vertical internals red: VM_top and VM_bottom red; H_left and H_right blue.\n\nThen similar analysis:\n\nTL: right: VM_top (red), bottom: H_left (blue) → right red, bottom blue → need exactly one of top or left.\n\nTR: left: VM_top (red), bottom: H_right (blue) → left red, bottom blue → need exactly one of top or right.\n\nBL: right: VM_bottom (red), top: H_left (blue) → right red, top blue → need exactly one of left or bottom.\n\nBR: left: VM_bottom (red), top: H_right (blue) → left red, top blue → need exactly one of bottom or right.\n\nSame as above: each square has exactly one of its boundary edges to be chosen red, others blue — 2 choices per square → 16 colorings.\n\nNow, case B: perpendicular pair red, say H_left and VM_top red; others blue.\n\nThis pair is at TL.\n\nSo H_left red, VM_top red; H_right blue, VM_bottom blue.\n\nNow, r_i = 2, need r_b = 4.\n\nNow, look at TL: bottom: H_left (red), right: VM_top (red) → both internal edges red.\n\nSo already two red edges. Then the two boundary edges: TL_top and TL_left must both be blue.\n\nBecause if any were red, TL would have more than 2 red.\n\nSo TL_top blue, TL_left blue.\n\nNow, TR: left: VM_top (red), bottom: H_right (blue) → left red, bottom blue.\n\nSo currently one red. To have exactly two, need exactly one of top or right to be red: i.e., TR_top or TR_right.\n\nSimilarly, BL: top: H_left (red), right: VM_bottom (blue) → top red, right blue → one red so far, so need exactly one of left or bottom: BL_left or BL_bottom.\n\nBR: top: H_right (blue), left: VM_bottom (blue) → top blue, left blue → both blue. So need both bottom and right to be red: BR_bottom red, BR_right red.\n\nNow, so far: BR forces BR_bottom and BR_right red.\n\nTR: needs exactly one of TR_top, TR_right red — but TR_right is boundary.\n\nSimilarly, BL: needs exactly one of BL_left, BL_bottom.\n\nNow, red boundaries so far: BR_bottom red, BR_right red → 2 reds.\n\nWe need total r_b=4, so two more reds.\n\nTR needs one red from {TR_top, TR_right}; BL needs one red from {BL_left, BL_bottom}.\n\nSo if we choose one for TR and one for BL, that’s two more reds → total r_b=4.\n\nAnd TL has no red boundary (both blue), so not contributing.\n\nSo possible.\n\nNow, choices: TR: 2 options (which of top or right red), BL: 2 options → 4 colorings.\n\nNow, check: TR: if TR_top red, TR_right blue, then edges: top red, left red, bottom blue, right blue → two red (top, left). Good.\n\nBR: top blue, left blue, bottom red, right red → two red.\n\nBL: say BL_left red, BL_bottom blue → left red, top red, bottom blue, right blue → two red.\n\nTL: top blue, left blue, bottom red, right red → two red. Good.\n\nYes.\n\nNow, similarly, if we choose another perpendicular pair, say H_left and VM_bottom red (so at BL corner).\n\nThen H_left red, VM_bottom red; H_right blue, VM_top blue.\n\nThen BL: top: H_left (red), right: VM_bottom (red) → both internal red → so boundary edges BL_left and BL_bottom must be blue.\n\nTL: bottom: H_left (red), right: VM_top (blue) → bottom red, right blue → so need exactly one of TL_top or TL_left.\n\nTR: bottom: H_right (blue), left: VM_top (blue) → both blue → so need TR_top and TR_right both red.\n\nBR: top: H_right (blue), left: VM_bottom (red) → left red, top blue → so need exactly one of bottom or right.\n\nNow, TR forces TR_top red, TR_right red → two red boundaries.\n\nThen need two more red boundaries (since r_b=4).\n\nTL: needs one from {TL_top, TL_left}\n\nBR: needs one from {BR_bottom, BR_right}\n\nSo choose one for TL, one for BR → 2×2=4 colorings.\n\nSimilarly for other two: {H_right, VM_top} (TR corner) and {H_right, VM_bottom} (BR corner).\n\nEach will give 4 colorings.\n\nNow, how many such pairs? There are 4 perpendicular pairs (one for each corner).\n\nEach gives 4 colorings → 4 × 4 = 16 colorings.\n\nNow, earlier,