nternal edges red, so its boundaries must be blue.\n\nThe opposite corner (BR) has both internal blue, so must have both boundaries red.\n\nAnd the adjacent corners (TR and BL) each have one internal red, one internal blue, so need one boundary red.\n\nSo the number of free choices: TR has 2 choices, BL has 2, others fixed: so 4.\n\nYes.\n\nNow, total for r_i=2: two same-direction pairs: each 16 → 32\n\nFour perpendicular pairs: each 4 → 16\n\nTotal 48.\n\nNow, r_i=1: 4 choices for which internal is red, each gives 4 colorings → 16\n\nr_i=3: by symmetry, 16\n\nr_i=0:1, r_i=4:1\n\nSum: 1+16+48+16+1 = 82\n\nBut is the symmetry valid? When we swap colors, a coloring with r_i=1 becomes one with r_i=3, and we assume bijection.\n\nBut is the swap always valid? Yes, because the condition is symmetric in red and blue.\n\nMoreover, no coloring is fixed under swap unless r_i=2 and r_b=4, but even then, some might be fixed, but we don't care since we are counting distinct colorings.\n\nSo total should be 82.\n\nBut let me verify with a different approach.\n\nAnother way: consider that for each square, the number of ways to color its edges with 2 red, 2 blue, but respecting shared edges.\n\nWe can model it as assigning colors to the 12 edges with constraints.\n\nNote that the four corner squares each share two internal edges? No.\n\nWe can use the fact that the coloring is determined by choices.\n\nI recall that in such grid problems, sometimes the answer is 70 or 90, but 82?\n\nWait, let me compute the total.\n\nI think I made a mistake in the both horizontal case.\n\nIn the both horizontal internal edges red: H_left and H_right red.\n\nThen for TL: bottom red, right: VM_top blue → so need exactly one of top or left red.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nBut when I say \"choose for each square independently\", but are the choices independent?\n\nFor example, I choose for TL to set TL_top red, TL_left blue.\n\nBut TL_top is only used in TL, so no conflict.\n\nSimilarly, TL_left only 