| > p^3 - p^2 $.\n\nBut also by AM-GM, $ 2|x||y| \\leq x^2 + y^2 = p^3 $, so this is possible.\n\nBut we can use that $ (|x| - |y|)^2 \\geq 0 $, so $ |x| - |y| \\geq 0 $, and we need it less than p.\n\nBut rather than inequalities, try to maximize p such that for some $ a,b $ with $ a^2 + b^2 = p $, the values $ x = a(a^2 - 3b^2) $, $ y = b(3a^2 - b^2) $, satisfy the triangle conditions with p.\n\nLet’s try the case where $ a = b $. Then $ p = 2a^2 $, so only if a=1, p=2. Try: z=1+i, |z| = sqrt(2), p=2. Then z^3 = (1+i)^3 = 1 + 3i + 3i^2 + i^3 = 1+3i-3-i = -2+2i. So real part -2, imag 2, absolute values 2,2. Triangle sides 2,2,2 — equilateral, works. So p=2 works, but small.\n\nBack to search for large p.\n\nTry p=985? 985 not prime. 997, as before.\n\nIs there a representation of 997 as sum of two squares? 997 mod 4 = 1, so yes.\n\nFind a,b: solve a^2 + b^2 = 997.\n\nTry a=31: 31^2=961, 997-961=36=6^2. Yes! a=31, b=6.\n\nThen compute x = Re(z^3) = a(a^2 - 3b^2) = 31*(961 - 3*36) = 31*(961 - 108) = 31*853\n\n31*853: 30*853=25590, +853=26443\n\ny = b(3a^2 - b^2) = 6*(3*961 - 36) = 6*(2883 - 36) = 6*2847=17082\n\nSo sides are p=997, |x|=26443, |y|=17082\n\nCheck triangle inequalities:\n\n1. 997 + 17082 = 18079 < 26443? Yes, 18079 < 26443 → fails.\n\nSo not valid.\n\nWe need |x| and |y| to be close to each other or both small.\n\nBut |x|^2 + |y|^2 = 997^3 ≈ 991 million, so both are large unless one is small.\n\nSo perhaps when a^2 - 3b^2 is small, so x is small.\n\nSet a^2 - 3b^2 ≈ 0, so a ≈ sqrt(3) b.\n\nSimilarly, or 3a^2 - b^2 ≈ 0, but that would make y small.\n\nTry to have x or y small.\n\nSuppose |x| < p and |y| < p, then since x^2 + y^2 = p^3 > p^2 for p>1, impossible. So at least one of |x| or |y| is greater than p.\n\nIn fact, since x^2 + y^2 = p^3, by pigeonhole, max(|x|,|y|) >= p^{3/2}/sqrt(2) > p for p>2.\n\nSo one side is >p.\n\nLet M = max(|x|,|y|), m = min.\n\nThen we need p + m > M.\n\nSo important condition: min(|x|,|y|) > M - p.\n\nBut M = max(|x|,|y|