nSo for a=15, b=26: p=15^2 + 26^2=225+676=901, again 901=17*53, not prime.\n\na=56, b=97: p=56^2+97^2=3136+9409=12545>1000.\n\nNow try with difference ±1, but p not prime.\n\nTry other representations.\n\nPerhaps try p= 677: is 677 prime? Yes. Can it be sum of two squares? 677 mod 4=1, yes.\n\nFind a,b: sqrt(677)≈26.02, try a=26, a^2=676, 677-676=1, so b=1. So z=26+i.\n\nThen x = a(a^2 - 3b^2)=26(676 - 3)=26*673=17498\n\ny = b(3a^2 - b^2)=1(3*676 -1)=2028-1=2027\n\nSo sides: 677, 17498, 2027\n\nCheck: 677+2027=2704 < 17498? yes, 2704 < 17498 → fails.\n\nSame issue.\n\nWe need a case where one of x or y is small.\n\nEarlier with a^2 - 3b^2 =1, x=a*1=a, small if a small, but we want large p.\n\nFor example, a=26, b=15 gives x=26, but p=901 not prime.\n\nAre there other p = a^2 + b^2 with a^2 - 3b^2 = ±1, ±2, etc., and p prime.\n\nTry a^2 - 3b^2 =0: not integer.\n\n=1: p=a^2 + b^2, with a^2=3b^2+1.\n\nSo p=3b^2+1 + b^2=4b^2+1.\n\nSo p=4b^2+1, prime.\n\nAnd a=sqrt(3b^2+1) ≈ b√3, so a integer when 3b^2+1 is square.\n\nThis is the Pell equation: a^2 - 3b^2 =1, which has solutions: (a,b) = (1,0), (2,1), (7,4), (26,15), (97,56), ...\n\nThen p=4b^2+1.\n\nFor b=0: p=1, not prime.\n\nb=1: p=4+1=5, prime.\n\na=2, b=1: p=4+1=5, as before.\n\nCheck: z=2+i, z^3= (2+i)^3=8+12i+6i^2+i^3=8+12i-6-i=2+11i. So real=2, imag=11. Sides 5,2,11. 5+2=7<11, fails! Earlier we had a case that worked, but this one fails.\n\nBut earlier we had p=17 that worked.\n\nFor p=5, it fails.\n\nNext: b=4, a=7, p=4*16+1=64+1=65, not prime (5*13).\n\nb=15, a=26, p=4*225+1=900+1=901, not prime.\n\nb=56, p=4*3136+1=12544+1=12545>1000.\n\nNow for y small: 3a^2 - b^2 =1, so b^2 =3a^2 -1.\n\nThen p=a^2 + b^2 = a^2 +3a^2 -1=4a^2 -1.\n\nSolutions (a,b): from earlier, a=1,b=2: b^2=4, 3*1-1=2, no; wait.\n\nFrom b^2 - 3a^2 = -1 or 1.\n\nFor b^2 - 3a^2 =1: b=2,a=1:4-3=1.\n\nb=7,a=4:49-48=1.\n\netc.\n\nSo for a=1,b=2: p=1+4=5, same as before.\n\na=4,b=7: p=16+49=65, not prime.\n\na=15,b=26:225+676=901, not prime.\n\nSam